hadiths[1605]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1595","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever knows that prayer is a binding truth and obligation shall enter Paradise.""**"
"1596","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, Allah has obligated upon His servants five prayers in every day and night.""**"
"1597","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the first thing that Allah the Exalted obligated upon people from their religion is the prayer, the last thing that remains is the prayer, and the first thing for which the servant will be brought to account is the prayer. Allah will say: 'Look into the prayer of My servant: if it is complete, let it be recorded as complete; and if it is deficient, He will say: Look, does he have any voluntary prayers?' If voluntary prayers are found for him, the obligatory prayer will be completed from the voluntary prayers. Then He will say: 'Look, is his zakah complete?' If his zakah is found to be complete, it will be recorded as complete; and if it is deficient, He will say: 'Look, does he have any voluntary charity?' If he has voluntary charity, his zakah will be completed from the voluntary charity.""**"
"1598","Ḥanẓalah al-Kātib رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever safeguards the five prayers—their bowings, their prostrations, and their appointed times—and knows that they are a truth from Allah, shall enter Paradise""**—or he said: **""Paradise is guaranteed for him,""** or he said: **""he is forbidden to the Fire.""**"
"1599","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The tribe of Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr dispatched Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah as an emissary to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He arrived, made his camel kneel at the door of the mosque, hobbled it, and entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated among his Companions. Ḍimām was a hairy man with two braided locks. He approached until he stood before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among his Companions and asked, ""Which of you is the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied, ""I am the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib."" He asked, ""Muḥammad?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" He said, ""O son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, I shall question you and be stern in questioning, so harbor no resentment in yourself."" He ﷺ said, ""I harbor no resentment; ask whatever seems fit to you."" He said, ""I adjure you by Allah—your God, the God of those before you, and the God of whoever comes after you—did Allah send you as a Messenger to us?"" He ﷺ replied, ""O Allah, yes."" He said, ""I adjure you by Allah—your God and the God of whoever comes after you—did Allah command you to instruct us to worship Him alone, associating nothing with Him, and to cast off these rivals that our forefathers used to worship alongside Him?"" He ﷺ replied, ""O Allah, yes."" He said, ""I adjure you by Allah—your God, the God of those before you, and the God of whoever comes after you—did Allah command you to perform these five prayers?"" He ﷺ replied, ""O Allah, yes."" He then continued mentioning the obligatory duties of Islam one by one: zakah, fasting, pilgrimage, and all the laws of Islam, adjuring him at each obligation as he had adjured him concerning the one before it. When he finished, he said, ""I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. I shall fulfill these obligations and avoid what you have forbidden me, neither adding nor diminishing anything."" He then turned back toward his camel. As he turned away, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If the man with the two braided locks has spoken the truth, he will enter Paradise.""** Ḍimām reached his camel, untied its hobble, and rode until he reached his people. When they gathered around him, the first words he spoke were: ""Wretched are al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā!"" They cried: ""Hold, O Ḍimām! Beware of leprosy, beware of vitiligo, beware of madness!"" He said: ""Woe to you! By Allah, they can neither harm nor benefit. Allah has sent a Messenger and revealed to him a Book by which He has rescued you from that which you were upon. I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone, without partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger. I have brought to you from him that which he has commanded you with and that which he has forbidden you from."" By Allah, by that evening there was not a single man or woman in his settlement who had not embraced Islam. Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: ""We never heard of an emissary of any people more virtuous than Ḍimām."""
"1600","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: A Bedouin from Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""Peace be upon you, O son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib."" The Prophet ﷺ said to him, ""And upon you be peace."" The man said, ""I am a man from your maternal kin of Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr, and I am the emissary and representative of my people to you. I will ask you, and my inquiry will be stern; I will adjure you, and my adjuration will be forceful."" The Prophet ﷺ said to him, ""Proceed as you wish, O brother of Banū Saʿd."" He asked, ""Who created you, who created those before you, and who is the Creator of those after you?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Allah."" The man said, ""Then I adjure you by Him: did He send you?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" He asked, ""Who created the seven heavens and the seven earths, and caused sustenance to flow between them?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Allah."" The man said, ""Then I adjure you by Him: did He send you?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" The man said, ""We have found in your scripture, and your emissaries instructed us, to pray five prayers during the night and the day at their appointed times. I adjure you by Him: did He command you with that?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" The man said, ""We have found in your scripture, and your emissaries instructed us, to fast the month of Ramadan. I adjure you by Him: did He command you with that?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" The man said, ""We have found in your scripture, and your emissaries instructed us, that you take from the surplus of our wealth and distribute it among our poor. I adjure you by Him: did He command you with that?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" The man said, ""As for the fifth—meaning immoralities—I shall not ask you concerning it, for I have no desire for it."" He then said, ""By the One Who sent you with the truth, I will certainly act upon them, as will whoever obeys me among my people!"" The man then departed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ smiled until his molars showed, and then said: > **""If he has spoken the truth, he shall certainly enter Paradise.""**"
"1601","Abū al-Ṭufayl ʿĀmir ibn Wāthilah رضي الله عنه reported: A man passed by a gathering of people and greeted them with peace, and they returned his greeting. When he passed beyond them, one of them said, ""By Allah, I hate this man for the sake of Allah."" The people of the gathering said, ""By Allah, foul is what you have said! By Allah, we will surely inform him. Rise, O so-and-so, and inform him."" Their messenger caught up to the man and told him what had been said. The man turned back and went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saying, ""O Messenger of Allah, I passed by a gathering of Muslims among whom was so-and-so; I greeted them and they returned my greeting, but when I moved past, a man caught up to me and told me that so-and-so said: 'By Allah, I hate this man for the sake of Allah.' Call him, O Messenger of Allah, and ask him why he hates me."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned him and asked him about what the man had reported. The man admitted it, saying, ""I indeed said that to him, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, ""Why do you hate him?"" He replied, ""I am his neighbor and well-acquainted with him. By Allah, I have never seen him pray any prayer at all except these prescribed obligatory prayers which both the righteous and the wicked perform."" The first man said, ""Ask him, O Messenger of Allah: has he ever seen me delay it past its appointed time, or perform poor ablution for it, or perform bowing and prostration poorly in it?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him, and he replied, ""No, by Allah."" The second man then said, ""By Allah, I have never seen him fast at all except this month which both the righteous and the wicked fast."" The first man said, ""Ask him, O Messenger of Allah: has he ever seen me neglect anything in it or diminish any of its rights?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him, and he replied, ""No."" The second man then said, ""By Allah, I have never seen him give anything to a petitioner, nor have I seen him spend anything of his wealth in the cause of Allah except this zakah which both the righteous and the wicked discharge."" The first man said, ""Ask him, O Messenger of Allah: have I ever concealed anything of zakah or haggled with the one collecting it?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him, and he replied, ""No."" Thereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the accusing neighbor: > **""Depart, for I do not know, perhaps he is better than you.""**"
"1602","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Qawqal رضي الله عنه reported: He came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, tell me: if I perform the prescribed obligatory prayers, fast Ramadan, treat as forbidden what is unlawful, and treat as permissible what is lawful, without adding anything to that—shall I enter Paradise?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Yes.""** Al-Nuʿmān said, ""By Allah, I shall never add anything to that!"""
"1603","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: A man from the Anṣār took an oath that he would never perform any voluntary deed whatsoever, nor would he ever leave anything of what Allah had prescribed upon him. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Why do you criticize a man who, if he were to swear an oath by Allah, Allah would surely fulfill it?""**"
"1604","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥudayj said: I heard a man from Kindah say: A man from the Anṣār among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""None of you leaves any deficiency in his prayer except that Allah completes it through his voluntary prayers.""**"
"1605","Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmur reported from a man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The first deed for which the servant will be held to account is his prayer. If he completed it, it will be recorded for him as complete; and if he did not complete it, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will say: 'Look, do you find any voluntary prayers for My servant with which to complete his obligatory prayer?' Then zakah will be treated likewise, and then all deeds will be accounted for in that manner.""**"
"1606","ʿĀʾidh ibn Qurt رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs a prayer and does not complete it, additions will be made to it from his voluntary prayers until it is perfected.""**"
"1607","ʿAbdullāh ibn Qurt رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs a prayer and does not complete it, it will be supplemented from his voluntary prayers.""**"
"1608","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The first deed for which the servant will be brought to account on the Day of Resurrection is the prayer. If it is sound, the rest of his deeds will be sound; and if it is corrupt, the rest of his deeds will be corrupt.""**"
"1609","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The first matter about which the servant will be questioned on the Day of Resurrection is that his prayer will be inspected: if it is sound, he has succeeded; and if it is corrupt, he has failed and lost.""**"
"1610","Al-Ḥasan reported from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه—and he regarded it as traced back to the Prophet ﷺ—that he said: > **""An enslaved servant will surely be held to account for his prayer. When there is a deficiency in it, it will be said to him: 'Why did you fall short in it?' He will reply: 'O Lord, You placed over me a master who preoccupied me from my prayer.' So Allah will say: 'I saw that you would steal from his wealth for yourself; could you not then have stolen from your work for your own soul?' Thus Allah's conclusive argument will be established against him.""**"
"1611","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ mentioned the prayer one day and said: > **""Whoever safeguards it, it will be for him a light, a proof, and salvation on the Day of Resurrection; and whoever does not safeguard it, he will have neither light, nor proof, nor salvation, and on the Day of Resurrection he will be alongside Pharaoh, Hāmān, and Ubayy ibn Khalaf.""**"
"1612","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no share in Islam for whoever has no prayer, and there is no prayer for whoever has no ablution.""**"
"1613","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Islam consists of eight shares: Islam (the confession of faith) is a share, and prayer is a share...""**"
"1614","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no faith for the one who has no trustworthiness, no prayer for the one who has no purification, and no religion for the one who has no prayer. Indeed, the position of prayer in religion is like the position of the head to the body.""**"
"1615","Abū Salamah reported from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever does not pray Witr has no prayer."" When news of this reached ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, she said: ""Who heard this from Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ? By Allah, the era is not distant, nor have I forgotten! Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ only said: > **'Whoever comes on the Day of Resurrection with the five prayers, having preserved their ablution, their appointed times, their bowings, and their prostrations, without compromising anything of them, will arrive possessing a covenant with Allah that He will not punish him. And whoever comes having fallen short in them will have no covenant with Allah: if He wills, He will have mercy on him, and if He wills, He will punish him.'**"""
"1616","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Three things, whoever preserves them is truly an ally of Allah, and whoever neglects them is truly an enemy: prayer, fasting, and ritual purification from major impurity.""**"
"1617","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to those around him from his Ummah: > **""Guarantee for me six things, and I will guarantee Paradise for you.""** I said, ""What are they, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Prayer, zakah, trustworthiness, chastity of the private parts, restraining the belly, and guarding the tongue.""**"
"1618","Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported from his father رضي الله عنه: > **""Whenever a person embraced Islam, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would first teach us the prayer""**—or he said: **""would teach him the prayer.""**"
"1619","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, Allah has obligated upon His servants five prayers in every day and night.""**"
"1620","Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنهما said: > **""The first prayer that was made obligatory upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was Zuhr (the Noon prayer).""**"
"1621","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه said: ""The first prayer in which we performed bowing was ʿAṣr. I asked, 'O Messenger of Allah, what is this?' He ﷺ replied: > **'I have been commanded with this.'**"""
"1622","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away while his head was in the lap of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه, and he was saying to ʿAlī: > **""[Fear] Allah regarding those whom your right hands possess; [fear] Allah, Allah regarding the prayer!""** That was the last speech spoken by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"1623","Wāṣil reported: I met a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ named Nājiyah al-Ṭufāwī رضي الله عنه who used to transcribe copies of the Qur'an. A woman came to him and said, ""I have come to ask you concerning the prayer."" He said, ""You must be a wicked woman, or you have come from a wicked man!"" She said, ""Rather, I have come from a wicked man. My family married me off when I was a young virgin to a man from Banū Tamīm. Days would pass by him wherein he would neither touch water nor pray. Then he would come after three days, perform ablution with water, peck two prostrations, and recite: {Maintain with care the [obligatory] prayers and [in particular] the middle prayer and stand before Allah, devoutly obedient}."" Nājiyah said to her: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed five prayers: Zuhr, ʿAṣr, Maghrib, ʿIshāʾ, and Ṣubḥ.""** The woman then went back to her family and said, ""Rescue me from my husband, for he is indeed a wicked man!"""
"1624","Bayḥarah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported: We came to the Prophet ﷺ, embraced Islam, and asked him to relieve us from the ʿAtamah (late evening) prayer. He asked, **""The ʿAtamah prayer?""** We said, ""We are preoccupied with milking our camels."" He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, if Allah wills, you will both milk [your camels] and pray.""**"
"1625","Abū al-Bakhtarī reported: Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه had intimate relations with a slave-girl and then said to her in Persian, ""Pray."" She replied, ""No."" He said, ""Perform a single prostration."" She replied, ""No."" It was said to him, ""O Abū ʿAbdillāh, what benefit will a single prostration be to her?"" Salmān said: > **""If she had prayed, she would have prayed; and the one who has a share in Islam is not like the one who has no share.""**"
"1626","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Teach your children the prayer when they reach seven years of age, discipline them for it when they reach ten, and separate them in their sleeping places.""**"
"1627","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported: After the passing of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we found a parchment in the scabbard of his sword wherein was written: > **""In the Name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful: Separate the sleeping places of boys and girls, and brothers and sisters, at seven years of age; discipline your children for the prayer at seven; accursed, accursed is the one who claims lineage to other than his own people—or to other than his emancipators; and accursed is the one who unlawfully seizes any portion of land boundaries [meaning the public roads of the Muslims].""**"
"1628","ʿAbdullāh ibn Khubayb رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When a young boy knows his right hand from his left, then command him to pray.""**"
"1629","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Command them to pray at seven years of age, and discipline them for it at thirteen.""**"
"1630","Abū al-Ḥawrāʾ reported: I said to al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما, ""What do you remember from the Prophet ﷺ?"" He replied: > **""The five prayers.""**"
"1631","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Keep watchful care over your children regarding the prayer and accustom them to goodness, for indeed goodness is a habit.""**"
"1632","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: When my eyesight failed, it was said to me, ""We can treat your eyes, but you must leave the prayer for a few days."" He said: ""No, for indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **'Whoever abandons the prayer will meet Allah while He is angry with him.'**"""
"1633","Makḥūl reported from Umm Ayman رضي الله عنها that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not abandon the prayer deliberately, for indeed, whoever abandons the prayer deliberately, the protection of Allah and His Messenger is disowned from him.""**"
"1634","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever abandons the prayer deliberately has committed open disbelief.""**"
"1635","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Whoever abandons the prayer deliberately, the covenant of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is disowned from him.""**"
"1636","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah رضي الله عنه reported: I entered upon ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه while he was covered with a sheet, and I asked, ""How do you find him?"" They said, ""As you see."" I said, ""Awaken him with the prayer, for you will never awaken him with anything more alarming to him than the prayer."" They said, ""The prayer, O Commander of the Faithful!"" He said: > **""Yes, by Allah, indeed! And there is no share in Islam for whoever abandons the prayer.""** He then prayed while his wound was gushing with blood."
"1637","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever does not pray has no religion.""**"
"1638","Al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever abandons the prayer has disbelieved.""**"
"1639","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever abandons the prayer deliberately, his deeds are rendered null and void.""**"
"1640","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the Ṣubḥ prayer is under the protection of Allah, Blessed and Exalted. So do not violate Allah's covenant of protection, for whoever violates His protection, Allah will pursue him until He flings him down upon his face.""**"
"1641","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: Al-Ḥajjāj ordered Sālim ibn ʿAbdillāh to execute a man. Sālim asked the man, ""Did you perform the Ṣubḥ prayer?"" The man replied, ""Yes."" Sālim said to him, ""Depart."" Al-Ḥajjāj asked him, ""What prevented you from killing him?"" Sālim replied, ""My father narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **'Whoever performs the Ṣubḥ prayer is under the protection of Allah for that day.'** And I disliked to kill a man whom Allah has granted protection."" Al-Ḥajjāj said to Ibn ʿUmar, ""Did you hear this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" Ibn ʿUmar replied, ""Yes."""
"1642","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: When ʿItbān ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه suffered loss of his sight, he sent a message to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying, ""I would love for you to come and pray in my home and supplicate for blessing for us."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out with a group of his Companions and entered upon him. They sat talking among themselves and mentioned Mālik ibn al-Dukhshum. A man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, that man is a refuge and shelter for the hypocrites!"" They spoke much against him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Does he not pray?"" They said, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah, but a prayer in which there is no good."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said twice: > **""I have been forbidden from killing those who pray.""**"
"1643","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade the killing of those who pray.""** And in another narration: > **""He forbade striking those who pray.""**"
"1644","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the morning prayer and his covenant of protection is violated, then the sanctuary of Allah has been breached and His protection infringed upon, and I shall demand restitution on behalf of His covenant.""**"
"1645","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever performs the morning prayer is under the protection of Allah, so beware lest Allah demand from you anything concerning His protection.""**"
"1646","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the Ṣubḥ prayer in congregation is under the protection of Allah. So whoever violates the protection of Allah, Allah will fling him on his face into the Fire.""**"
"1647","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the morning prayer is under the protection of Allah. O son of Adam, let not Allah seek you out for anything concerning His protection!""**"
"1648","Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported from his father رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the Ṣubḥ prayer is under the protection of Allah, and his reckoning rests upon Allah.""**"
"1649","Al-Ḥārith, the freed slave of ʿUthmān, reported: ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه was seated one day and we were seated with him. The muezzin arrived, so ʿUthmān called for water in a vessel—I reckon it contained a mudd—and performed ablution. He then said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution like this ablution of mine, after which he said: > **""Whoever performs ablution like this ablution of mine, then stands and performs the Zuhr prayer, whatever occurred between it and the Ṣubḥ prayer will be forgiven for him. Then, when he performs ʿAṣr, whatever occurred between it and the Zuhr prayer will be forgiven for him. Then, when he performs Maghrib, whatever occurred between it and the ʿAṣr prayer will be forgiven for him. Then, when he performs ʿIshāʾ, whatever occurred between it and the Maghrib prayer will be forgiven for him. Thereafter, he might spend his night tossing and turning; then if he stands, performs ablution, and prays the Ṣubḥ prayer, whatever occurred between it and the ʿIshāʾ prayer will be forgiven for him. And these are the good deeds that eliminate evil deeds.""** They said, ""These are the good deeds; so what are the enduring righteous deeds (al-Bāqiyāt al-Ṣāliḥāt), O ʿUthmān?"" He replied: ""They are: *Lā ilāha illā Allāh* (there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah), *Subḥānallāh* (Glory be to Allah), *Al-ḥamdu lillāh* (All praise is due to Allah), *Allāhu akbar* (Allah is the Greatest), and *Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh* (There is no power and no strength except with Allah)."""
"1650","ʿĀmir ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ reported: I heard Saʿd and several people among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ say: There were two brothers during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and one of them was regarded as more virtuous than the other. The more virtuous one passed away first, and the other brother lived after him for forty nights and then died. The virtue of the first brother over the other was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he said, ""Did he not use to pray?"" They said, ""Indeed he did, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What makes you realize what his prayer has earned him? The parable of the prayer is only like that of an abundant, sweet running river at a man's door, into which he plunges five times every day. What do you see remaining of his dirt?""**"
"1651","Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī reported: I was with Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه under a tree. He took hold of a dry branch from it and shook it until its leaves fell away. He then said, ""O Abū ʿUthmān, will you not ask me why I did this?"" I asked, ""And why did you do it?"" He said: ""Thus did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do while I was with him under a tree. He took hold of a dry branch from it and shook it until its leaves fell away, and then said: 'O Salmān, will you not ask me why I did this?' I asked, 'And why did you do it?' He ﷺ replied: > **'Indeed, when a Muslim performs ablution and excels in his ablution, then performs the five prayers, his sins fall away just as these leaves fall away.'** And he recited: {And establish prayer at the two ends of the day and at the approach of the night. Indeed, good deeds do away with misdeeds. That is a reminder for those who remember} [Hūd: 114]."""
"1652","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The parable of the five prayers is like that of a sweet, flowing—or abundant—river at the door of one of you, in which he bathes five times every day; what would remain upon him of his dirt?""**"
"1653","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to say: > **""Indeed, every prayer sheds whatever sins preceded it.""**"
"1654","Abū al-Raṣāfah, a Bedouin from the people of the Levant belonging to Bāhilah, reported from Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No Muslim person reaches the time of a prescribed prayer, stands, performs ablution and excels in it, and prays and excels in his prayer, except that whatever sins were between it and the prayer before it are forgiven for him.""**"
"1655","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The five prayers are an expiation for what is between them.""** The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: > **""What do you think if a man were working, and between his home and his workplace were five rivers, so that when he reached his workplace he worked as Allah willed, becoming soiled with dirt or sweat, and each time he passed by a river he bathed—what would that leave of his dirt? Such is the prayer: whenever he commits a misdeed, then supplicates and seeks forgiveness, whatever preceded it is forgiven for him.""** And in another narration: > **""Then he performs a prayer and seeks forgiveness, and Allah forgives him whatever preceded it.""**"
"1656","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, these five verified prayers are expiations for the sins committed between them, so long as major sins are avoided.""**"
"1657","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The five prayers, and Friday to the next Friday, are expiations for what is between them, as long as major sins are avoided.""** And he ﷺ said: > **""In Friday there is an hour wherein no Muslim man or woman asks Allah for good except that He grants it to them.""** And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The parable of the five prayers is like an abundant river at the door of one of you, in which he bathes five times every day; what would remain of his dirt?""**"
"1658","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You burn, you burn [with sins]! Then when you pray the Ṣubḥ prayer, it washes it away. Then you burn, you burn! Then when you pray Zuhr, it washes it away. Then you burn, you burn! Then when you pray ʿAṣr, it washes it away. Then you burn, you burn! Then when you pray Maghrib, it washes it away. Then you burn, you burn! Then when you pray ʿIshāʾ, it washes it away. Then you sleep, and nothing is recorded against you until you awaken.""**"
"1659","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah has an angel who proclaims at every prayer: 'O children of Adam, rise up toward your fires that you have kindled against yourselves, and extinguish them!'""**"
"1660","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A caller is dispatched at the arrival of every prayer and proclaims: 'O children of Adam, rise and extinguish what you have kindled against yourselves!' So they rise, purify themselves—and their misdeeds fall away from their eyes—and pray, so whatever was between the two prayers is forgiven for them. Then they kindle [sins] in the time between that; when the first prayer (Zuhr) arrives, he proclaims: 'O children of Adam, rise and extinguish what you have kindled against yourselves!' So they rise, purify themselves, and pray, and whatever was between the two is forgiven for them. When ʿAṣr arrives, the like occurs; when Maghrib arrives, the like occurs; and when ʿIshāʾ arrives, the like occurs. Then they sleep, having been forgiven.""** The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: > **""Thus, there is one who journeys into goodness, and one who journeys into evil.""**"
"1661","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, the prayers are the good deeds: the expiation for what is between the first prayer (Zuhr) and ʿAṣr is the ʿAṣr prayer; the expiation for what is between ʿAṣr and Maghrib is the Maghrib prayer; and the expiation for what is between Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ is the ʿIshāʾ prayer. Then the Muslim retires to his bed without any sin upon him, as long as major sins are avoided.""** He then recited: {Indeed, good deeds do away with misdeeds} [Hūd: 114]."
"1662","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayers are expiations for what is between them; Allah has said: {Indeed, good deeds do away with misdeeds} [Hūd: 114].""**"
"1663","Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb reported: He stayed overnight with Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه to observe his exertion in worship. Salmān arose to pray during the latter part of the night—as though Ṭāriq did not see what he had expected—and mentioned that to him. Salmān said: ""Safeguard these five prayers, for indeed they are expiations for these wounds (minor sins) as long as fatal sins (such as murder) are avoided. When the people perform the ʿIshāʾ prayer, they disperse into three categories: one for whom it is a liability and not an asset, one for whom it is an asset and not a liability, and one for whom it is neither an asset nor a liability. As for the one for whom it is a liability and not an asset: a man who seized the darkness of the night and the heedlessness of people, mounting his steed in acts of disobedience; that is against him and not for him. As for the one for whom it is an asset and not a liability: a man who seized the darkness of the night and the heedlessness of people, standing up to pray; that is for him and not against him. And as for the one for whom it is neither an asset nor a liability: a man who prayed and then slept; that is neither for him nor against him. Beware of over-exhausting yourself in worship (al-ḥaqḥaqah), and adhere to moderation and consistency."""
"1664","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The obligatory prayer expiates what preceded it up until the next prayer; Friday expiates what preceded it up until the next Friday; the month of Ramadan expiates what preceded it up until the next Ramadan; and Hajj expiates what preceded it up until the next Hajj.""** He ﷺ then said: > **""It is not permissible for a Muslim woman to perform Hajj except in the company of a husband or a mahram (unmarriageable male relative).""**"
"1665","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The parable of the five prayers is like that of a sweet river flowing at the door of one of you, in which he bathes five times every day; what would remain upon him of dirt?""**"
"1666","Abū Muslim al-Thaʿlabī reported: I entered upon Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه while he was in the mosque removing lice and burying them in the pebbles. I said, ""O Abū Umāmah, a man narrated to me from you that you heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **'Whoever performs ablution and completes it thoroughly—washing his hands and his face, and wiping over his head and his ears—then stands up for an obligatory prayer, Allah forgives him on that day whatever his feet walked toward, whatever his hands grasped, whatever his ears listened to, whatever his eyes looked at, and whatever evil thoughts occurred within his soul.'**"" Abū Umāmah said, ""By Allah, I have indeed heard it from the Prophet ﷺ repeatedly!"""
"1667","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The five prayers, and Friday to Friday, are expiations for what is between them, as long as major sins are avoided.""**"
"1668","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Muslim prays while his sins are placed upon his head; whenever he prostrates, they fall away from him, so he finishes his prayer with his sins having fallen away from him.""**"
"1669","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, when the believing servant stands up for prayer, his sins are placed upon his head, and then they disperse from him just as the branches of a tree disperse to the right and to the left.""**"
"1670","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When a servant stands to pray, his sins are gathered upon his neck; then whenever he bows in rukūʿ, they disperse and fall away.""**"
"1671","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no state in which a servant can be that is more beloved to Allah than seeing him prostrating, rubbing his face in the dust [in devotion].""**"
"1672","Al-Ḥārith reported from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, who said: We were with the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque awaiting the prayer when a man stood up and said, ""I have committed a sin."" The Prophet ﷺ turned away from him. When the Prophet ﷺ finished the prayer, the man stood up and repeated what he had said. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Did you not pray this prayer with us and perform its purification properly?"" The man replied, ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: ""Then indeed, it is an expiation for your sin.""**"
"1673","Yūsuf ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Salām said: I came to Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه in the Levant (al-Shām), and he asked, ""What brought you to this land, my son, and what purpose directed you here?"" I replied, ""Nothing brought me except maintaining the bond that existed between you and my father."" He took my hand, seated me before him, and said: ""Wretched is the hour in which a lie is attributed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ! I heard the Prophet ﷺ saying: > **""There is no Muslim who commits a sin, then performs ablution, then prays two or four rakʿahs—whether obligatory or non-obligatory—and then seeks Allah's forgiveness, except that Allah forgives him.""**"
"1674","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him about the best of deeds. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Prayer."" The man asked, ""Then what?"" He ﷺ replied: ""Prayer."" The man asked again, and he repeated: ""Prayer,"" three times. When the man pressed further, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Jihād in the path of Allah."" The man said, ""Indeed, I have two living parents."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I enjoin you to treat your parents well."" The man said, ""By the One Who sent you with the truth as a Prophet, I shall certainly wage jihād and leave them behind!"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""You know best.""**"
"1675","A man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked, ""Which deed is the best?"" Shuʿbah said: He ﷺ replied: > **""The best of deeds is prayer at its appointed time, dutifulness to parents, and jihād.""**"
"1676","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""O ʿĀʾishah! Forsake sins, for that is the finest emigration (hijrah); and guard the prayers, for indeed they are the most virtuous righteousness.""**"
"1677","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the prayers at their appointed times, completes their ablution thoroughly, and fulfills their standing, their humility (khushūʿ), their bowing, and their prostrating—the prayer ascends radiant and luminous, saying: 'May Allah preserve you as you have preserved me.' But whoever prays them outside their appointed times, does not complete their ablution thoroughly, and does not fulfill their humility, their bowing, or their prostrating—the prayer ascends dark and murky, saying: 'May Allah cause you to be lost as you have lost me.' Then, when it reaches wherever Allah wills, it is folded up just as a worn-out garment is folded, and then struck against his face.""**"
"1678","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us while we were seven individuals—four from our freed slaves and three from our Arabs—leaning our backs against his mosque. He asked, ""What has caused you to sit here?"" We replied, ""We sat waiting for the prayer."" He paused in silence for a brief moment, then turned toward us and asked: ""Do you know what your Lord says?"" We replied, ""No."" He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, your Lord says: 'Whoever performs the five prayers at their appointed times, guards them, and does not neglect them out of disdain for their right, has a covenant with Me that I shall admit him into Paradise. But whoever does not pray them at their appointed times, does not guard them, and neglects them out of disdain for their right, has no covenant with Me: if I will, I may punish him; and if I will, I may forgive him.'""**"
"1679","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed by his Companions one day and asked them: ""Do you know what your Lord, Blessed and Exalted, says?"" They replied, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He asked this three times, then said: > **""[Allah says:] 'By My Might and My Majesty, no one performs it at its appointed time except that I shall admit him into Paradise; and whoever prays it outside its appointed time, if I will, I shall have mercy upon him, and if I will, I shall punish him.'""**"
"1680","ʿIyāḍ رضي الله عنه—a man from the tribe of ʿAbd al-Qays—narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ saying: > **""Hold fast to the remembrance of your Lord, and offer your prayer at its earliest time, for indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will multiply your reward.""**"
"1681","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Jibrīl led me in prayer: he prayed Ẓuhr when the sun declined past the meridian; he prayed ʿAṣr when an object's shadow was equal to its length; he prayed Maghrib when the sun set; he prayed ʿIshāʾ when the twilight disappeared; and he prayed Fajr when dawn broke. Then on the following day, he prayed Ẓuhr when the shadow of everything was equal to its length; he prayed ʿAṣr when an object's shadow was twice its length; he prayed Maghrib when the sun set; he prayed ʿIshāʾ at the end of the first third of the night; and he prayed Ṣubḥ when the sun was on the verge of rising. Then he said: 'The time for prayer is between these two times.'""**"
"1682","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه recounted that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed them that Jibrīl عليه السلام came to him and led him in the prayers at two distinct times, with the exception of Maghrib: > **""He came to me and led me in Ẓuhr when my shadow was like the strap of my sandal; then he came to me and led me in ʿAṣr when my shadow was equal to my height; then he came to me for Maghrib and led me the moment the sun set; then he came to me for ʿIshāʾ and prayed the moment the twilight vanished; then he came to me for Fajr and led me the moment the dawn shone forth. Then he came to me the following day and prayed Ẓuhr when the shadow was equal to my height; then he came to me for ʿAṣr and led me when my shadow was twice my height; then he came to me for Maghrib and led me when the sun set—not changing it from its first time; then he came to me for ʿIshāʾ and led me when the first third of the night had passed; then he delayed Fajr until dawn was fully luminous so that I could not see any star in the sky. Then he said: 'Whatever is between these two is the appointed time.'""**"
"1683","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to pray Ẓuhr when the sun declined past the meridian, ʿAṣr while the sun was still white and pure, Maghrib when the sun set, and ʿIshāʾ when the twilight vanished; and as for Fajr, he would sometimes pray it as soon as the dawn broke, and sometimes delay it [until the morning became bright].""**"
"1684","Bayān said: I said to Anas رضي الله عنه, ""Narrate to me concerning the times of prayer observed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" He said: > **""He used to pray Ẓuhr at the declining of the sun (dalūk al-shams); he prayed ʿAṣr between your first prayer [Ẓuhr] and ʿAṣr; he prayed Maghrib at sunset; he prayed ʿIshāʾ at the vanishing of the twilight; and he prayed the morning prayer (Ghadāh) at the break of dawn when sight begins to distinguish things clearly. All that is between these is the appointed time—or he said: prayer.""**"
"1685","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه narrated: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ asking him about the appointed times of prayer. So he instructed Bilāl رضي الله عنه [over two days], who prayed early [on the first day] and delayed [on the second day]. The Prophet ﷺ then said: > **""The time is whatever lies between the two.""**"
"1686","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: A man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the time of prayer. When the sun declined, Bilāl called the adhān for Ẓuhr, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for ʿAṣr when we thought that a man's shadow was longer than him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for Maghrib when the sun set, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for ʿIshāʾ when the daylight's whiteness disappeared—which is the twilight—and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for Fajr when dawn broke, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. On the following day, Bilāl called the adhān for Ẓuhr when the sun declined, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed it until the shadow of everything was equal to its length, then commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for ʿAṣr, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed it until the shadow of everything was twice its length, then commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for Maghrib when the sun set, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed it until the daylight whiteness—which is the twilight in our view—was on the verge of disappearing, then commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he called the adhān for ʿIshāʾ when the twilight disappeared, and we slept and woke up repeatedly until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us and said: > **""No one among mankind is waiting for this prayer except you. Indeed, you remain in prayer as long as you are awaiting it. Were it not that I would place hardship upon my ummah, I would have commanded that this prayer be delayed until midnight or close to midnight.""** Then Bilāl called the adhān for Fajr, and the Prophet ﷺ delayed it until the sun was about to rise, then commanded him, so he called the iqāmah and he ﷺ prayed. Then he ﷺ said: > **""The time is whatever lies between these two.""**"
"1687","Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī or Bashīr ibn Abī Masʿūd رضي الله عنهما—both of whom had the honor of the Prophet's ﷺ companionship—narrated: Jibrīl عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ when the sun declined past the meridian and said, ""O Muḥammad, pray Ẓuhr."" So he stood and prayed Ẓuhr. Then Jibrīl came to him when the shadow of an object was equal to its length and said, ""O Muḥammad, pray ʿAṣr."" So he stood and prayed. Then Jibrīl came to him when the sun set and said, ""O Muḥammad, pray Maghrib."" So he prayed. Then he came to him when the red twilight vanished and said, ""O Muḥammad, stand and pray ʿIshāʾ."" So he stood and prayed. Then he came to him when dawn broke and said, ""O Muḥammad, stand and pray Ṣubḥ."" So he stood and prayed. Then he came to him on the following day when the shadow of everything was equal to its length and said, ""O Muḥammad, stand and pray Ẓuhr."" So he stood and prayed Ẓuhr. Then he came to him when the shadow of everything was twice its length and said, ""O Muḥammad, pray ʿAṣr."" So he stood and prayed. Then he came to him when the sun set—at that exact same time—and said, ""O Muḥammad, pray Maghrib."" So he stood and prayed. Then he came to him when a period of the night had elapsed and said, ""O Muḥammad, stand and pray."" Then he came to him when dawn had grown fully luminous and said, ""O Muḥammad, pray Ṣubḥ."" So he stood and prayed. Then he said: > **""Whatever is between these two is the appointed time.""**"
"1688","Mujāhid said: I used to lead the mount of my master, Qays ibn al-Sāʾib رضي الله عنه, and he would ask, ""Has the sun declined?"" When I said, ""Yes,"" he would pray Ẓuhr and say: > **""Thus did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do: the Prophet ﷺ used to pray ʿAṣr while the sun was white and vibrant; he used to pray Maghrib while the fasting person might still doubt whether it was time to break his fast; and he used to pray Fajr when light enveloped the sky.""**"
"1689","ʿAlqamah narrated that a man asked ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه about the time of Ẓuhr, and he said: > **""It begins when a man's shadow is directly underfoot until the shadow of everything becomes equal to its length.""** The man then asked him about the time of ʿAṣr, and he said: > **""Pray it while the sun is still white and vibrant.""** And he asked him about the time of Maghrib, and he said: > **""When the sun has set.""**"
"1690","Qatādah reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""Indeed, prayer has an appointed time just like the appointed time for Ḥajj.""**"
"1691","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""We complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the intense heat of the sun-baked ground [during the midday prayer], but he did not grant us relief [by delaying it further].""**"
"1692","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about praying in the scorching midday heat (al-hājirah), but he did not grant us relief.""**"
"1693","Masrūq said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه led us in prayer when the sun declined past the meridian. I asked Sulaymān, ""Is this Ẓuhr?"" He replied, ""Yes."" ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه said: > **""By the One besides Whom there is no deity worthy of worship, this is the appointed time of this prayer.""**"
"1694","Khashf ibn Mālik reported: > **""ʿAbdullāh [ibn Masʿūd] رضي الله عنه used to pray Ẓuhr while the grasshoppers were leaping violently from the heat of the scorching ground.""**"
"1695","Khabbāb رضي الله عنه said: We complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the heat of the scorching stones, but he did not grant us relief, and he said: > **""When the sun declines past the meridian, then pray.""**"
"1696","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and one of us would hold pebbles in his hand [to cool them]; once they had cooled, he would place them down and prostrate upon them.""**"
"1697","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما said: We complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the scorching ground, but he did not grant us relief, and he said: > **""Abundantly recite: 'Lā ḥawla wa lā quwwata illā billāh' (There is no power and no might except with Allah), for indeed it wards off ninety-nine gates of distress, the least of which is grief.""**"
"1698","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the shadow reaches a cubit and a half up to two cubits, then offer the Ẓuhr prayer.""**"
"1699","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه narrated: Abū Maḥdhūrah called the adhān for Ẓuhr while ʿUmar was in Makkah, raising his voice loudly when the sun declined past the meridian. ʿUmar said, ""O Abū Maḥdhūrah! Did you not fear that your throat would rupture?"" He replied, ""I wanted you to hear it."" ʿUmar رضي الله عنه then said: Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""Cool down the prayer when the heat intensifies, for severe heat is from the simmering flare of Hell. Hell disputed within itself until its parts consumed one another, so it sought permission from Allah, Mighty and Majestic, for two breaths, and He granted it permission: thus, the severity of heat is from its simmering heat, and the severity of bitter frost is from its freezing cold (zamharīr).""**"
"1700","Al-Qāsim ibn Ṣafwān narrated from his father رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Delay the Ẓuhr prayer until it cools, for indeed the severity of heat is from the raging heat of Hell.""**"
"1701","Shuʿbah said: I heard Ḥajjāj ibn Ḥajjāj al-Aslamī—who was their imam—narrating from his father, who had performed Ḥajj with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, from a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ [deemed to be ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd], who reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the severity of heat is from the raging heat of Hell; therefore, when the heat intensifies, delay the prayer until it cools.""**"
"1702","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the severity of heat is from the raging heat of Hell, so delay the prayer until it cools.""**"
"1703","ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Delay the Ẓuhr prayer until it cools, for indeed the severity of heat is from the raging heat of Hell.""**"
"1704","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""The sun rises from Hell upon the horn of Satan—or between the two horns of Satan—and it does not rise by the span of a reed except that a gate among the gates of the Fire is opened; then when the heat reaches its peak, all of its gates are opened wide.""**"
"1705","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jāriyah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Delay Ẓuhr until it cools.""**"
"1706","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray Ẓuhr during the winter days at such a time that we could not tell whether more of the day had elapsed or more remained.""**"
"1707","Abū Arwā رضي الله عنه said: > **""I used to pray the ʿAṣr prayer with the Prophet ﷺ in Madīnah, and then I would reach Dhū al-Ḥulayfah before the sun set—and it is a distance of two parasangs.""**"
"1708","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command the delaying of the ʿAṣr prayer.""**"
"1709","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd reported: > **""Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to delay the ʿAṣr prayer.""**"
"1710","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: > **""The two men from the Anṣār whose dwellings were farthest from the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ were Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir—from the people of Qubāʾ—and Abū ʿAbs ibn Jabr, whose dwelling was among Banū Ḥārithah. They would both pray ʿAṣr with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and then return to their respective clans while their people had not yet prayed, owing to the promptness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in offering the ʿAṣr prayer.""**"
"1711","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray ʿAṣr early enough that a man could walk all the way to Banū Ḥārithah ibn al-Ḥārith and return before the sun set.""**"
"1712","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We used to pray with the Prophet ﷺ, and then I would go to my clan and say to them: 'Stand and pray, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has already prayed!'""**"
"1713","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, this prayer—meaning ʿAṣr—was made obligatory upon those who came before you, but they neglected it. So whoever among you preserves it today shall be given his reward twice over. And there is no prayer after it until the witness is seen—meaning the star.""**"
"1714","Abū Ayyūb reported from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما, who said: > **""The time for ʿAṣr remains valid as long as the time for Maghrib has not arrived.""**"
"1715","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Nawfal ibn Muʿāwiyah reported from his father [or grandfather Nawfal ibn Muʿāwiyah] رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""That one of you should lose his entire family and wealth is far better for him than missing the appointed time of the ʿAṣr prayer.""**"
"1716","Abū Ṭarīf رضي الله عنه said: > **""I was with the Prophet ﷺ during the siege of al-Ṭāʾif, and he would pray [Maghrib / ʿAṣr] at such an early hour that if a man were to shoot an arrow, he would clearly see where his arrows landed.""**"
"1717","Al-Zibriqān reported: A group of Quraysh were gathered together when Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه passed by them. They sent two of their young servants to ask him about the Middle Prayer (al-Ṣalāt al-Wusṭā), and he replied, ""It is the ʿAṣr prayer."" Then two men from among them went up to him and asked him personally, and he said, ""It is Ẓuhr."" They then proceeded to Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه and questioned him, and he replied: > **""It is Ẓuhr. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray Ẓuhr in the intense midday heat while there would only be one or two rows behind him, as people were taking their midday rest (qaylūlah) or engaged in their trade. Thereupon Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: 'Guard strictly the prayers, especially the Middle Prayer, and stand before Allah in devout obedience' [al-Baqarah: 238].""**"
"1718","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The most virtuous prayer is the Maghrib prayer; and whoever prays two rakʿahs after it, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1719","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Prophet ﷺ fought an enemy and was not free from battle until ʿAṣr was delayed past its appointed time. When he saw that, he said: > **""O Allah! Whoever prevented us from offering the Middle Prayer, fill their homes with fire and fill their graves with fire!""**"
"1720","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The Middle Prayer is the ʿAṣr prayer.""**"
"1721","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""On the Day of the Confederates (al-Aḥzāb), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was occupied and unable to pray Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, and he was reminded of them after Maghrib [so he made them up].""**"
"1722","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on the Day of the Confederates: > **""They distracted us from the Middle Prayer! May Allah fill their homes and their graves with fire!""**"
"1723","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that on the Day of the Trench (al-Khandaq), the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""May Allah fill their homes and their graves with fire, just as they kept us occupied from the Middle Prayer until the sun set!""**"
"1724","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه arrived and stayed in Damascus as a guest of Abū Kulthūm al-Dawsī. They began discussing the Middle Prayer, so Abū Hurayrah said: We differed concerning it just as you have differed while we were in the courtyard of the home of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Among us was the righteous man, Abū Hāshim ibn ʿUtbah ibn Rabīʿah ibn ʿAbd Shams, who said, ""I will find out the truth of that for you."" So he went to the Prophet ﷺ—and he was frank and unhesitant with him—asked permission, entered upon him, and then came out to us and informed us: > **""Indeed, it is the ʿAṣr prayer.""**"
"1725","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Aflaḥ reported: A group of the Companions sent me to Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما to ask him about the Middle Prayer. He said: > **""We used to say among ourselves that it is the prayer during which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was directed toward the Qiblah: the Ẓuhr prayer.""**"
"1726","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""They distracted us from the Middle Prayer—the ʿAṣr prayer; may Allah fill their bellies and their hearts with fire!""**"
"1727","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We used to pray Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return to our homes a mile away, and I could still clearly see where arrows would land.""**"
"1728","Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه said: > **""We used to pray Maghrib with the Prophet ﷺ and then depart to the marketplace; if one of us were to shoot an arrow, you would surely see where his arrow landed.""**"
"1729","ʿAlī ibn Bilāl reported from several men among the Anṣār, who said: > **""We used to pray Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and then depart, practicing archery as we walked until we reached our quarters, and the landing spots of our arrows were never hidden from our sight.""**"
"1730","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My ummah will continue to remain upon the pristine natural state (fiṭrah) so long as they offer the Maghrib prayer before the stars appear.""**"
"1731","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Offer the Maghrib prayer at the time the fasting person breaks his fast, and hasten to pray it before the stars appear.""**"
"1732","Abū Ṭarīf رضي الله عنه said: > **""I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he besieged al-Ṭāʾif, and he would lead us in prayer [at its earliest time] such that if a man were to shoot an arrow, he would clearly see where his arrow landed.""**"
"1733","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: > **""We used to pray Maghrib with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return to Banū Salamah—which was at the farthest edge of Madīnah—and we could still clearly see where our arrows fell.""**"
"1734","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik reported that a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ informed him: > **""They used to pray the Maghrib prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, then return to Banū Salamah while still being able to see where arrows landed when shot.""**"
"1735","Abū Maḥdhūrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you call the adhān for Maghrib, deliver it swiftly while the sun is dropping below the horizon.""**"
"1736","Abū Maḥdhūrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""At the time of Maghrib, deliver the call swiftly while the sun is dropping below the horizon.""**"
"1737","Al-Ḥārith ibn Wahb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My ummah will continue to remain steadfast upon Islam so long as they do not delay Maghrib until the stars entangle in imitation of the Jews, and so long as they do not rush the Fajr prayer [before its proper time] in imitation of the Christians, and so long as they do not leave funerals solely to the family of the deceased.""**"
"1738","Al-Ṣunābiḥī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My ummah will continue to hold firmly to their religion so long as they do not wait for the entanglement of the stars for Maghrib in imitation of the Jews, and so long as they do not delay Fajr in imitation of the Christians.""**"
"1739","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd said: We were with ʿAbdullāh [ibn Masʿūd] رضي الله عنه on the road to Makkah. When the sun set, he said, ""This is the darkening of the night (ghasaq al-layl)."" Then he called the adhān and said: > **""By the One besides Whom there is no deity worthy of worship, this is the appointed time of this prayer.""**"
"1740","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd also narrated: We were with Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, and when the sun set, he said: > **""By the One besides Whom there is no deity worthy of worship, this is the moment the sun has dipped (dalakat al-shams) and the time for prayer has commenced.""**"
"1741","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""The 'dulūk' of the sun means its setting. The Arabs say when the sun has set: 'dalakat' (it has dipped below the horizon).""**"
"1742","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning the verse: *'{Until the darkness of the night (ghasaq al-layl)}'* [al-Isrāʾ: 78]: > **""It refers to the late night prayer (al-ʿIshāʾ al-Ākhirah).""**"
"1743","Al-Munkadir reported from the Prophet ﷺ: One night, the Prophet ﷺ came out after having delayed the ʿIshāʾ prayer until a portion of the night had passed, while the people were waiting in the mosque. He asked: > **""What are you waiting for?"" They replied, ""We are waiting for the prayer."" He ﷺ said: ""Indeed, you will continue to be in prayer as long as you are waiting for it."" Then he said: ""Indeed, it is a prayer that was never prayed by any of the nations before you."" Then he raised his head toward the sky and said: ""The stars are the guardians of the sky; so when the stars are extinguished, there shall come upon the sky that which it was promised. And I am the guardian of my Companions; so when I am taken away, there shall come upon my Companions that which they were promised. And my Companions are the guardians of my ummah; so when my Companions are taken away, there shall come upon my ummah that which they were promised. O Bilāl, call the iqāmah!""**"
"1744","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the ʿIshāʾ prayer one night, then came out to the mosque to find the people awaiting the prayer. He said: > **""Indeed, there is no one from among the adherents of any religion who remembers Allah, Mighty and Majestic, at this hour except you.""** (ʿAbdullāh) said: And this verse was revealed: *'{They are not all alike; among the People of the Scripture is an upright community who recite the revelations of Allah during the night while prostrating... and whatever good they do, they will never be denied the reward thereof, and Allah is All-Knowing of the righteous}'* [Āl ʿImrān: 113–115]."
"1745","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: One night, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was detained with some of his family or wives, so he did not come out to us for the late night prayer (ʿIshāʾ) until a portion of the night had passed. When he came to us, some of us were praying while others were lying down. He gave us glad tidings, saying: > **""Indeed, none among the People of the Scripture performs this prayer.""** Thereupon, the verse was revealed: *{They are not [all] the same...}* [Āl ʿImrān: 113]."
"1746","Abū al-Zubayr narrated: I asked Jābir رضي الله عنه, ""Did you hear the Prophet ﷺ say: 'A man is in prayer as long as he is waiting for the prayer'?"" He replied, ""We waited for the Prophet ﷺ for the night prayer (al-ʿAtamah), and he was detained from coming to us until it was close to midnight or had reached midnight. Then the Prophet ﷺ arrived, and we performed the prayer. Afterward, he said: 'Sit down.' He then addressed us, saying: > **""Indeed, the people have prayed and gone to sleep, but you will continually be regarded as being in prayer as long as you are waiting for the prayer. Were it not for the weakness of the weak and the old age of the elderly, I would have delayed this prayer until the middle of the night.""**"
"1747","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ [waiting for the ʿIshāʾ prayer]; I fell asleep and then woke up, then fell asleep and woke up again. A man among the Muslims stood up and called out: > **""The prayer! The prayer!""** [And the narrator mentioned the rest of the hadith.]"
"1748","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the ʿIshāʾ prayer one night until we fell asleep, then awoke, then fell asleep, then awoke—and he was detained only on account of a delegation that had come to him—then he came out.""**"
"1749","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Prophet ﷺ delayed the ʿIshāʾ prayer late into the night. ʿUmar رضي الله عنه called out to him, ""The women and children have gone to sleep!"" Thereupon, the Prophet ﷺ [came out and] said: > **""No one among the inhabitants of the earth is waiting for this prayer except you.""**"
"1750","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Prophet ﷺ delayed the ʿIshāʾ prayer until the people in the mosque departed, except for ʿUthmān ibn Maẓʿūn and a small group of the Prophet’s ﷺ Companions—about fifteen or sixteen men, not reaching seventeen. ʿUthmān said, ""I will not leave tonight until the Prophet ﷺ comes out, so that I may pray with him and learn what kept him."" The Prophet ﷺ came out around one-third of the night, accompanied by Bilāl رضي الله عنه. Seeing no one in the main area of the mosque, he then heard the soft tone of their voices from a corner of the mosque. He walked over to them, greeted them with peace, and asked, ""What has kept you at this hour?"" They replied, ""O Prophet of Allah, we waited for you so that we might attend the prayer with you."" He ﷺ said to them: > **""No nation before you has ever prayed this prayer of yours, and you have remained in prayer ever since [you began waiting]. Indeed, the stars are a security for the heavens; when the stars are extinguished, there shall come upon the inhabitants of heaven that which they are promised. And I am a security for my Companions; when I depart, there shall come upon my Companions that which they are promised. And my Companions are a security for my Ummah; when my Companions depart, there shall come upon my Ummah that which they are promised.""**"
"1751","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mustawrid رضي الله عنه narrated: The Prophet ﷺ was detained one night until there remained only a dozen or so men in the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to them and said: > **""No one has reached the evening waiting for the prayer except you. Indeed, Allah has made the stars a security for the dwellers of heaven; when they are extinguished, that which the dwellers of heaven are promised draws near. And Allah has made my Companions a security for my Ummah; when my Companions pass away, there shall come upon my Ummah that which they are promised.""**"
"1752","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were it not for the weakness of the weak and the illness of the sick, I would have delayed the late night prayer (al-ʿAtamah).""**"
"1753","A man from the tribe of Juhaynah رضي الله عنه narrated: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, ""When should I pray the late ʿIshāʾ prayer?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""When darkness fills the depth of every valley.""**"
"1754","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about the time of ʿIshāʾ, and he said: > **""When darkness fills the depth of every valley.""**"
"1755","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to delay the late ʿIshāʾ prayer.""**"
"1756","Umm Anas رضي الله عنها narrated: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, sleep overcomes my eyes before the late ʿIshāʾ prayer."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: > **""Perform it early, O Umm Sulaym. When darkness fills the depth of every valley, the time of prayer has arrived; so pray, and there is no sin upon you.""**"
"1757","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ delayed the ʿIshāʾ prayer for nine nights—or eight nights—until one-third of the night had passed. Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه said to him: > **""O Messenger of Allah, if you were to pray it earlier, it would be more suitable for our standing in prayer at night.""** He said: ""So after that, he ﷺ prayed it earlier."""
"1758","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not let the Bedouins prevail over you concerning the name of your prayer; for indeed, it is [named] al-ʿIshāʾ in the Book of Allah. The Bedouins only call it al-ʿAtamah on account of milking their camels [at that late hour].""**"
"1759","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I was a man prone to falling asleep; after praying Maghrib, while still in my clothes, I would fall asleep before ʿIshāʾ. So I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about that, and he granted me a concession.""**"
"1760","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever sleeps before ʿIshāʾ, may his eye find no sleep!""** ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها added, ""I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sleep before it, nor engage in conversation after it."""
"1761","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, said: > **""I never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sleeping before ʿIshāʾ, nor engaging in idle talk after it; he was either remembering Allah and thereby gaining reward, or sleeping and thereby remaining safe.""** ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها also said: > **""Conversation late at night is permitted only for three: a bridegroom, a traveler, or one engaged in the Tahajjud prayer at night.""**"
"1762","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is to be no late-night conversation after the prayer—meaning the late ʿIshāʾ prayer—except for one of two persons: one who is praying, or a traveler.""**"
"1763","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prohibited sleeping before ʿIshāʾ and talking after it.""**"
"1764","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: We used to take turns guarding and serving the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, staying the night near him in case he needed something or a matter arose during the night for which he might dispatch us. As those seeking reward and taking turns grew in number, we sat talking. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us during the night and said: > **""What is this whispering? Have I not forbidden you from whispering?""** We said, ""We repent to Allah, O Prophet of Allah!"" [And he mentioned the rest of the hadith.]"
"1765","Shaddād ibn Aws رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever composes or recites a line of poetry after the late ʿIshāʾ prayer, his prayer for that night will not be accepted.""**"
"1766","Maḥmūd ibn Labīd al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray the Fajr prayer when the dawn becomes bright, for indeed it yields the greatest reward.""**"
"1767","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My Ummah will continue to remain upon the innate natural disposition (Fiṭrah) as long as they pray the Fajr prayer when the morning has brightened.""**"
"1768","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray the Fajr prayer when the morning has brightened, for indeed it yields the greatest reward—or: it brings greater reward for you.""**"
"1769","Bilāl رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray the Fajr prayer when the dawn becomes bright, for indeed it yields the greatest reward.""**"
"1770","ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah reported from his father, from his grandfather [Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān] رضي الله عنه, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray the Fajr prayer when the dawn becomes bright, for indeed it brings greater reward for you—or: it yields the greatest reward.""**"
"1771","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray the morning prayer when the dawn has brightened, for indeed it yields the greatest reward.""**"
"1772","Ibn Bujayd narrated from his grandmother Ḥawwāʾ رضي الله عنها—who was among those women who gave the pledge of allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ—that she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Pray the Fajr prayer when the dawn becomes bright, for indeed it yields the greatest reward.""**"
"1773","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd narrated: > **""ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to perform the Fajr prayer when the dawn had brightened.""**"
"1774","Al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd narrated: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""Be moderate and brief in prayer, for behind you are the elderly, the weak, and those with urgent needs.""** [Al-Ḥārith] added, ""We used to pray Fajr with our imam while still dressed in our garments, and he would recite one of the long chapters containing over a hundred verses; then we would go to ʿAbdullāh [ibn Masʿūd] and still find him in prayer [having delayed starting it until the dawn brightened]."""
"1775","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Bilāl رضي الله عنه: > **""Delay the morning prayer until there is sufficient light, such that the people can see where their arrows fall due to the brightness of the dawn.""**"
"1776","Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Perform the morning prayer in sufficient light, to the extent that the people can see where their arrows fall.""**"
"1777","Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated: I was with Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما at a funeral when I heard the voice of a person crying out aloud. He sent someone to him and silenced him. I asked, ""O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, why did you silence him?"" He replied, ""Indeed, the deceased is tormented by it until he is placed in his grave."" I then said to him, ""Sometimes I pray the morning prayer behind you, and when I turn around I cannot discern the face of the person sitting next to me [due to darkness], while at other times you pray it when the light has spread."" He replied: > **""In that manner did I see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray, and I love to pray it just as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying it.""**"
"1778","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ṣunābiḥī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""My Ummah will never cease to be in goodness so long as they do not commit three things: so long as they do not delay Maghrib waiting for total darkness in imitation of the Jews; so long as they do not delay Fajr until the vanishing of the stars in imitation of the Christians; and so long as they do not leave the preparation and burial of funerals solely to the family of the deceased.""**"
"1779","Zayd ibn Ḥārithah رضي الله عنه narrated: A man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the time of the morning prayer, and he ﷺ replied, ""Pray it with me today and tomorrow."" When he was at the plain of Namirah near al-Juḥfah, he prayed it as soon as the dawn broke. Later, when he was at Dhū Ṭuwā, he delayed it until people began to wonder, ""Has the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away?"" and they said, ""What if we pray?"" Then the Prophet ﷺ came out and prayed it just before sunrise. Afterward, he turned toward the people and asked, ""What did you say?"" They replied, ""We said: 'What if we pray?'"" He said: > **""Had you done so, a punishment would have afflicted you.""** Then he called the questioner and said: > **""The prayer is between these two times.""**"
"1780","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The Prophet ﷺ was asked about the time of the morning prayer; so he prayed it when the dawn broke, and then [the following day] he prayed it when the morning had brightened. Afterwards he said: > **""Where is the one who asked about the time of the morning prayer? The time is between these two [extremes].""**"
"1781","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jāriyah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Fajr one day while it was still dark (ghalas), and then on another day he prayed it after the light had spread. Then he said: > **""The time [for prayer] is between these two.""**"
"1782","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ about the time of the morning prayer. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed it while it was still dark, and on the following day he prayed it when the light had spread. Then he said: ""Where is the questioner?"" The man replied, ""Here I am."" He ﷺ said: > **""The time is between [the times of] yesterday and today.""**"
"1783","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We used to pray the morning prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and then we would depart while we could not recognize one another [due to the darkness].""**"
"1784","ʿUrwah ibn Muḍarris رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform the Fajr prayer as soon as the dawn broke.""**"
"1785","Ḥarmalah رضي الله عنه narrated: I set out with a delegation of my tribe to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He led us in the morning prayer; when he said the Taslīm, I looked at the face of the person beside me and could hardly recognize him due to the darkness. I then said, ""O Messenger of Allah, advise me."" He ﷺ said: > **""Fear Allah; and when you are among people, if you hear them saying things [about you] that please you, then adopt those traits; and if you hear them saying things [about you] that you dislike, then abandon them.""**"
"1786","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""Women used to attend the morning prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they would return wrapped in their woolen cloaks, unrecognizable on account of the darkness.""**"
"1787","Bilāl رضي الله عنه narrated: I called the Adhān on a bitterly cold morning, and the people were slow in coming to the prayer. The Prophet ﷺ asked, ""What has happened to the people, O Bilāl?"" I said, ""The cold has detained them."" Thereupon, the Prophet ﷺ supplicated: > **""O Allah, remove the cold from them!""** Bilāl added, ""I then saw them fanning themselves during the morning prayer [from the warmth that ensued]."""
"1788","ʿAmr ibn Dīnār reported that he heard the son of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd say: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to pray the morning prayer in the early darkness, just as Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما prayed it in the early darkness, and he would pray Maghrib as soon as the sun set. He would say: > **""Indeed, it is just as Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, said: *{...until the darkness of the night and [also] the Quran [recitation] of dawn. Indeed, the recitation of dawn is ever witnessed.}* [Al-Isrāʾ: 78]""**"
"1789","ʿUmārah ibn Ruʾaybah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs the prayer before the rising of the sun and before its setting, and bears witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, shall enter Paradise.""**"
"1790","Abū ʿUbaydah reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""The two guard shifts from among the angels of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic—the guardians of the night and the guardians of the day—meet at the break of dawn. Recite if you wish: *{...and [also] the Quran of dawn. Indeed, the recitation of dawn is ever witnessed.}* [Al-Isrāʾ: 78]""**"
"1791","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما passed by a man who was sleeping after the morning prayer. He nudged him with his foot until he awoke and said: > **""Did you not know that Allah, the Exalted, gazes upon His creation at this hour, and enters a multitude of them into Paradise through His mercy?""**"
"1792","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: When we returned from the expedition of al-Ḥudaybiyah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked, ""Who will keep guard for us tonight?"" I said, ""I will."" He said, ""You will fall asleep."" He repeated, ""Who will keep guard for us tonight?"" I said, ""I will."" He said, ""You will fall asleep."" He repeated this several times, and each time I said, ""I will, O Messenger of Allah!"" Finally he said, ""Then you shall be the one."" I kept watch over them until, right before dawn, the saying of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—""You will fall asleep""—caught up with me, and I fell asleep. Nothing woke us except the heat of the sun upon our backs. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arose, performed ablution just as he usually did, prayed the two sunnah rakʿahs of Fajr, and then led us in the morning prayer. When he finished, he ﷺ said: > **""Had Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, willed that you not sleep past it, you would not have slept; but He willed that this be a precedent for those who come after you. Thus shall it be done by whoever sleeps past a prayer or forgets it.""** ʿAbdullāh continued: Then the Prophet's ﷺ camel and the people's camels wandered off. The people went searching and retrieved their mounts, except for the camel of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ told me, ""Go in this direction."" I went where he indicated and found its halter tangled around a tree such that only a human hand could have tied it. I brought it to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, by the One Who sent you with the truth, I found its halter tangled around a tree such that only a hand could untie it!"" And Sūrat al-Fatḥ was revealed to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"1793","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: We returned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from al-Ḥudaybiyah, and they camped upon soft sandy ground. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Who will keep guard for us?""** Bilāl رضي الله عنه replied, ""I will."" [And the narrator mentioned the rest of the hadith.]"
"1794","Dhū Mikhbar رضي الله عنه—an Abyssinian man who served the Prophet ﷺ—narrated: We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and he was marching swiftly on the return because provisions were scarce. Someone said, ""O Prophet of Allah, the people have fallen behind you."" So he halted, and the people halted with him until all rejoined. He said to them, ""Would you like to take a brief rest?"" So he dismounted and they dismounted. Then he asked: ""Who will keep watch for us tonight?"" I said, ""I will, may Allah make me your ransom!"" He handed me the rein of his camel and said, ""Take it, and do not be helpless and negligent."" I took the rein of the Prophet's ﷺ camel and the rein of my own camel, withdrew a short distance, and let them graze. While watching them, sleep overcame me, and I felt nothing until I felt the heat of the sun upon my face. I woke up, looked left and right, and saw the two mounts nearby. I grabbed their reins and went to the nearest person, waking him and asking, ""Have you prayed?"" He said, ""No."" The people woke one another until the Prophet ﷺ awoke. He ﷺ asked: ""O Bilāl, is there any water in the vessel?"" Bilāl said, ""Yes, may Allah make me your ransom."" He brought ablution water, and the Prophet ﷺ performed ablution without wetting the soil much. He then ordered Bilāl to call the Adhān. The Prophet ﷺ stood and performed the two rakʿahs before Fajr without haste. Then he ordered him to call the Iqāmah and led the prayer without haste. Someone asked, ""O Prophet of Allah, were we negligent?"" He replied: > **""No; Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, took our souls and then returned them to us, and now we have performed the prayer.""**"
"1795","Dhū Mikhbar رضي الله عنه, the nephew of al-Najāshī, narrated: I was with the Prophet ﷺ on a military expedition. They traveled by night as much as they traveled, and then they halted. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me and said, ""O Dhū Mikhbar!"" I replied, ""At your service and to your pleasure, O Messenger of Allah!"" He took the head of my camel and said, ""Sit here, and do not be helpless and neglectful tonight."" I held the camel's head, but sleep overcame my eyes and I fell asleep. The camel slipped away and went off. I did not wake up except by the heat of the sun. The Prophet ﷺ came to me and said, ""O Dhū Mikhbar!"" I said, ""At your service and to your pleasure, O Messenger of Allah!"" He said, ""By Allah, you were helpless and neglectful tonight just as I told you."" We then moved away from that spot, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer. When he finished the prayer, he supplicated for the camel to return, and a whirlwind came driving it back. On the following day, when the dawn broke, he ordered Bilāl to call the Adhān, then ordered him to call the Iqāmah, and then led us in prayer. When he finished the prayer, he ﷺ said: > **""This is our prayer of yesterday.""** Then he recommenced the prayer of that day."
"1796","Abū Qatādah al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated: While we were traveling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ began to lean from his mount due to drowsiness. I supported him with my hand, and he awoke. We traveled on, and he leaned again, so I supported him and he awoke. He said, ""Is that Abū Qatādah?"" I replied, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said, ""May Allah protect you just as you have protected us tonight!"" Then he said, ""I see that we have burdened you; let us turn aside from the road."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ made his camel kneel, and every man among us used the arm of his saddle or mount as a pillow. None of us awoke until the sun had risen. I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are ruined! We have missed the prayer!"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You are not ruined, and you have not missed the prayer; indeed, missing the prayer only applies to one who is awake, not to one who is asleep. Is there any water?""** I brought him a small leather water container, and he performed ablution. He then handed it back to me with some water remaining in it and said: > **""Preserve this, for there shall be an extraordinary matter concerning it.""** He ordered Bilāl to call the Adhān, then he prayed two rakʿahs. He then moved from that spot, ordered Bilāl to call the Iqāmah, and led the morning prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If the people obey Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, they will be guided aright and act gently with themselves; but if they disobey them, they will bring hardship upon themselves.""** When the people had missed the Prophet ﷺ during the night, Abū Bakr and ʿUmar had told them: ""Remain by the water until morning,"" but they refused. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ reached them late in the day when they were on the verge of dying of thirst. They cried, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are perishing!"" He called for that water container and a small bowl, tucked the container under his arm, and began pouring into the vessel while the people drank until all had drunk their fill. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called out: > **""Is there anyone else seeking water?""** He then returned the water container, and it contained as much water as when he had started. We asked Abū Qatādah, ""How many were you?"" He said, ""We were eighty men with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, and twelve men were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."""
"1797","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey and halted to rest during the night; none awoke until the sun had risen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Bilāl رضي الله عنه to call the Adhān, and then he prayed two rakʿahs. Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""The entire world and everything in it would not please me more than this concession.""**"
"1798","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported—and the narrator deemed it to be raised to the Prophet ﷺ—that he said: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, and on the next day at its proper time.""**"
"1799","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, and on the next day at its proper time.""**"
"1800","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us, whenever one of us slept through a prayer or forgot it until its appointed time had passed, to pray it along with the following obligatory prayer.""**"
"1801","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it, and on the following day at its appointed time.""**"
"1802","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated regarding a person who forgets a prayer, that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He should pray it whenever he remembers it.""**"
"1803","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey during which they slept until the sun had risen. He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, you were dead and Allah returned your souls to you. So whoever sleeps past a prayer, let him pray it when he awakes; and whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers.""**"
"1804","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer or sleeps past it, let him perform it whenever he remembers it.""**"
"1805","Bilāl رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""They slept alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey until the sun had risen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered Bilāl to call the Adhān, then he prayed two rakʿahs [of Sunnah]; then Bilāl called the Iqāmah, and the Prophet ﷺ led them in the prayer after the sun had risen.""**"
"1806","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: I was with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey, and he asked, ""Who will keep guard for us tonight?"" I replied, ""I will."" The Prophet ﷺ slept, the people slept, and I also fell asleep; none of us awoke except by the heat of the sun. He ﷺ said: > **""O people! Indeed, these souls are a loaned trust in the bodies of the servants; He withholds them and releases them whenever He wills. So attend to your needs at your ease.""** We attended to our needs unhurriedly and performed ablution. The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution, prayed the two sunnah rakʿahs of Fajr, and then led us in the prayer."
"1807","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer, its appointed time is when he remembers it.""**"
"1808","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه narrated: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one night, and he halted for rest at the end of the night. We woke up after the sun had already risen. He ﷺ said, ""Depart! Depart!"" We traveled until the sun was high in the sky, then he dismounted. He ordered Bilāl to call the Adhān, and each man among us prayed two rakʿahs [of Sunnah]. Then he led us in the prayer. We asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, should we repeat it tomorrow at its proper time?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Allah has forbidden us from usury, so would He accept it from us?""**"
"1809","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it whenever he remembers it.""**"
"1810","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about a man who was heedless of a prayer until the sun set or rose: ""What is its expiation?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: > **""He must perform ablution and do so thoroughly, then perform the prayer and do so with excellence, and seek the forgiveness of Allah; and there is no expiation for it other than that. Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: *{...and establish prayer for My remembrance.}* [Ṭā-Hā: 14]""**"
"1811","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was occupied from Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr on the Day of the Confederates (al-Aḥzāb), and he remembered them after Maghrib. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""They distracted us from the prayer until the daylight was gone; may Allah fill their graves with fire!""** Then he ﷺ prayed both of them after Maghrib."
"1812","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out for the expedition of Tabūk, he marched with them through the night until the pre-dawn hour. He halted with them at dawn and said, ""O Bilāl, watch the prayer for us!"" Bilāl replied, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" But sleep overcame Bilāl and he fell asleep, and they all slept until the scorching sun caused them distress. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ arose and performed Tayammum (dry ablution). He then said to Bilāl: > **""Call the Adhān and the Iqāmah.""** Bilāl asked, ""Now?"" He ﷺ replied, ""Yes."" So they prayed after the morning had fully brightened."
"1813","Jundab رضي الله عنه narrated: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey. A group of people came to him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we were heedless of the prayer and did not pray until the sun rose."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Perform ablution and pray. Indeed, this is not mere forgetfulness; this is from Satan. When one of you retires to his bed, let him say: 'In the Name of Allah, I seek refuge with Allah from Satan the outcast' (Bismi Allāh, aʿūdhu billāhi mina al-shayṭāni al-rajīm).""**"
"1814","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not wake up until the heat of the sun between his shoulders caused him discomfort. When he awoke, the people paused; then the Iqāmah was called, and he stepped forward and led them in prayer. When he finished, he ﷺ said: > **""When one of you falls asleep and his eyes overcome him, let him do like this; for indeed, Allah, the Exalted, takes the souls at the time of their death, and those that do not die during their sleep.""**"
"1815","Maymūnah bint Saʿd رضي الله عنها narrated that she asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, give us a verdict regarding a man who forgets a prayer until the sun rises or sets: what is its expiation?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied: > **""When he remembers it, let him perform it and perfect his prayer; and let him perform ablution and perfect his ablution; for that is its expiation.""**"
"1816","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""There were two camel drivers with us on the night the Messenger of Allah ﷺ slept past the Fajr prayer until the sun had risen.""**"
"1817","Abū Jumuʿah Ḥabīb ibn Sibāʿ رضي الله عنه—who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Maghrib having forgotten to pray ʿAṣr. He asked his Companions, ""Did you see me pray ʿAṣr?"" They replied, ""No, O Messenger of Allah."" > **""So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the muezzin, who called the Adhān and then the Iqāmah; he ﷺ then prayed ʿAṣr, invalidated the first prayer [as an obligation], and then prayed Maghrib.""**"
"1818","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever forgets a prayer and remembers it while praying behind the imam, let him complete his prayer, then make up the prayer he forgot, and then repeat the prayer he performed with the imam.""**"
"1819","ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, there will be rulers after me who will sometimes pray the prayer on time and sometimes delay it beyond its time. So pray with them: if they pray it on time and you pray it with them, it is a reward for you and for them; but if they delay it beyond its time and you pray it with them, you will have the reward and they will bear the burden. Whoever separates from the main body (al-Jamāʿah) dies a death of Jāhiliyyah (pre-Islamic ignorance); and whoever dies having broken a covenant will arrive on the Day of Resurrection with no defense for himself.""**"
"1820","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Masʿūd reported from his father, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, that al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah once delayed the prayer. ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd stood up, called the iqāmah, and led the people in prayer. Al-Walīd sent a message to him asking: ""What prompted you to do what you did? Did an order reach you from the Commander of the Faithful (Amīr al-Muʾminīn)? If so, how excellent is what you did! Or have you introduced an innovation?"" ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه replied: > **""No order came to me from the Commander of the Faithful, nor have I introduced an innovation; rather, Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, and His Messenger ﷺ have forbidden us from waiting for you for our prayer while you attend to your personal affairs.""**"
"1821","Shaddād ibn Aws رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There will come after me leaders who will delay the prayer past its appointed times. Therefore, perform the prayer at its proper time, and treat your prayer with them as a voluntary prayer (subḥah).""**"
"1822","The stepson of ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit (Abū Ubayy) رضي الله عنه reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he said: ""There will soon come rulers whom various matters will distract until they do not perform the prayer at its appointed time."" We asked: ""What do you instruct us to do, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: > **""Perform the prayer at its proper time; and if you happen to catch the prayer with them, then make your prayer with them a voluntary one.""**"
"1823","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه narrated: I asked the Prophet ﷺ concerning the words of Allah, the Mighty and Sublime: *{Those who are heedless of their prayer}* [al-Māʿūn: 5]. He ﷺ said: > **""They are those who delay the prayer past its prescribed time.""**"
"1824","Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd reported: I asked my father, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه: ""O my father! What do you say regarding the verse: *{Those who are heedless of their prayer}*? Which of us does not suffer absentmindedness? Which of us does not have wandering thoughts in his mind?"" Saʿd رضي الله عنه replied: > **""It is not that; it refers only to wasting the time—entertaining oneself until the prescribed time is lost.""**"
"1825","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he said: ""There will come rulers after me who will delay the prayer past its appointed time."" I asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, what should one who lives to see them do?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Perform the prayer at its appointed time; then, whenever you are present with them at the time of their prayer, pray along with them.""**"
"1826","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, there will come after me corrupt rulers who will perform the prayer outside its prescribed time. When they do that, pray the prayer at its appointed time, and make your prayer with them a voluntary one.""**"
"1827","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If the people knew what [immense reward and virtue] lies in calling the adhān, they would surely contend with one another over it with swords.""**"
"1828","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The caller to prayer (muʾadhdhin) is forgiven as far as his call reaches, and every moist and dry thing that hears his voice seeks forgiveness for him.""**"
"1829","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The caller to prayer is forgiven to the full extent of his voice, and he receives a reward equivalent to the reward of everyone who prays along with him.""**"
"1830","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Hand of the Most Merciful is over the head of the caller to prayer, and he is surely forgiven to the furthest reach of his voice, wherever it extends.""**"
"1831","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The callers to prayer will have the longest necks among all people on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1832","Bilāl رضي الله عنه said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the people engage in trade and business for their livelihoods, while we are unable to do that."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Are you not pleased that the callers to prayer will have the longest necks among all people on the Day of Resurrection?""**"
"1833","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""What an excellent man Bilāl is! None follows him except a true believer; he is the master of the callers to prayer, and the callers to prayer will have the longest necks among all people on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1834","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The callers to prayer will have the longest necks among all people on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1835","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The callers to prayer will have the longest necks among all people on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1836","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه said: > **""I regretted that I had not requested the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to appoint al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn as callers to prayer.""**"
"1837","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said: ""I wished that the Prophet ﷺ had conferred the calling of the prayer upon us."" It was asked: ""Why is that?"" He رضي الله عنه replied: > **""Because they will have the longest necks among the people of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1838","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were I to swear an oath, I would surely be truthful: the most beloved of Allah's servants to Allah are the custodians of the sun and the moon—meaning the callers to prayer—and indeed, they will be recognized on the Day of Resurrection by the length of their necks.""**"
"1839","Al-Aʿmash reported from Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The callers to prayer will have the longest necks among all people on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1840","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the best of Allah's servants are those who watch the sun, the moon, and the stars for the remembrance of Allah.""**"
"1841","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Verily, the callers to prayer and those reciting the Talbiyah will emerge from their graves with the caller to prayer proclaiming the adhān and the pilgrim chanting the Talbiyah.""**"
"1842","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""Teach me or direct me to a deed that will admit me into Paradise."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Become a caller to prayer."" The man said: ""I cannot do that."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Become an imām."" The man said: ""I cannot do that."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Then stand directly opposite the imām [in the prayer row].""**"
"1843","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: An elderly, frail man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, teach me a deed through which I may draw near to Allah, my Lord, the Mighty and Sublime."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""You must engage in jihād in the cause of Allah."" The man said: ""I cannot do that; I have grown too old and weak."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Then become a caller to prayer.""**"
"1844","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The caller to prayer who seeks reward solely from Allah is like the martyr drenched in his own blood; whatever he desires from Allah between the adhān and the iqāmah is granted to him.""**"
"1845","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: ""If I had not heard it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once and again—until he counted seven times—I would never have narrated it. I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""Three kinds of people will be upon dunes of musk on the Day of Resurrection; terror will not overwhelm them, nor will they panic when mankind is gripped with terror: a man who learned the Qurʾān and stood by it, seeking thereby the Countenance of Allah and what is with Him; a man who called the five daily prayers each day and night, seeking thereby the Countenance of Allah and what is with Him; and a slave whose worldly servitude did not prevent him from the obedience of his Lord.""**"
"1846","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three persons will not be overwhelmed by the Great Terror, nor will the Reckoning touch them; they shall abide upon a dune of musk until the reckoning of all creation is concluded: a man who recited the Qurʾān seeking the Countenance of Allah and led a people in prayer while they were well-pleased with him; a caller who summons to the prayers seeking the Countenance of Allah; and a servant who conducted himself excellently in what is between him and his Lord and in what is between him and his masters.""**"
"1847","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whenever the adhān is called in a settlement, Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, grants it safety from His punishment on that day.""**"
"1848","Maʿqil ibn Yasār رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no people among whom the call to prayer is proclaimed in the morning except that they remain in the protection of Allah until evening; and there is no people among whom the call to prayer is proclaimed in the evening except that they remain in the protection of Allah until morning.""**"
"1849","Maʿqil ibn Yasār رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah does not listen as attentively to anything as He listens to the adhān and to a beautiful voice reciting the Qurʾān.""**"
"1850","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The gates of heaven are opened for five things: the recitation of the Qurʾān, the clash of two opposing forces, the descent of rain, the supplication of the oppressed person, and the calling of the adhān.""**"
"1851","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: When Allah, Blessed and Exalted, willed to teach His Messenger the adhān, Jibrīl عليه السلام brought him a riding beast called al-Burāq. As the Prophet went to mount it, it became restive, so Jibrīl said to it: ""Be still! For by Allah, no servant more noble in the sight of Allah than Muḥammad ﷺ has ever mounted you."" He mounted it until he reached the Veil that borders the Most Merciful, Blessed and Exalted. While he was in that state, an angel emerged from behind the Veil. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: ""O Jibrīl, who is this?"" Jibrīl replied: ""By the One Who sent you with the truth! I am the closest of creation in station, yet I have never seen this angel since I was created until this very hour."" The angel proclaimed: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar (Allah is the Greatest, Allah is the Greatest)."" It was proclaimed from behind the Veil: ""My servant has spoken the truth: I am the Greatest, I am the Greatest."" The angel then said: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh (I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah)."" It was proclaimed from behind the Veil: ""My servant has spoken the truth: there is no deity except Me."" The angel said: ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh (I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah)."" It was proclaimed from behind the Veil: ""My servant has spoken the truth: I have sent Muḥammad."" The angel said: ""Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh, ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ, qad qāmati al-ṣalāh (Come to prayer, come to success, the prayer has been established)."" Then he said: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar."" It was proclaimed from behind the Veil: ""My servant has spoken the truth: I am the Greatest, I am the Greatest."" Then he said: ""Lā ilāha illallāh (There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah)."" It was proclaimed from behind the Veil: ""My servant has spoken the truth: there is no deity except Me."" Then the angel took Muḥammad ﷺ by the hand and brought him forward, and he led the inhabitants of the heavens in prayer, among whom were Ādam and Nūḥ عليهما السلام. Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī [al-Bāqir] remarked: > **""On that day, Allah perfected for Muḥammad ﷺ supreme honor and preeminence over the inhabitants of the heavens and the earth.""**"
"1852","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""When the Prophet ﷺ was taken up in the Night Journey to the heavens, Allah revealed the adhān to him; he descended with it, and Jibrīl عليه السلام taught it to him.""**"
"1853","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported: A man from the Anṣār passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was sorrowful—this man used to prepare food and people would gather at his home. He entered his mosque and began to pray, and while doing so, drowsiness overtook him. A visitor came to him in his sleep and said: ""I know what has grieved the Prophet ﷺ,"" and he recounted the entire story of the adhān. The Prophet ﷺ then said: > **""Abū Bakr was informed of the like of what you were informed of; so command Bilāl to call the adhān with that.""**"
"1854","Saʿd al-Qaraẓ رضي الله عنه reported: The very beginning of the adhān was that a man from the Anṣār was shown it in a vision, so he informed the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ ordered Bilāl to call the adhān, and the Anṣārī dictated the words to him: > **""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh, Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh; Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh, Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh; then he repeated: Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh, Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh; Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh, Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh; Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh, Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh; Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ, Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ; Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Lā ilāha illallāh.""**"
"1855","Saʿd al-Qaraẓ رضي الله عنه narrated: Bilāl used to pronounce the phrases of the adhān two by two, and he would double the testimonies (tarjīʿ). Facing the Qiblah, he would say: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh"" twice, and ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh"" twice; then he would return and repeat: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh"" twice, and ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh"" twice while facing the Qiblah. Then he would turn to his right and say: ""Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh"" twice, then turn to his left and say: ""Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ"" twice, then face the Qiblah and say: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Lā ilāha illallāh."" And his iqāmah was singled: > **""Qad qāmati al-ṣalāh (The prayer has been established) was uttered once.""**"
"1856","Bilāl رضي الله عنه reported that he used to call the adhān for the Prophet ﷺ, proclaiming: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh, Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh."" Then he would turn to the right of the Qiblah and say: ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh, Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh."" Then he would turn facing behind the Qiblah and say: ""Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh, Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh."" Then he would turn to his left and say: ""Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ, Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ."" Then he would face the Qiblah and say: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Lā ilāha illallāh."" And he would pronounce the iqāmah for the Prophet ﷺ in single phrases, saying: > **""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh, wa-ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh; Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh, Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ; Qad qāmati al-ṣalāh, Qad qāmati al-ṣalāh; Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Lā ilāha illallāh.""**"
"1857","Bilāl رضي الله عنه reported that he used to call the adhān for the dawn prayer (Ṣubḥ) and say: ""Ḥayya ʿalā khayril-ʿamal (Come to the best of deeds)."" Thereupon, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him: > **""Substitute in its place: 'Al-ṣalātu khayrun minan-nawm' (Prayer is better than sleep), and leave off: 'Ḥayya ʿalā khayril-ʿamal'.""**"
"1858","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: Bilāl came to the Prophet ﷺ to inform him of the dawn prayer, but he said: ""Command Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer."" Bilāl returned to him and perceived heaviness and fatigue in him, so the Prophet repeated: ""Command Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer."" Bilāl went out and called the adhān, adding to his call: ""Al-ṣalātu khayrun minan-nawm (Prayer is better than sleep)."" The Prophet ﷺ asked him: ""What is this phrase you have added to your adhān?"" Bilāl replied: ""I noticed heaviness in you and wished for you to be invigorated."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Go and add it to your adhān, and command Abū Bakr to lead the people in prayer.""**"
"1859","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that Bilāl came to the Prophet ﷺ at the time of the dawn adhān and found him asleep, so he called out: ""Al-ṣalātu khayrun minan-nawm (Prayer is better than sleep)."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not disapprove of it and incorporated it into the adhān: > **""And no prayer is ever called before its prescribed time except the Fajr prayer.""**"
"1860","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: Bilāl came to the Prophet ﷺ to notify him of the dawn prayer and found him sleeping, so he called out: ""Al-ṣalātu khayrun minan-nawm (Prayer is better than sleep)."" > **""Consequently, it was confirmed and retained as part of the dawn adhān.""**"
"1861","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reported: > **""Bilāl called the adhān for the Prophet ﷺ in phrases repeated two by two, and he performed the iqāmah in like manner.""**"
"1862","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported: I was with the Prophet ﷺ in an army, and I went out to pasture the riding mounts of my companions. When I returned in the evening, my companions met me, hastening toward one another and saying: > **""While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the caller to prayer called the adhān and said: 'Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh wa-ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh'..."" and he mentioned the rest of the hadith.**"
"1863","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ رضي الله عنه said: > **""During the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the adhān was pronounced in pairs of phrases, and the iqāmah was pronounced in single phrases.""**"
"1864","Suwayd ibn Ghafalah reported: > **""The final phrase of Bilāl's adhān was: 'Lā ilāha illallāh, wallāhu akbar' (There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and Allah is the Greatest).""**"
"1865","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The initial adhān was ordained solely to make it convenient for the worshippers to prepare for their prayer. Therefore, when you hear the adhān, perform wudūʾ thoroughly; and when you hear the iqāmah, hasten to attain the opening takbīr, for it is the pinnacle of the prayer and its perfection; and do not precede the reciter (the imām) in bowing or prostration.""**"
"1866","Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you hear the caller announcing the prayer, say just as he says.""**"
"1867","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه reported concerning the Prophet ﷺ: Whenever he heard the caller to prayer, he would repeat what he said, until the caller reached *Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh, ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ*, whereupon he ﷺ said: > **""Lā ḥawla wa-lā quwwata illā billāh (There is no might nor power except through Allah).""**"
"1868","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When you hear the caller to prayer, say just as he says.""**"
"1869","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith reported from his father that whenever the Prophet ﷺ heard the caller to prayer, he would say as he said. When the caller said: *Ḥayya ʿalā al-ṣalāh, ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ*, the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Lā ḥawla wa-lā quwwata illā billāh (There is no might nor power except through Allah).""**"
"1870","Hilāl ibn Yasāf reported that he heard Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه relate that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""Whoever hears the caller to prayer and repeats what he says will have a reward equal to his.""**"
"1871","Maymūnah رضي الله عنها narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood between the row of men and the row of women and said: > **""O assembly of women! When you hear the adhān of this Abyssinian and his iqāmah, say just as he says; for indeed, you will have for every single letter a million (one thousand thousand) degrees of reward.""** ʿUmar رضي الله عنه asked: ""This is for the women—what then is for the men?"" The Prophet ﷺ replied: > **""Double that, O ʿUmar!""**"
"1872","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted for rest one night, and Bilāl called the adhān. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever says the like of what he says and testifies with the like of his testimony shall enter Paradise.""**"
"1873","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""Four acts are of harsh negligence: that a person hears the caller to prayer say: 'Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh, Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh' and does not say as he says; that a person wipes his face before concluding his prayer; that a person urinates standing up; and that a person performs prayer while there is nothing between him and the Qiblah to serve as a barrier (sutrah).""**"
"1874","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā reported: Whenever ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه heard the caller calling the adhān, he would repeat what he said. When the caller said: *Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh, wa-ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh*, ʿAlī رضي الله عنه would say: > **""I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, and that those who have denied Muḥammad are indeed the liars.""**"
"1875","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever says when the caller calls: 'O Allah, Lord of this established call and beneficial prayer, send blessings upon Muḥammad, and be pleased with me with a pleasure after which there is never any wrath'—Allah will surely answer his supplication.""**"
"1876","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Al-Wasīlah is a station in the presence of Allah above which there is no rank; therefore, ask Allah to grant me al-Wasīlah.""** *(And in al-Ṭabarānī's wording: ""So ask Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, to grant me al-Wasīlah over all His creation."")*"
"1877","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Invoke blessings upon me, for indeed it is purification for you, and ask for me al-Wasīlah in Paradise.""** We asked him—or he informed us—saying: > **""It is a rank at the highest summit of Paradise, reserved for a single man, and I hope that I am that man.""**"
"1878","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard the caller to prayer, he would say: ""O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and established prayer, send blessings upon Muḥammad and grant him his request on the Day of Resurrection."" He would make those around him hear it, and he loved that they should say the like of that when they heard the caller. He ﷺ said: > **""Whoever says the like of that when he hears the caller to prayer, the intercession of Muḥammad ﷺ becomes binding for him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1879","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard the call to prayer, he would say: ""O Allah, Lord of this perfect call and established prayer, send blessings upon Your servant and Messenger, and place us within his intercession on the Day of Resurrection."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever says this upon hearing the call, Allah will place him within my intercession on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1880","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Ask Allah to grant me al-Wasīlah; for no servant asks it for me in this worldly life except that I shall be a witness or an intercessor for him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1881","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever hears the call to prayer and says: 'I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone, without partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger; O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad, elevate him to the station of al-Wasīlah with You, and place us within his intercession on the Day of Resurrection'—intercession becomes incumbent for him.""**"
"1882","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""No Muslim says upon hearing the call to prayer—magnifying Allah when the caller magnifies, testifying that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah, and testifying that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah—and then says: 'O Allah, grant Muḥammad al-Wasīlah and supreme excellence, place his degree among the highest of the high (al-ʿIlliyyūn), place his love among the chosen ones, and place his remembrance among those brought near'—except that intercession becomes incumbent for him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"1883","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that whenever he heard the caller calling the adhān, he would say: > **""I bear witness to it alongside every witness who testifies, and I take it upon myself against every denier who rejects it.""**"
"1884","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the caller to prayer proclaims the adhān, the gates of heaven are opened.""**"
"1885","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Behold! Indeed, supplication is never rejected between the adhān and the iqāmah; so make supplication!""**"
"1886","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: > **""When the call to prayer is proclaimed, Satan turns his back and flees as far as al-Rawḥāʾ so that he does not hear the sound of the adhān; the gates of heaven are opened wide, and supplication is answered.""**"
"1887","Bilāl رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you call the adhān, place your two fingers in your ears, for indeed that makes your voice louder and carry further.""**"
"1888","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not have the adhān called for any prayer during travel, sufficing only with the iqāmah, except for the dawn prayer (Ṣubḥ); for indeed, he would have both the adhān and the iqāmah called for it.""**"
"1889","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAdī رضي الله عنه reported: > **""The Prophet ﷺ would not have the adhān called during travel except for the dawn prayer, calling only the iqāmah for other prayers.""**"
"1890","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on one of his journeys, he heard a caller proclaiming: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar."" The Prophet of Allah ﷺ remarked: ""He is upon the primordial nature (al-Fiṭrah)."" The caller then said: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh."" The Prophet of Allah ﷺ remarked: > **""He has exited from the Fire!""** We hurried toward him, and behold, he was a shepherd tending livestock whom the prayer time had reached, so he proclaimed the call for it."
"1891","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on one of his journeys, he heard a caller proclaiming: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar."" He ﷺ said: ""He is upon the primordial nature."" The caller said: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh."" He ﷺ said: ""He has testified with the testimony of truth."" The caller said: ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He has exited from the Fire! Look, and you will find him to be either a solitary goat shepherd or a hunter with hunting dogs.""** They went and looked, and they found him to be a shepherd who was reached by the prayer time, so he proclaimed the call for it."
"1892","ʿAbdullāh ibn Rabīʿah al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه narrated: The Prophet ﷺ was on a journey when he heard a caller saying: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah."" The caller said: ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah."" The Prophet ﷺ then said: ""You will find him to be a shepherd of sheep or a man away from his family."" They descended into the valley and came upon a dead sheep. The Prophet ﷺ asked: ""Do you see this dead sheep as insignificant to its owners?"" They replied: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: > **""The worldly life is more insignificant in the sight of Allah than this dead sheep is to its owners.""**"
"1893","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey when he heard a caller saying: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""He has cast off all rivals [unto Allah]."" The caller said: ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""He has exited from the Fire."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""You will find him to be an isolated goat shepherd far from his kin, or a keeper of hunting hounds.""**"
"1894","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a march when he heard someone proclaiming: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""The call of truth."" The caller said: ""Ashhadu allā ilāha illallāh."" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""The word of pure sincerity (al-Ikhlāṣ)."" The caller said: ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Its speaker has exited from the Fire! You will find this person to be a goat shepherd or a hunter pursuing game with dogs.""**"
"1895","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey when he heard a man saying, ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar; Ashhadu an lā ilāha illā Allāh, Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh."" Thereupon, the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He has exited from polytheism.""**"
"1896","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Prophet ﷺ went out one day accompanied by Abū Bakr, ʿUmar, Zayd ibn Thābit, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهم, while the Prophet ﷺ was riding his she-camel, al-Jadʿāʾ. When he emerged into the open ground, the Prophet ﷺ heard a man proclaiming, ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar."" The Prophet ﷺ paused to listen. When the man repeated, ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar,"" the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, he has borne the testimony of truth.""** When the man said, ""Ashhadu an lā ilāha illā Allāh"" (I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah), the Prophet ﷺ said three times: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I consider that this man has been delivered from the Fire.""** Then the Prophet ﷺ remarked: > **""This man is a huntsman with hounds.""** So Ibn Masʿūd and Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما went to see, and they found him to be exactly as he described."
"1897","Ṣafwān ibn ʿAssāl رضي الله عنه narrated: While we were in the company of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he heard a man calling the Adhān. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""[He is] upon the primordial disposition (al-Fiṭrah).""** When the man said, ""Ashhadu an lā ilāha illā Allāh"" (I bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah), the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""He has testified to the truth.""** When he said, ""Ashhadu anna Muḥammadan Rasūlullāh"" (I bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah), he ﷺ said: > **""He has been delivered from the Fire.""**"
"1898","Saʿd al-Qaraẓ رضي الله عنه narrated that whenever the Prophet ﷺ came to Qubāʾ at any hour, Bilāl would call the Adhān so that the people would know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had arrived and gather around him. One day, the Prophet ﷺ arrived without Bilāl accompanying him. Some Abyssinians began looking toward one another. Thereupon, Saʿd climbed up a date-palm trunk and called the Adhān. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him: > **""What prompted you to call the Adhān, O Saʿd?""** He replied, ""May my father and mother be ransomed for you! I saw you with only a few people, and I did not see Bilāl with you. I noticed those Abyssinians looking at one another and gazing toward you, so I feared for you on their account and called the Adhān."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""You did rightly, O Saʿd. Whenever you do not see Bilāl with me, call the Adhān.""** Thus, Saʿd called the Adhān three times during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"1899","Abū Maḥdhūrah رضي الله عنه said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ assigned the Adhān to us and to our freed slaves, the provision of water (al-Siqāyah) to Banū Hāshim, and the service of [custodianship] to Banū ʿAbd al-Dār.""**"
"1900","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbdān al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The Caliphate belongs to Quraysh, governance and judicial authority to the Anṣār, and the call to prayer (al-Daʿwah) to the Abyssinians.""**"
"1901","Abū Usayd رضي الله عنه narrated: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Makkah, Abū Maḥdhūrah came to him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, grant me permission to call the Adhān."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Call the Adhān.""** Bilāl used to call the Adhān while the Prophet ﷺ was in Makkah, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ departed, Abū Maḥdhūrah remained behind [as the muezzin of Makkah]."
"1902","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Imām is a guarantor, and the muezzin is a trustee.""**"
"1903","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Imām is a guarantor and the muezzin is a trustee. O Allah, guide the Imāms and forgive the muezzins.""** They said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you have made us compete with one another for calling the Adhān after you."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, there will come after me—or after you—a people whose muezzins will be the lowest among them.""**"
"1904","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Imām is a guarantor and the muezzin is a trustee. O Allah, forgive the muezzins and guide the Imāms.""**"
"1905","Abū Maḥdhūrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The muezzins are the trustees of the Muslims regarding their breaking of the fast and their pre-dawn meals (Suḥūr).""**"
"1906","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""I do not like that your muezzins should be your blind ones.""** The narrator added: And I believe he also said: > **""Nor should they be your reciters.""**"
"1907","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Bilāl calls the Adhān during the night, so eat and drink until Ibn Umm Maktūm calls the Adhān.""**"
"1908","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah رضي الله عنه narrated: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to appoint me as the Imām of my people, so he ﷺ said: > **""Lead the prayer according to the prayer of the weakest among the people, and do not appoint a muezzin who takes a wage for his Adhān.""**"
"1909","Yaḥyā al-Bakkāʾ narrated that a man said to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, ""Indeed, I love you for the sake of Allah."" Ibn ʿUmar replied: > **""But I hate you for the sake of Allah!""** The man asked, ""And why is that?"" He said: > **""Because you sing melodically in your Adhān and you accept payment for it.""**"
"1910","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The muezzin who calls the Adhān seeking divine reward is like a martyr drenched in his blood until he finishes his Adhān; everything moist and dry bears witness for him, and when he dies, his body does not decompose in his grave.""**"
"1911","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The muezzin who calls the Adhān seeking divine reward is like the martyr drenched in his blood; when he dies, he does not decompose in his grave.""**"
"1912","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: We were with the Prophet ﷺ, and he sought Bilāl to call the Adhān for them, but he was not found. So the Prophet ﷺ instructed another man, who then called the Adhān. Bilāl arrived afterward and intended to pronounce the Iqāmah, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Only the one who called the Adhān should pronounce the Iqāmah.""**"
"1913","Ibrāhīm [al-Nakhaʿī] narrated that Ibn Masʿūd, ʿAlqamah, and al-Aswad prayed without calling the Adhān or Iqāmah. Sufyān said: ""The Iqāmah of the city sufficed them."" And in another narration, Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه stated: > **""The Iqāmah of the city is sufficient.""**"
"1914","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The polytheists occupied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ away from the prayers of Ẓuhr, ʿAṣr, Maghrib, and ʿIshāʾ until a portion of the night had elapsed. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed Bilāl, who called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed Ẓuhr; then he commanded him, and he called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed ʿAṣr; then he commanded him, and he called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed Maghrib; then he commanded him, and he called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed ʿIshāʾ.""**"
"1915","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: On the Day of the Trench, the Prophet ﷺ was kept occupied from performing the Ẓuhr, ʿAṣr, Maghrib, and ʿIshāʾ prayers. So he instructed Bilāl, who called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed Ẓuhr; then he commanded him, and he called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed ʿAṣr; then he commanded him, and he called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed Maghrib; then he commanded him, and he called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and he prayed ʿIshāʾ. He ﷺ then said: > **""There is no group of people upon the face of the earth remembering Allah besides you.""**"
"1916","Al-Jaʿd Abū ʿUthmān narrated: Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه passed by us in the mosque of Banū Thaʿlabah and asked: > **""Have you already prayed?""** We replied, ""Yes,"" and that was for the Fajr prayer. Thereupon, he ordered a man who called the Adhān and Iqāmah, and then he led his companions in prayer."
"1917","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Bilāl, allow an interval between your Adhān and your Iqāmah so that the one eating may finish his meal without haste, and the one performing ablution may relieve his need without hurry.""**"
"1918","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When the call to establish prayer (Iqāmah) is pronounced, the gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered.""**"
"1919","Qatādah narrated that whenever someone came to notify ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه of the prayer, he would say: > **""Welcome to those who speak with justice, and to the prayer, welcome and greetings!""**"
"1920","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""Whenever Bilāl said, 'Qad qāmatiṣ-ṣalāh' (The prayer has been established), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would rise and pronounce the opening Takbīr.""**"
"1921","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated: Bilāl called the Adhān before dawn, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to return and proclaim: > **""Behold, the servant had fallen asleep!""** So Bilāl climbed back up saying, ""Would that Bilāl’s mother had been bereft of him, and that he were drenched from the spurting blood of his forehead!"""
"1922","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that a man walked out of the mosque after the muezzin had called the Adhān. He commented, ""As for this man, he has disobeyed Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ."" Then he added: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us: > **""When you are in the mosque and the call to prayer is proclaimed, none of you should depart until he has prayed.""**"
"1923","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No one hears the call to prayer in this mosque of mine and then exits it—unless out of necessity—and then does not return to it, except a hypocrite.""**"
"1924","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the prayer is established, there is no prayer except the one for which the Iqāmah was called.""**"
"1925","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Iqāmah for the dawn prayer was called, and a man stood up to pray the two [Sunnah] rakʿahs. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ pulled him by his garment and said: > **""Are you praying the dawn prayer as four rakʿahs?""**"
"1926","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to Gabriel عليه السلام: > **""Which places on earth are the best?""** Gabriel replied, ""I do not know."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Then ask your Lord, the Exalted and Majestic, concerning that.""** Gabriel wept and said, ""O Muḥammad, do we have the right to question Him? It is He Who informs us of what He wills."" Gabriel then ascended to heaven and returned, saying: ""The best places are the Houses of Allah on earth."" The Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""Then which places are the worst?""** Gabriel ascended to heaven again and returned, saying: ""The worst places are the marketplaces."""
"1927","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that a man asked the Prophet ﷺ, ""Which places are best, and which places are worst?"" He ﷺ said: > **""The best of places are the mosques, and the worst of places are the marketplaces.""**"
"1928","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The worst gatherings are the marketplaces and the roads, and the best gatherings are the mosques. If you do not sit in the mosque, then keep to your home.""**"
"1929","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated that a man asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, which lands are most beloved to Allah, and which lands are most hateful to Allah?"" He ﷺ said: > **""I do not know until I ask Gabriel.""** Then Gabriel came to him and informed him that the places most beloved to Allah are the mosques, and the places most hateful to Allah are the marketplaces."
"1930","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""All lands will perish on the Day of Resurrection except the mosques, for indeed they will be joined one to another.""**"
"1931","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no morning or evening except that the parcels of the earth call out to one another, 'O neighbor, did a righteous servant pass over you today who prayed upon you or remembered Allah?' If she says, 'Yes,' she regards herself as having gained excellence over the other.""**"
"1932","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would pray in the place where al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn had urinated, and he ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, when a servant prostrates a prostration to Allah, Allah purifies the place of his prostration down to seven earths.""**"
"1933","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray wherever was convenient in the house. She said to him, ""O Messenger of Allah, you might pray in a place where a menstruating woman walks; what if you were to designate a specific prayer area to pray in?"" He ﷺ said: > **""How wondrous of you, O ʿĀʾishah! Do you not know that the believer’s prostration purifies its place down to seven earths?""**"
"1934","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""The mosques are the Houses of Allah on earth; they shine for the inhabitants of heaven just as the stars of the sky illuminate for the dwellers of the earth.""**"
"1935","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise that is far more spacious than it.""**"
"1936","Bishr ibn Ḥayyān narrated: Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه came to us while we were building our mosque. He paused beside us, offered greetings of peace, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: > **""Whoever builds a mosque and prays in it, Allah, the Exalted and Majestic, builds for him in Paradise something far superior to it.""**"
"1937","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, even if it be like the nest of a sandgrouse for its eggs, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1938","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah the size of a sandgrouse’s nest, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1939","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""** And in the narration of al-Ṭabarānī: > **""Even if it were like the nest of a sandgrouse.""**"
"1940","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a house in which Allah is worshipped from lawful wealth, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise made of pearls and rubies.""**"
"1941","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""** I asked, ""And what about these small prayer stations along the road to Makkah?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Even those.""**"
"1942","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, seeking neither ostentation nor good reputation thereby, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1943","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1944","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1945","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque seen [and approved] by Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise, and if he dies on that day, He forgives him; and whoever digs a grave seen [and approved] by Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise, and if he dies on that day, He forgives him.""**"
"1946","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him [a house] in Paradise that is vaster than it.""**"
"1947","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""** And in the narration of Imām Aḥmad: > **""For indeed, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise that is vaster than it.""**"
"1948","Nubayṭ ibn Sharīṭ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"1949","Abū Qirṣāfah رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""Build mosques and remove litter from them, for whoever builds a mosque for Allah, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise.""** A man asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, even these small mosques that are built along the road?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""Yes, and removing litter from them is the dowry for the wide-eyed maidens of Paradise (al-Ḥūr al-ʿĪn).""**"
"1950","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that they were carrying sun-dried bricks for the construction of the mosque, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with them. He said: I approached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was bracing a brick against his stomach. Thinking it was burdensome for him, I said, ""Hand it to me, O Messenger of Allah!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Take another one, O Abū Hurayrah, for there is truly no life except the life of the Hereafter.""**"
"1951","Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated: I was participating in the construction of the mosque with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would say: > **""Bring the Yamāmī near the clay, for he has the best touch for it and has the strongest shoulders among you.""**"
"1952","Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated: I arrived before the Prophet ﷺ while his Companions were constructing the mosque. It seemed that their work was not pleasing him fully. So I took up a shovel and began mixing the clay with it. My handling of the shovel and my workmanship pleased him, and he ﷺ said: > **""Leave the Ḥanafī with the clay, for indeed he has the best mastery over the clay among you.""**"
"1953","Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated: I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was laying the foundations of the Mosque of Madinah, and I began carrying stones as others were carrying them. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, O people of al-Yamāmah, you are the most skillful in blending clay mixtures, so mix the clay for us.""** Thus, I would mix the clay for them, and they would carry it."
"1954","Sayyār ibn al-Maʿrūr narrated: I heard ʿUmar [ibn al-Khaṭṭāb] رضي الله عنه delivering a sermon, wherein he said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ built this mosque, and we were with him—the Emigrants and the Helpers. So when the crowding becomes intense, let any of you prostrate upon the back of his brother.""** And upon seeing people praying in the path, he said: > **""Pray inside the mosque!""**"
"1955","Al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Masʿūd said: > **""The first to acquire the recitation of the Qurʾān directly from the mouth of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd; the first to build a mosque for Allah wherein prayer is offered was ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir; and the first to proclaim the Adhān was Bilāl.""**"
"1956","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه narrated that when his wife passed away, he began urging the people: > **""Carry her bier and vie eagerly to carry it! For she and her maids used to carry stones by night for the mosque that was founded upon piety (al-Taqwā), while we used to carry two stones at a time during the day.""**"
"1957","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that a woman used to clean up specks of dirt and litter from the mosque. When she passed away, the Prophet ﷺ was not notified of her burial. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whenever someone among you dies, inform me.""** He then led the funeral prayer over her and said: > **""Indeed, I saw her in Paradise on account of her gathering specks of dirt from the mosque.""**"
"1958","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: A bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and pledged allegiance to him in the mosque, then turned away. He then stood, spread his legs, and began to urinate. The people rushed toward him to reprimand him, but the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Do not interrupt the man's urination.""** Then he called him over and asked: > **""Are you not a Muslim?""** He replied, ""Yes, indeed."" He ﷺ asked: > **""Then what prompted you to urinate in the mosque?""** The man said, ""By the One Who sent you with the truth, I merely thought it was ordinary open ground like any other, so I urinated in it."" The Prophet ﷺ then ordered a large bucket of water, and it was poured over his urine."
"1959","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""A bedouin came and urinated in the mosque, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded that his place be dug up and a bucket of water poured over it.""**"
"1960","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""ʿUmar [ibn al-Khaṭṭāb] رضي الله عنه used to perfume the Mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with incense every Friday.""**"
"1961","ʿUmar [ibn al-Khaṭṭāb] رضي الله عنه said: > **""Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'It is appropriate that we expand our mosque,' I would never have expanded it.""**"
"1962","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a group of the Anṣār who were building a mosque, and the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Make your mosque spacious, and you will fill it.""**"
"1963","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated from one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ who said: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to establish mosques in our neighborhoods, to construct them well, and to keep them purified.""**"
"1964","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, my father left behind a debt owed to a Jewish man."" He ﷺ said: > **""I will come to you on Saturday, if Allah wills.""** This was during the fruit harvest when fresh dates were being picked. On Saturday morning, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived. When he entered upon me in my date grove, he approached the small stream and performed ablution from it. He then proceeded to the local prayer place (mosque) and prayed two rakʿahs. Afterward, I brought him to a tent belonging to me and spread a hair carpet for him, and placed a cushion stuffed with date-palm fiber for him to lean upon, and he reclined on it. It was not long before Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه arrived; as if having seen what the Prophet of Allah ﷺ had done, he performed ablution and prayed two rakʿahs. Shortly thereafter, ʿUmar رضي الله عنه arrived, and he also made ablution and prayed two rakʿahs, as though following the lead of his two companions. They both entered; Abū Bakr sat near his head, and ʿUmar sat near his feet."
"1965","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmayr al-Sadūsī narrated that he brought a small water-skin from the Prophet ﷺ, wherein the Prophet ﷺ had washed his face, rinsed his mouth, spat into the water, washed his hands, and then filled the skin, saying: > **""Whenever you stop by any water source, replenish the water-skin upon whatever remains in it. Then when you reach your land, sprinkle it over that designated plot and adopt it as a mosque.""** They consequently adopted it as a mosque. ʿUmar [ibn Shaqīq] said, ""And I indeed have prayed in it."""
"1966","Zayd ibn ʿĪsā al-Khuzāʿī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you build the Mosque of Ṣanʿāʾ, construct it to the right of the mountain called Ṣayr [Ḍayn].""**"
"1967","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray while in Makkah toward Bayt al-Maqdis (Jerusalem) with the Kaʿbah placed in front of him. After migrating to Madinah, he prayed toward it for sixteen months, after which he was directed toward the Kaʿbah.""**"
"1968","Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Muʿallā رضي الله عنه narrated: During the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we used to head out early in the morning and pass by the mosque to pray therein. One day we passed by while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was seated upon the pulpit, and he said: > **""Indeed, a momentous matter has taken place today.""** I drew closer to the Prophet ﷺ, and he recited this verse: *{We have certainly seen the turning of your face toward the heaven, and We will surely turn you to a qiblah with which you will be pleased...}* [al-Baqarah: 144] until he finished the verse. Beside me was a companion of mine, so I said to him, ""Bow two rakʿahs!"" He replied, ""Not until we observe what he does."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then descended and led the people that day in the Ẓuhr prayer facing toward the Kaʿbah."
"1969","Kathīr ibn ʿAbdillāh [ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf] narrated from his father, from his grandfather [ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf al-Muzanī رضي الله عنه], who said: > **""We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he arrived in Madinah, and he prayed toward Bayt al-Maqdis for seventeen months, after which the direction was redirected toward the Kaʿbah.""**"
"1970","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned toward Bayt al-Maqdis while praying Ẓuhr, and then he turned his face toward the Kaʿbah. Thereupon, the foolish among the people said: > **""'{What has turned them from their Qiblah which they used to face?}' [Al-Baqarah: 142]""**"
"1971","Anas رضي الله عنه reported: The announcer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived while the Imam was in prayer, having already prayed two rakʿahs. The announcer proclaimed: > **""The Qiblah has indeed been redirected toward the Kaʿbah, so perform the remaining two rakʿahs facing the Kaʿbah!""**"
"1972","ʿUmārah ibn Ruʾaybah رضي الله عنه reported: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in one of the two evening prayers when the Qiblah was redirected. > **""The Prophet ﷺ turned, and we turned along with him during two rakʿahs.""**"
"1973","ʿUmārah ibn Aws رضي الله عنه—who had prayed toward both Qiblahs—reported: While we were in one of the two evening prayers, a caller called out at the door: ""The Qiblah has indeed been redirected to the Kaʿbah!"" > **""I bear witness that our Imam turned toward the Kaʿbah, and the men, women, and children turned as well, such that some of us were praying here and some of us there.""**"
"1974","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was redirected from the direction of al-Shām toward the Kaʿbah, so he prayed toward the Kaʿbah in Rajab, at the turn of seventeen months after his arrival in Medina.""**"
"1975","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported: The Prophet ﷺ used to pray toward Bayt al-Maqdis. When the Qiblah was redirected, a man went to the people of Qubāʾ and found them praying the morning prayer. He said, ""Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has been commanded to pray toward the Kaʿbah."" > **""Thereupon, their Imam turned around until he faced the Qiblah along with them.""**"
"1976","ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reported: > **""Before coming to Medina, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to call the people in Mecca to faith in Allah and belief in Him as speech without deeds, while the Qiblah was toward Bayt al-Maqdis. When he emigrated to us, the obligatory duties were revealed; Medina abrogated Mecca and what had been commanded in it, and the Sacred House (al-Bayt al-Ḥarām) abrogated Bayt al-Maqdis, so faith became speech and action together.""**"
"1977","Tuwaylah bint Aslam رضي الله عنها—who was among those women who gave the pledge of allegiance—reported: While we were at our place praying among Banū Ḥārithah, ʿAbbād ibn Bishr ibn Qayẓī arrived and said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has indeed turned toward the Sacred House and the Kaʿbah."" > **""So the men shifted to the place of the women, and the women shifted to the place of the men, and they prayed the remaining two rakʿahs facing the Kaʿbah.""**"
"1978","Tuwaylah bint Muslim رضي الله عنها reported: We prayed Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr in the mosque of Banū Ḥārithah, facing the Mosque of Īliyāʾ (Jerusalem), and we had prayed two rakʿahs when someone came to tell us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had faced toward the Sacred House. So the men shifted to the place of the women, and the women shifted to the place of the men, and we prayed the remaining two prostrations (rakʿahs) facing the Sacred House. A man from Banū Ḥārithah then narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Those are men who believed in the unseen.""**"
"1979","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported: While I was with the Prophet ﷺ, a Jewish man asked permission to enter, and permission was granted to him. He said, ""Al-sāmu ʿalayk"" (Death be upon you). The Prophet ﷺ replied, ""Wa ʿalayk"" (And upon you). I intended to speak, but he entered a second time and said the same, and the Prophet ﷺ replied, ""Wa ʿalayk."" Then he entered a third time and said, ""Al-sāmu ʿalayk."" I said, ""Rather, death be upon you, and the wrath of Allah, O brothers of apes and swine! Do you greet the Messenger of Allah with that which Allah has never greeted him with?"" The Prophet ﷺ looked at me and said: > **""Gently! Indeed, Allah does not love foul speech or vulgarity. They uttered words, and we returned them upon them; it will not harm us in the least, but it will cling to them until the Day of Resurrection. Indeed, they do not envy us over anything as much as they envy us over Friday, to which Allah guided us while they went astray from it; and over the Qiblah, to which Allah guided us while they went astray from it; and over our saying 'Āmīn' behind the Imam.""**"
"1980","Jābir ibn Usāmah al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported: I met the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among his Companions in the marketplace, and I asked, ""Where does the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intend to go?"" They said, ""He intends to mark out a mosque for your people."" He said: > **""I came and he had already marked out a mosque for them and planted a piece of wood in the direction of its Qiblah, establishing it as the Qiblah.""**"
"1981","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported: We prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a cloudy day during a journey facing other than the Qiblah. When he completed the prayer and pronounced the taslīm, the sun appeared. We said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we prayed facing other than the Qiblah!"" He ﷺ said: > **""Your prayer has indeed been elevated with its full merit to Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"1982","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه disliked praying inside the prayer niche (al-miḥrāb), and he said: > **""These belong only to churches, so do not imitate the People of the Book.""**"
"1983","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ghābir al-Alhānī reported: Ḥābis ibn Saʿd al-Ṭāʾī رضي الله عنه—who had lived during the time of the Prophet ﷺ—entered the mosque in the pre-dawn hour (al-saḥar) and saw people praying in the front portion of the mosque. He said, ""Show-offs, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah! Chase them away, for whoever chases them away has obeyed Allah and His Messenger!"" So the people approached them and turned them out. He then said: > **""Indeed, the angels pray in the front portion of the mosque during the pre-dawn hours.""**"
"1984","Murrah al-Hamdānī reported: I told myself that I would pray two rakʿahs behind every single pillar in the Mosque of Kūfah. While I was praying, Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه was present in the mosque, so I went to inform him of what I was doing, but another man preceded me and told him what I was doing. Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""If he only knew that Allah, Majestic and Mighty, is near the closest pillar, he would not have passed beyond it until completing his prayer.""**"
"1985","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The superiority of a house close to the mosque over a distant house is like the superiority of a warrior in the path of Allah over one who stays behind.""**"
"1986","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: > **""We were forbidden from praying in an elevated, ostentatious mosque.""**"
"1987","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: > **""He forbade us—or we were forbidden—from praying in an elevated, ostentatious mosque.""**"
"1988","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported: The Anṣār said, ""Until when will the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray toward these palm branches?"" So they gathered dinars and brought them to the Prophet ﷺ, saying, ""Let us renovate and adorn this mosque."" He ﷺ said: > **""I have no desire to turn away from the path of my brother Moses (Mūsā); rather, let it be a thatch-roof like the thatch-roof of Moses.""**"
"1989","Maʿqil ibn Yasār رضي الله عنه reported: We were with the Prophet ﷺ on a journey and stopped at a place where garlic grew in abundance. Some of the Muslims consumed it, and then they came to the prayer place to pray with the Prophet ﷺ, but he forbade them from it. Later, they came again to the prayer place and he noticed its odor emanating from them, so he ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this plant must never approach our mosque.""**"
"1990","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from these vegetables—garlic, onions, leeks, and radishes—must never approach our mosque; for indeed, the angels are offended by that which offends the children of Adam.""**"
"1991","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from these two plants—garlic and onions—must never approach our place of prayer; and let him come to me so that I may wipe his face and seek refuge for him.""**"
"1992","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this offensive herb—meaning garlic—let him remain in his home.""**"
"1993","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه reported: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Khaybar, the people came upon garlic and began eating it. Thereupon, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this offensive herb must never approach our mosque.""**"
"1994","Anas رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Beware of these two foul-smelling plants, that you should eat them and enter our mosques! If you must eat them, then extinguish their odor thoroughly with fire.""**"
"1995","ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this plant—meaning garlic—must never approach our mosques.""**"
"1996","Khuzaymah ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this offensive herb must never approach our mosques.""**"
"1997","Bashīr al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه—who enjoyed the companionship of the Prophet ﷺ—reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this offensive herb—meaning garlic—must never approach our mosques.""**"
"1998","Abū Thaʿlabah رضي الله عنه reported: He took part in the expedition of Khaybar alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The men found onions and garlic in its gardens and ate from them out of extreme hunger. When the people went to the mosque, the mosque filled with the odor of onions and garlic. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever eats from this offensive plant must never come near us.""**"
"1999","Abū Ghālib reported from Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه—and he believed he raised it to the Prophet ﷺ—who said: > **""Garlic, onions, and leeks are of the filth of Iblīs.""**"
"2000","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Spitting in the mosque is an evil deed, and burying it is a good deed.""**"
"2001","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""When one of you expectorates phlegm in the mosque, let him conceal his phlegm lest it strike the skin or garment of a believer and harm him.""**"
"2002","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever expectorates phlegm in the mosque and does not bury it, it is an evil deed; and if he buries it, it is a good deed.""**"
"2003","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Spitting in the mosque is a sin, and its expiation is burying it.""**"
"2004","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you spits in the mosque, let him not spit to his right, but rather to his left or beneath his foot.""**"
"2005","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command them, when they were in prayer, not to perch hastily on the tips of their feet (al-istiwfāz), and he would say: > **""When one of you spits in prayer, let him not spit in front of his face nor to his right, but rather beneath his foot, and then let him rub it into the earth.""**"
"2006","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The phlegm spat in the direction of the Qiblah will be resurrected on the Day of Resurrection upon the face of the one who cast it.""**"
"2007","Anas رضي الله عنه reported: > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ spit into his garment during prayer, and then rub it between his fingers.""**"
"2008","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever spits in the direction of the Qiblah and does not conceal it, it will come on the Day of Resurrection scorching hot until it falls between his two eyes.""**"
"2009","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood one day and commenced the prayer. He saw phlegm in the direction of the Qiblah, so he removed his sandals, walked toward it, and scraped it off; he did this three times. When he completed his prayer, he turned his face toward the people, praised and glorified Allah, and then said: > **""O people, when any one of you stands in prayer, he stands in a momentous station before a Magnificent Lord, asking for a momentous matter: attaining Paradise and salvation from the Fire. Indeed, when one of you stands in prayer, he stands before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, facing his Lord, with his recording angel on his right and his devilish companion on his left. Therefore, let none of you spit in front of him nor to his right, but rather to his left or beneath his foot; then let him rub it firmly, for he is indeed rubbing it into the ear of Satan. By the One Who sent me with the truth, were the veil between you and him to be removed, or were he permitted to speak, he would surely complain of the agony he suffers from that!""**"
"2010","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, when the servant stands in prayer, the gardens of Paradise are opened for him, the veils between him and his Lord are removed, and the wide-eyed maidens of Paradise face toward him, so long as he does not blow his nose or expectorate phlegm.""**"
"2011","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded a man to lead the people in Ẓuhr prayer, but he spat in the direction of the Qiblah while leading the people. When the time for ʿAṣr prayer arrived, the Prophet ﷺ sent for another man to lead. The first man became apprehensive and came to the Prophet ﷺ, asking, ""O Messenger of Allah, was something revealed concerning me?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""No, but you spat in front of you while leading the people, thereby causing offense to Allah and the angels.""**"
"2012","ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm رضي الله عنه reported: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spit to his right, to his left, and in front of him.""**"
"2013","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd reported: We were with ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه and he wished to spit. Although there was an empty space to his right, he disliked spitting to his right, even though he was not in prayer."
"2014","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you finds a louse in the mosque, let him bury it.""**"
"2015","Mālik ibn Yukhāmir reported: > **""I saw Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه killing lice and fleas in the mosque.""**"
"2016","A man from the Anṣār رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you finds a louse on his garment, let him wrap it up tightly in his garment and not cast it into the mosque.""**"
"2017","An elder from the people of Mecca from Quraysh reported: A man found a louse on his garment and caught it to cast it into the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Do not do so! Keep it on your garment until you exit the mosque.""**"
"2018","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ underwent cupping—or constructed an enclosure—in the mosque. The narrator asked Ibn ʿUyaynah, ""In the prayer area of his house?"" He replied: > **""No, in the Mosque of the Messenger ﷺ.""**"
"2019","Abū al-ʿĀliyah reported from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ who said: > **""I remember for you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed ablution in the mosque.""**"
"2020","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reported: > **""We ate roasted meat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day while we were in the mosque. When the prayer was called, we did nothing more than wipe our hands with the pebbles.""**"
"2021","Bilāl رضي الله عنه reported: He came to the Prophet ﷺ to notify him of the prayer: > **""And he found him taking his pre-dawn meal (suḥūr) in the prayer place of his home.""**"
"2022","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: > **""A jar of fresh date beverage (faḍīkh) that was effervescing was brought to the Prophet ﷺ while he was in the Mosque of al-Faḍīkh, and he drank from it; for that reason it was named the Mosque of al-Faḍīkh.""**"
"2023","Asmāʾ bint Yazīd رضي الله عنها reported: Abū Dharr al-Ghifārī رضي الله عنه used to serve the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Whenever he finished his service, he would take refuge in the mosque, for it was his home in which he slept. One night, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered and found Abū Dharr lying asleep on the ground in the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ nudged him with his foot until he sat upright, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him, ""Do I not see you sleeping?"" Abū Dharr replied: > **""O Messenger of Allah, where else should I sleep? Do I have any home other than this?""**"
"2024","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه reported: > **""He used to serve the Prophet ﷺ, and whenever he finished his service, he would come to the mosque and lie down in it.""**"
"2025","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, for the mosques there are pillars (constant devotees); the angels are their companions. If they are absent, the angels inquire about them; if they fall ill, the angels visit them; and if they have a need, the angels assist them.""** He ﷺ then said: > **""The one who sits in the mosque attains one of three virtues: a beneficial brother gained in faith, a word of profound wisdom, or an anticipated mercy from Allah.""**"
"2026","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The mosque is the home of every pious person. And Allah has guaranteed for whoever has the mosque as his home comfort, mercy, and safe passage across the Bridge (al-Ṣirāṭ) to the good pleasure of Allah and into Paradise.""**"
"2027","Abū ʿUthmān reported: Salmān رضي الله عنه wrote to Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه: ""O my brother, let the mosque be your home, for indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The mosque is the home of every pious person, and Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has guaranteed for whoever makes the mosques his homes comfort, mercy, and safe passage across the Bridge (al-Ṣirāṭ).'""**"
"2028","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the houses of Allah upon the earth are the mosques, and it is incumbent upon Allah to honor His visitor.""**"
"2029","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما reported: I heard my grandfather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, say: > **""Whoever frequents the mosques regularly will attain a beneficial brother for the sake of Allah, fine knowledge, a word guiding him to righteousness, a word averting him from ruin, the abandonment of sins out of modesty and fear of Allah, a blessing, or an anticipated mercy.""**"
"2030","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, those who frequent and maintain the houses of Allah are the people of Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"2031","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever loves the mosque and is attached to it, Allah loves him.""**"
"2032","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported: > **""We stayed in Medina for two years before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived among us, establishing the prayer and inhabiting the mosques.""**"
"2033","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Satan is the wolf of humankind, just like the wolf of sheep that seizes the isolated and straying sheep. Therefore, beware of isolated mountain paths, and hold fast to the congregation, the community, and the mosque.""**"
"2034","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There are six gatherings wherein a believer is under the guarantee of Allah Almighty so long as he is in any of them: in a congregational mosque, visiting an ill person, attending a funeral, staying in his home, or in the company of an equitable leader whom he honors and respects.""**"
"2035","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no good in the congregating of women except in the mosque, or at the funeral of one who was killed.""**"
"2036","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه saw a group of people leaning their backs against the Qiblah wall of the mosque between the Fajr adhān and the iqāmah, so he said: > **""Do not place a barrier between the angels and their prayer.""**"
"2037","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Let each one of you pray in his own mosque, and do not go chasing after other mosques.""**"
"2038","Abū ʿAmr al-Shaybānī reported: > **""Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to patrol the mosque, and whenever he found anyone lingering in it, he would expel him, except for a person engaged in prayer.""**"
"2039","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, among the portents of the Hour is that a man will walk through the length and breadth of the mosque without praying two rakʿahs in it.""**"
"2040","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""In the end times, there will be people who sit in the mosques in circles with their focus set upon the worldly life. Do not sit with them, for Allah has no need of them.""**"
"2041","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, everything has its rubbish, and the rubbish of the mosque is [idle disputing and swearing]: 'No, by Allah!' and 'Yes, by Allah!'""**"
"2042","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not take the mosques as pathways (thoroughfares), except for remembrance of Allah or prayer.""**"
"2043","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported: A man entered the mosque calling out for a lost camel/item, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""May you never find it!""**"
"2044","Thawbān رضي الله عنه reported: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever you see reciting poetry in the mosque, say to him three times: 'May Allah shatter your mouth!' And whoever you see inquiring about lost property in the mosque, say to him three times: 'May you never find it!' And whoever you see buying or selling in the mosque, say: 'May Allah grant no profit to your trade!'""** He added: ""In this manner the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed us."""
"2045","It is narrated on the authority of Ibn Sīrīn—or another narrator—that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه heard a man announcing lost property [in the mosque], so he silenced him, reprimanded him, and said: > **""We have indeed been forbidden from doing this.""**"
"2046","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Swords shall not be unsheathed nor shall arrows be scattered in the mosques; oaths by Allah shall not be sworn in the mosques; no one—whether a resident or a guest—shall be prevented from taking a midday rest (qaylūlah) in the mosques; and they shall not be constructed with images nor adorned with glass; for they were only built for the preservation of trust and honored with noble sanctity.""**"
"2047","A freed slave of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: While I was with Abū Saʿīd and he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, we entered the mosque, where a man was sitting in the middle of the mosque in the *iḥtibāʾ* posture (with his knees drawn up to his chest), intertwining his fingers with one another. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to him, but the man did not notice the gesture of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So the Prophet ﷺ turned to Abū Saʿīd and said: > **""When one of you is in the mosque, he must not intertwine his fingers, for intertwining them is from Satan; and one of you remains in prayer as long as he is in the mosque until he departs from it.""**"
"2048","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Legal penalties (ḥudūd) shall not be executed in the mosques.""**"
"2049","Abū al-Dardāʾ, Abū Umāmah, and Wāthilah رضي الله عنهم narrated: We heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Keep away from your mosques your young children, the insane, your disputes, your raised voices, the unsheathing of your swords, and the execution of your legal penalties; perfume them with incense once every seven days, and set up places of purification at the gates of your mosques.""**"
"2050","Makḥūl traced it back to Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه, who elevated it to the Prophet ﷺ, saying: > **""Keep away from your mosques your young children, your disputes, your legal penalties, and your buying and selling; perfume them with incense on the day of your Friday gathering, and place your places of purification at their gates.""**"
"2051","Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrated: We were in the company of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه in the mosque when a man began turning over arrows in his hands. Abū Saʿīd said: > **""Did this man not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade brandishing weapons in the mosque?""**"
"2052","ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal al-Muzanī رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Do not pray in the resting places of camels, for indeed they were created from the jinn; do you not see their eyes and their demeanor when they take fright? But pray in the pens of sheep, for they are closer to mercy.""**"
"2053","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray in the pens of sheep, but he would not pray in the resting places of camels or cattle.""**"
"2054","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray in the pens of sheep, and do not pray in the resting places—or kneeling spots—of camels.""**"
"2055","Usayd ibn Ḥuḍayr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Perform ablution after eating camel meat and do not pray in their resting places; and do not perform ablution after eating mutton, and pray in their pens.""**"
"2056","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about praying in the pens of sheep. He said: > **""Wipe the dust from their noses and pray in their pens, for indeed they are among the beasts of Paradise.""**"
"2057","Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""Admit my Companions to see me."" When they entered upon him, he removed the veil covering his face and said: > **""May Allah curse the Jews and Christians; they took the graves of their Prophets as places of worship.""**"
"2058","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""May Allah curse the Jews; they took the graves of their Prophets as places of worship.""**"
"2059","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Among the worst of people are those whom the Hour overtakes while they are still alive, and those who take graves as places of worship.""**"
"2060","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not pray toward a grave, and do not pray upon a grave.""**"
"2061","ʿAwn ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated: I met Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه and said, ""Nothing brought me to the Levant except to meet you, so narrate to me what you heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying, ""O Allah, have mercy upon us and forgive us,"" and he [also] narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade us from praying toward graves or sitting upon them.""**"
"2062","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade praying between graves.""**"
"2063","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: During the illness in which he passed away, the Prophet ﷺ repeatedly bade me to permit the people to enter upon him, fainting between each time, and on each of the three occasions he warned: > **""May Allah curse a people who took the graves of their Prophets as places of worship.""**"
"2064","Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May Allah curse the Jews; they took the graves of their Prophets as places of worship."" [The narrator added: And I believe he also said: ""Expel the Jews from the land of the Ḥijāz.""]**"
"2065","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ prayed: > **""O Allah, I seek refuge in You lest my grave be taken as an idol; for indeed, the wrath of Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, is exceedingly severe against a people who took the graves of their Prophets as places of worship.""**"
"2066","Umm Ayman رضي الله عنها narrated: The Prophet ﷺ said to me, ""Hand me the prayer mat (khumrah) from the mosque."" I said, ""I am menstruating."" He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, your menstruation is not in your hand.""**"
"2067","ʿAṭiyyah ibn Sufyān ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The delegation of Thaqīf came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during Ramaḍān, so he pitched a tent for them in the mosque; then when they embraced Islam, they fasted alongside him.""**"
"2068","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه narrated: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ perform ablution in the most excellent manner, and then he said: > **""Whoever performs ablution like this ablution of mine, then comes to the mosque and prays two rakʿahs therein, all his past sins will be forgiven.""**"
"2069","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No Muslim performs ablution, perfecting his ablution, and then comes to the mosque and prays two rakʿahs therein, and then asks Allah for forgiveness, except that Allah forgives him.""**"
"2070","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When a man purifies himself and then comes to the mosque awaiting the prayer, his two recording angels—or his recording angel—write down ten good deeds for him for every step he takes. And the one who sits awaiting the prayer is like the one devoutly standing in prayer, and he is recorded among those who pray from the moment he leaves his house until he returns to it.""**"
"2071","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""No servant leaves his house in the morning or the evening going to the mosque except that his steps are such that one step serves as an expiation [for a sin] and another step elevates him in rank.""**"
"2072","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever goes to the congregational mosque, one step erases a bad deed and another step records for him a good deed, both while going and while returning.""**"
"2073","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No Muslim performs ablution, perfecting his ablution, and then walks to one of the houses of Allah to perform an obligatory prayer therein, except that with one step a good deed is written for him, with another a bad deed is erased from him, and with another he is elevated a rank.""**"
"2074","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you performs ablution, perfecting his ablution, and then heads out to the mosque motivated by nothing other than the prayer, his left foot continues to erase a bad deed and the other firmly records a good deed until he enters the mosque.""**"
"2075","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Going to the mosque in the morning and evening is part of jihād in the cause of Allah.""**"
"2076","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who walk in the darkness to the mosques of complete light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2077","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that Banū Salamah said, ""O Messenger of Allah, should we sell our houses and move near to you, for there is a valley between us and you?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Remain where you are, for indeed you are its anchors (protecting the borders); and no servant takes a step toward prayer except that Allah records a reward for him on account of it.""**"
"2078","Zayd ibn Ḥārithah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who walk in the darkness to the mosques of a complete light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2079","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who walk in the darkness to the mosques of complete light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2080","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah shall illuminate with a radiant light on the Day of Resurrection those who make their way to the mosques through the darkness.""**"
"2081","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who walk to the mosques in the darkness of complete light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2082","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who walk to the mosques in the darkness of complete light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2083","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever walks in the darkness of the night to the mosque will meet Allah, Mighty and Majestic is He, with light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2084","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه also narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever walks in the darkness of night to a mosque, Allah will grant him light on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2085","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who walk in the darknesses to the mosques of a magnificent light from Allah on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2086","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Give glad tidings to those who travel early in the darkness to the mosques of pulpits of light on the Day of Resurrection; the people will be stricken with terror while they will not be terrified.""**"
"2087","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs ablution in his house, perfecting his ablution, and then comes to the mosque, he is a visitor unto Allah; and it is the duty of the host to honor his visitor.""**"
"2088","Abū Wāqid al-Laythī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever frequently attends this prayer, all his previous sins will be forgiven.""**"
"2089","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When you come to the prayer, come to it with dignity and tranquility; pray whatever portion you catch, and make up whatever you missed.""**"
"2090","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you come to the prayer, come with tranquility upon you; pray whatever portion you catch, and make up whatever you were preceded in.""**"
"2091","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated: While we were praying with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he heard the clamor of men behind him. When he finished his prayer, he asked, ""What was the matter with you?"" They said, ""We rushed to catch the prayer."" He said: > **""Do not do so. Let each of you pray whatever portion he catches, and make up whatever he missed.""**"
"2092","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه narrated: I was walking with the Prophet ﷺ on our way to the prayer, and he was taking short steps. He asked, ""Do you know why I am taking short steps?"" I replied, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He ﷺ said: > **""The servant remains in prayer as long as he is in pursuit of the prayer.""**"
"2093","Thābit narrated: I was walking with Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه at al-Zāwiyah when he heard the adhān. He then began taking short steps until I entered the mosque. He then said: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once walked with me in this exact manner and asked, ""Do you know, O Anas, why I walked with you in this manner?"" I replied, ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He ﷺ said: > **""So that the number of steps in pursuit of the prayer may be multiplied.""**"
"2094","Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه hastened toward the prayer, so he was questioned regarding that, whereupon he said: > **""Is not the prayer the most deserving thing toward which you should hasten?""**"
"2095","A man from the tribe of Ṭayyiʾ narrated from his father that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه went out toward the mosque and began jogging. It was said to him, ""Do you do this when you yourself forbid it?"" He replied: > **""I only intended to catch the initial portion of the prayer and the opening takbīr.""**"
"2096","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""When entering the mosque, the Prophet ﷺ used to say: 'O Allah, open for me the gates of Your mercy' (Allāhumma-ftaḥ lī abwāba raḥmatik); and when exiting, he would say: 'O Allah, open for me the gates of Your bounty' (Allāhumma-ftaḥ lī abwāba faḍlik).""**"
"2097","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī that whenever he enters the mosque, he should invoke blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and say: 'O Allah, forgive us our sins and open for us the gates of Your mercy' (Allāhumma-ghfir lanā dhunūbanā wa-ftaḥ lanā abwāba raḥmatik); and when he exits, he should invoke blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ and say: 'O Allah, open for us the gates of Your bounty' (Allāhumma-ftaḥ lanā abwāba faḍlik).""**"
"2098","Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not prevent the female servants of Allah from the mosques, but let them go out unperfumed.""**"
"2099","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not prevent the female servants of Allah from the mosques of Allah.""**"
"2100","Sālim narrated regarding ʿUmar رضي الله عنه: ʿUmar was a man of intense protective jealousy (ghayrah). When he went out to the prayer, ʿĀtikah bint Zayd [his wife] would follow him. He disliked her going out, yet he disliked preventing her because he used to narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When your women seek permission from you to go to the prayer, do not prevent them.""**"
"2101","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه was asked whether elderly women used to attend the prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He replied: > **""Yes, and young women as well; they used to pray behind our shoulders with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2102","Umm Salamah bint Ḥakīm رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""I witnessed elderly women praying the obligatory prayers with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2103","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The women used to pray the Dawn prayer (al-Ghadāh) with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and then depart wrapped in their woolen garments (murūṭ).""**"
"2104","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no good in a gathering of women except in the mosque, or at the funeral of one who was killed.""**"
"2105","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best places of prayer (masājid) for women are the innermost parts of their homes.""**"
"2106","Umm Ḥumayd رضي الله عنها—the wife of Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī رضي الله عنه—came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I love praying alongside you."" He ﷺ said: > **""I know that you love praying with me, yet your prayer in your private room is better than your prayer in your inner room, and your prayer in your inner room is better than your prayer in your house courtyard, and your prayer in your house courtyard is better than your prayer in the mosque of your people, and your prayer in the mosque of your people is better than your prayer in my mosque.""** [She then gave orders and a place of prayer was established for her in the innermost and darkest part of her home, where she continued to pray until she met Allah, Mighty and Majestic.]"
"2107","Umm Ḥumayd رضي الله عنها narrated: I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, our husbands prevent us from praying alongside you, though we love praying with you."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Your prayer in your innermost rooms is better than your prayer in your outer chambers; and your prayer in your outer chambers is better than your prayer in your courtyards; and your prayer in your courtyards is better than your prayer in the congregation.""**"
"2108","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A woman's prayer in her innermost room is better than her prayer in her outer room; her prayer in her outer room is better than her prayer in her courtyard; and her prayer in her courtyard is better than her prayer outside.""**"
"2109","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""A woman's prayer in her innermost room is more virtuous than her prayer in her outer room; her prayer in her outer room is more virtuous than her prayer in her courtyard; and her prayer in her courtyard is more virtuous than her prayer anywhere else."" He then added: ""Indeed, when a woman goes out, Satan elevates his gaze toward her.""**"
"2110","Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah narrated from his mother رضي الله عنها, who said: > **""I saw the elderly women praying alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the mosque.""**"
"2111","She [the mother of Sulaymān ibn Abī Ḥathmah] رضي الله عنها also narrated: > **""I saw women from among the elderly praying the obligatory prayers alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2112","Umm Sulaym bint Abī Ḥakīm رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""I witnessed elderly women praying alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2113","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""No woman prays in a place that is better for her than the innermost part of her home, except if it be al-Masjid al-Ḥarām or the Mosque of the Prophet ﷺ, or an elderly woman who goes out shuffling in her leather socks (khuffayn).""**"
"2114","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه also used to swear with solemn emphasis: > **""There is no place of prayer better for a woman than her home—except during Ḥajj or ʿUmrah—save for an elderly woman who has lost all desire for marriage and walks taking short, shuffling steps.""** [The sub-narrator asked: ""What is meant by 'shuffling steps' (*fī manqalayhā*)?"" He replied: ""An old woman whose stride has become shortened.""]"
"2115","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""No prayer performed by a woman is more beloved to Allah than that performed in the darkest place of her home.""**"
"2116","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""A woman is an inviolable sanctuary (ʿawrah), and indeed when she goes out, Satan elevates his gaze toward her; and she is never closer to Allah than when she is in the innermost recess of her home.""**"
"2117","Maymūnah bint Saʿd رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""No woman goes out displaying an ostentatious fragrance so that men look at her, except that she remains under the wrath of Allah until she returns to her house.""**"
"2118","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Women are indeed an inviolable sanctuary (ʿawrah). A woman may leave her house having no harmful intention, yet Satan gazes up at her and whispers: 'You do not pass by anyone except that you fascinate him.' A woman may put on her clothes and be asked: 'Where do you intend to go?' and she replies: 'I want to visit a sick person, attend a funeral, or pray in a mosque.' Yet no woman ever worships her Lord in a manner comparable to worshiping Him within her own home.""**"
"2119","Abū ʿAmr al-Shaybānī narrated: He saw ʿAbdullāh [ibn Masʿūd] رضي الله عنه sending the women out of the mosque on Friday, saying: > **""Go out to your homes; that is better for you.""**"
"2120","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: ""The men and women among the Children of Israel used to pray together. When a woman had a male companion (lover), she would wear wooden platform shoes to appear taller for him. Thereupon, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, cast menstruation upon them."" Ibn Masʿūd used to say: > **""Send them out from where Allah has sent them out.""** (We asked Abū Bakr: ""What are the *qālabayn*?"" They replied: ""Two elevated platform shoes made of wood."")"
"2121","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, when a servant sits in his place of prayer after the prayer, the angels invoke blessings upon him, and their prayer for him is: 'O Allah, forgive him.' And if he sits waiting for the prayer, they invoke blessings upon him, and their prayer for him is: 'O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy upon him.'""**"
"2122","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Performing ablution thoroughly despite adversity, exerting one's steps towards the mosques, and awaiting prayer after prayer thoroughly washes away sins.""**"
"2123","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The one who waits for a prayer after a prayer is like a knight whose steed gallops briskly with him in the cause of Allah, and he is engaged in the greater guard duty (al-ribāṭ al-akbar).""**"
"2124","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A servant continues to be in a state of prayer as long as he remains in his place of prayer waiting for the prayer, while the angels say: 'O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy upon him,' until he departs or breaks his ablution.""** (The narrator asked: ""What does breaking ablution mean?"" He replied: ""I asked Abū Saʿīd the same question, and he said: 'Passing wind silently or audibly.'"")"
"2125","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked: ""Shall I not inform you of that which expiates sins?"" They replied: ""Certainly, O Messenger of Allah."" He ﷺ said: > **""Performing ablution thoroughly despite difficulties, taking frequent steps to the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another—that indeed is the ribāṭ (the true spiritual vigilance).""**"
"2126","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Walking on foot to congregational prayers is an expiation for sins, as is performing ablution thoroughly in freezing cold mornings, and waiting for one prayer after another.""**"
"2127","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A righteous dream is glad tidings from Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and it constitutes one of seventy parts of prophethood; and indeed, this fire of yours is one part of seventy parts of the scorching heat of Hell; and whoever comes to the mosque waiting for the prayer is considered in prayer as long as he does not break his ablution; and whoever follows up one prayer after another is considered in prayer as long as he does not break his ablution.""**"
"2128","A woman from among those who gave the pledge of allegiance رضي الله عنها narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited us along with his companions from Banū Salamah. We presented food to him and he ate; then we brought him water for ablution and he performed ablution. He then turned to his companions and said: 'Shall I not inform you of the expiators of sins?' They replied: 'Certainly.' He ﷺ said:"" > **""Performing ablution thoroughly despite unpleasant conditions, taking frequent steps towards the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another.""**"
"2129","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah effaces sins and expiates misdeeds?"" They said: ""Certainly, O Messenger of Allah."" He ﷺ said: > **""Performing ablution thoroughly in difficult circumstances, taking frequent steps to the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another—and that is the ribāṭ (steadfast vigil).""**"
"2130","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ate a meal in the house of al-ʿAbbās or in the house of Ḥamzah, and said: > **""Certain people of my nation will surely plunge unjustifiably into that which Allah bestowed upon His Messenger of spoils (fayʾ), having no share in the Hereafter other than that; and the expiations of sins are: performing ablution thoroughly, taking frequent steps to the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another.""**"
"2131","Abū Umayyah al-Thaqafī narrated: Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه came out when he prayed Zuhr and said: ""Stay in your places until I return to you."" Then he came out wearing an upper cloak, and when he had prayed ʿAṣr, he said: ""Shall I not narrate to you something done by the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?"" We said: ""Certainly."" He said: ""They prayed the first prayer (Zuhr) with him and then remained seated. The Prophet ﷺ came out to them and said: 'Have you not moved since?' They replied: 'No.' He ﷺ said:"" > **""If only you could see your Lord opening a gate of heaven and showing your gathering to His angels, boasting of you while you wait for the prayer!""**"
"2132","ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""One of you continues to be in a state of prayer so long as the prayer is what holds him back.""**"
"2133","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The virtue of a man's prayer in congregation over his prayer alone is twenty-odd degrees.""** (And in another narration: **""by twenty-five degrees.""** And in another: **""each of them equal to his entire prayer.""** And in another: **""each of them equal to his prayer in his house.""**)"
"2134","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer in congregation surpasses the prayer said alone by twenty-seven degrees.""**"
"2135","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The prayer in congregation surpasses the prayer of an individual—or a man's prayer alone—by twenty-five prayers.""**"
"2136","ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the prayer rows; and between an individual prayer and the congregation is twenty-five degrees.""**"
"2137","Ṣuhayb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A man's prayer in congregation equals twenty-five degrees over his prayer alone.""**"
"2138","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The congregational prayer surpasses a man's prayer alone by twenty-four shares—meaning his prayer becomes twenty-fivefold.""**"
"2139","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The congregational prayer surpasses a man's prayer alone by twenty-five prayers.""**"
"2140","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, Allah, the Blessed and Exalted, is truly pleased with and admires the prayer offered in congregation.""**"
"2141","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is truly pleased with and admires prayer offered in congregation.""**"
"2142","Qabāth ibn Ashyam al-Laythī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer of two men where one leads the other is purer in the sight of Allah than the prayer of four praying separately; and the prayer of four where one leads them is purer in the sight of Allah than the prayer of eight praying separately; and the prayer of eight where one leads them is purer in the sight of Allah than the prayer of a hundred praying separately.""**"
"2143","Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated that he heard Abū Hurayrah and Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهم say: We heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in his final sermon saying: > **""Whoever preserves these five prescribed prayers in congregation will be among the first to cross the Ṣirāṭ like flashing lightning, and Allah will resurrect him in the foremost company of the righteous, and for every day and night he preserved them he will receive the reward of a thousand martyrs slain in the cause of Allah.""**"
"2144","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever would be pleased to meet Allah tomorrow as a Muslim, let him regularly preserve these five prayers wherever the call to them is proclaimed.""**"
"2145","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If those who remain behind from the ʿIshāʾ and Fajr prayers only knew what reward awaits them in both, they would surely come to them even if they had to crawl.""**"
"2146","Abū ʿUmayr ibn Anas reported on the authority of his paternal uncles from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: > **""No hypocrite attends them both regularly""—meaning the morning (Fajr) and ʿIshāʾ prayers.** (Abū Bishr said: ""Meaning, he does not maintain consistency in attending them."")"
"2147","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""If people only knew what virtue lies in attending the late evening prayer (al-ʿatamah) on Wednesday night, they would surely come to it even if they had to crawl.""**"
"2148","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever prays ʿIshāʾ in congregation and then prays four rakʿahs before leaving the mosque, it will be equivalent for him to the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr).""**"
"2149","A man from the tribe of Nakhaʿ narrated: I heard Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه when death approached him say: ""I shall narrate to you a hadith that I heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said:"" > **""Worship Allah as though you see Him, for even if you do not see Him, He surely sees you; count yourself among the deceased; beware of the supplication of the oppressed, for it is answered; and whoever among you is able to attend the two prayers—ʿIshāʾ and Fajr—even by crawling, let him do so.""**"
"2150","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No prayer is heavier upon the hypocrites than the ʿIshāʾ and Fajr prayers; and if they knew what virtue is in both of them, they would surely come to them even if they had to crawl.""**"
"2151","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Whenever we found a man absent from the Fajr and ʿIshāʾ prayers, we would think ill of him.""**"
"2152","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Whenever we found a man absent from the morning prayer (ṣalāt al-ghadāh), we would think ill of him.""**"
"2153","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever prays ʿIshāʾ in congregation has surely attained his share of the Night of Decree (Laylat al-Qadr).""**"
"2154","Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān رضي الله عنه narrated: ""It was an intensely dark and rainy night, so I thought to myself: 'If only I seize the opportunity tonight to attend the late evening prayer (al-ʿatamah) with the Prophet ﷺ.' So I did so. When the Prophet ﷺ finished the prayer, he caught sight of me while holding a dried date-palm branch upon which he leaned as he walked. He said: 'What brings you here at this hour, O Qatādah?' I said: 'I wished to seize the blessing of attending the night prayer with you, O Prophet of Allah.' He then handed me the palm branch and said:"" > **""Indeed, Satan has taken your place behind you in your home; so take this palm branch and hold onto it until you reach your house, then corner him in the angle of the house and strike him with the branch.""** (Qatādah said: ""I exited the mosque, and the palm branch shone with light like a candle. I guided myself by its light until I reached my family and found that they had gone to sleep. I looked into the corner, and there was a hedgehog-like creature; I continued striking it with the palm branch until it fled."")"
"2155","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever prays the morning prayer in congregation is under the protection and covenant of Allah; and whoever violates the covenant of Allah, Allah will throw him face-first into the Fire.""**"
"2156","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs ablution and then comes to the mosque, prays two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer, and then sits until he prays Fajr, his prayer on that day will be recorded among the prayer of the truly righteous, and he will be inscribed among the honored delegation of the Most Merciful.""**"
"2157","Bilāl the Muʾadh-dhin رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I called the adhān on a freezing cold night, but no one came. Then I called again, and still no one came—doing so three times. The Prophet ﷺ asked: 'What is the matter with them?' I said: 'The bitter cold has kept them back.' Thereupon, the Prophet ﷺ supplicated:"" > **""O Allah, withhold the cold from them!""** (Bilāl said: ""I bear witness that I later saw them fanning themselves at the morning prayer on account of the heat."")"
"2158","Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated: Al-Ḥārith ibn Ḥassān رضي الله عنه—who was graced with companionship of the Prophet ﷺ—got married. In those days, when a man married, he would remain secluded behind the curtain for several days and not emerge for the morning prayer in congregation. It was said to him: ""Are you going out while you have just consummated your marriage with your wife tonight?"" He replied: > **""By Allah, any woman who prevents me from praying the morning prayer in congregation is surely an evil woman!""**"
"2159","Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is sheer callousness, disbelief, and hypocrisy that a person hears Allah's caller proclaiming the call to prayer and inviting to success, and yet does not respond to him.""**"
"2160","Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه also reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is sufficient misery and bitter ruin for a believer that he hears the muʾadh-dhin calling to prayer and does not respond to him.""**"
"2161","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever hears the call to prayer and does not respond, without suffering any harm or valid excuse, there is no prayer for him.""**"
"2162","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were it not for the women and offspring inside the homes, I would have established the ʿIshāʾ prayer and commanded my young men to set ablaze with fire all that is within those houses.""**"
"2163","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Men living around the mosque who do not attend the late ʿIshāʾ prayer in congregation must desist, or I will surely burn around their houses with bundles of firewood.""**"
"2164","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: Ibn Umm Maktūm رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, my home is distant, I am blind, and I hear the call to prayer."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""If you hear the call to prayer, then respond to it, even if you must come crawling or creeping.""**"
"2165","Ibn Umm Maktūm رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to the mosque and saw a sparse attendance among the people, so he said: ""I was on the verge of appointing an imam to lead the people and then going out, so that whenever I found a man staying back from the prayer in his house, I would burn it down upon him."" Ibn Umm Maktūm said: ""O Messenger of Allah, between me and the mosque are date palms and trees, and I cannot find a guide at all hours. Is there leeway for me to pray in my house?"" The Prophet ﷺ asked: ""Do you hear the iqāmah?"" He said: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: > **""Then come to it!""**"
"2166","Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah رضي الله عنه narrated: A blind man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""I hear the call to prayer, but I may not find a guide and it becomes arduous for me. May I designate a place of prayer within my dwelling?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: ""Does the call reach you?"" He replied: ""Yes."" He ﷺ said: > **""When you hear it, then respond!""** (And in another narration: **""Then respond to the caller of Allah!""**)"
"2167","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated: Ibn Umm Maktūm—who was blind, and concerning whom Allah revealed: *{He frowned and turned away, because there came to him the blind man}*, and who was a nobleman of Quraysh—came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, may my father and mother be ransomed for you! As you see, I have grown old, my bones have grown fragile, my eyesight has departed, and my guide is not compatible with me. Do you find any dispensation for me to pray the prayers in my home?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: ""Do you hear the muʾadh-dhin in the house where you reside?"" He answered: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I find no dispensation for you. And if this person who stays behind from the prayer in congregation only knew what awaits the one who walks to it, he would surely come to it even if he had to crawl upon his hands and feet.""**"
"2168","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه narrated: Ibn Umm Maktūm came to the Prophet ﷺ—and he was blind—and pleaded his hardship, requesting a dispensation regarding attending the ʿIshāʾ and Fajr prayers, saying: ""Between me and you lies a flood channel."" The Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""Do you hear the call to prayer?""** (He answered, ""Yes,"" once or twice, but the Prophet ﷺ did not grant him any concession for that.)"
"2169","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I was truly on the verge of commanding Bilāl to call the iqāmah for prayer, then proceed to people who heard the call and did not respond, and burn down their houses over them.""**"
"2170","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""If a man were to invite people to a piece of meat on a bone or two sheep hooves, they would eagerly answer him; yet they are called to this prayer in congregation and do not come! I was truly on the verge of ordering a man to lead the people in congregational prayer, then proceeding to people who heard the call and did not respond, to set their houses ablaze with fire upon them. Indeed, none holds back from it except a hypocrite!""**"
"2171","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Whoever hears 'Come to success' (Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ) and does not respond has indeed abandoned the Sunnah of Muḥammad ﷺ.""**"
"2172","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, that which I fear most for my nation is the Book and milk (herds). As for milk, people will wander off to pastures out of fondness for it and abandon the Friday and congregational prayers; and as for the Book, parts of it will be opened to certain people who will then dispute with it against those who believe.""**"
"2173","A man from Banū al-Dīl narrated: ""I set out with some young camels of mine to deliver them to the herdsman. I passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was leading the people in the Zuhr prayer, but I passed along and did not pray with him. When I delivered my camels and returned, this was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. He asked: 'O so-and-so, what prevented you from praying with us when you passed by us?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I had already prayed in my home.' The Prophet ﷺ replied:"" > **""Even so (you should have joined us)!""**"
"2174","Ibn Abī al-Kharīf narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""My brother and I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Masjid al-Khayf. We had already performed the obligatory prayer in our lodging, so we did not pray with the congregation. He ﷺ asked: 'What prevented you two from praying with us?' We answered: 'We had already performed the obligatory prayer in our lodging.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""When a man performs the obligatory prayer in his house and then catches a congregation, let him pray with them, and his prayer at home will be counted as a voluntary prayer.""**"
"2175","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Prophet ﷺ noticed two men sitting in the rear of the people at Masjid al-Khayf. He ordered them to be brought, and they were brought with their shoulders trembling out of awe. He ﷺ asked: ""What kept you two from praying with us?"" They answered: ""We had already prayed in our camp."" He ﷺ said: > **""Should you not have prayed with us? For it would have been a voluntary prayer for you, and your first prayer would remain the obligatory one.""**"
"2176","ʿAbdullāh ibn Sarjis رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man sitting in the mosque while the people were praying. When he concluded the prayer, he ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays in his house and then enters the mosque while the people are praying, let him pray with them, and it will be a voluntary prayer for him.""**"
"2177","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from the outskirts of Madinah intending to pray, but found that the people had already finished the prayer. So he turned towards his house, gathered his household, and led them in prayer.""**"
"2178","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Two or more constitute a congregation.""**"
"2179","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying alone and said: ""Is there not a man who will give charity to this person by praying with him?"" A man stood up and prayed with him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""These two are a congregation.""**"
"2180","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led his Companions in the Zuhr prayer. Then a man from his Companions entered, and the Prophet ﷺ asked him: ""What held you back from the prayer, O so-and-so?"" The man mentioned an excuse that detained him. Then he stood up to pray, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Is there not a man who will give charity to this person by praying with him?""** (So a man stood up and prayed with him.)"
"2181","Al-Walīd ibn Mālik narrated: A man entered the mosque and began to pray. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Is there not a man who will give charity to this person by praying with him?"" A man stood up and prayed with him, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""These two are a congregation.""**"
"2182","Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه narrated that a man entered the mosque after the Prophet ﷺ had finished praying. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Is there not a man who will give charity to this person by praying with him?""**"
"2183","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had completed the Zuhr prayer and was sitting in the mosque when a man arrived, entered, and began praying alone. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Is there not a man who will give charity to this person by praying with him?""**"
"2184","Thābit—narrating most likely from Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه—narrated that a man arrived after the Prophet ﷺ had already prayed, and stood praying alone. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Who will trade in good deeds with this person by praying with him?""**"
"2185","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""An obligatory prayer in the central congregational mosque (al-Masjid al-Jāmiʿ) is equivalent to a righteous, flawless Hajj (ḥajj mabrūr), and a voluntary prayer is like an accepted pilgrimage; and the prayer in the central congregational mosque is favored over other mosques by five hundred prayers.""**"
"2186","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""No concession was ever granted for abandoning the congregational prayer except for one who is in fear or one who is sick.""**"
"2187","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When dinner is served and the prayer is called, begin with dinner.""**"
"2188","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When dinner is served and the time for prayer has arrived, begin with dinner.""**"
"2189","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated in a marfūʿ tradition that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When dinner is served and the prayer has been established (iqāmah called), begin with dinner.""**"
"2190","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When dinner is served and the prayer has been established, begin with dinner.""**"
"2191","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the prayer is established while one of you is fasting, let him begin with the evening meal before the Maghrib prayer, and do not rush through your dinner.""**"
"2192","Samurah ibn Jundab رضي الله عنه narrated that on the Day of Khaybar, on a rainy day, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ announced: > **""Pray in your dwellings.""** (And in another narration: **""out of dislike that he should cause hardship for us.""**)"
"2193","Nuʿaym ibn al-Naḥḥām رضي الله عنه narrated: ""On a freezing cold night, while I was under my blanket, I heard the muʾadh-dhin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and wished that he would say, 'Pray in your dwellings.' When he reached *Ḥayya ʿalā al-falāḥ* (Come to success), he called out:"" > **""Pray in your dwellings!""** (Nuʿaym said: ""I later inquired about it, and found that the Prophet ﷺ had indeed commanded him to say so."")"
"2194","Nuʿaym ibn al-Naḥḥām رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The call for the morning prayer was announced on a bitterly cold day while I was wrapped in my wife's woolen mantle. I said to myself: 'If only the caller would say: And whoever remains seated, there is no blame upon him!' Suddenly, at the end of his call, the caller of the Prophet ﷺ proclaimed:"" > **""And whoever remains seated, there is no blame upon him.""** (And in the narration of al-Ṭabarānī: When he said, *'Al-ṣalātu khayrun min al-nawm'* [Prayer is better than sleep], he proclaimed: **""And whoever remains seated, there is no blame upon him.""**)"
"2195","Nuʿaym ibn al-Naḥḥām رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I was with my wife under her woolen sheet on a bitterly cold morning when the caller of the Prophet ﷺ began calling to the Fajr prayer. When I heard him, I thought to myself: 'I wish he would say: Whoever remains sitting at home, there is no blame upon him.' When the caller proclaimed, 'Prayer is better than sleep,' he immediately added:"" > **""And whoever remains seated at home, there is no blame upon him.""**"
"2196","ʿAmr ibn Aws narrated: ""A Companion who heard the caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed me that when the prayer was called, or when the time for prayer arrived (or words to that effect), the caller announced on account of rain:"" > **""Pray in your dwellings!""**"
"2197","Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""We were in the company of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when ʿUrfuṭah ibn Nahyak stood up and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my family and I earn our living from hunting; we have a share of provision and blessing in it, but it occupies us away from the remembrance of Allah and from the congregational prayer, while we stand in need of it. Do you deem it lawful or do you forbid it?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied:"" > **""I deem it lawful, for Allah, the Exalted and Majestic, has made it lawful. It is a noble pursuit, and Allah is most entitled to accept an excuse. There were messengers of Allah before me who all hunted or sought game. And when you are absent from the congregational prayer while seeking livelihood, it suffices you that you maintain love for the congregation and its people, and love for the remembrance of Allah and those who remember Him.""**"
"2198","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prayed in a single garment wrapped across his body (mutawashshiḥan), using its excess fabric to protect himself from the heat and cold of the ground.""**"
"2199","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Mughīrah al-Makhzūmī narrated: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying in the house of Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, wearing a single garment with nothing else upon him.""**"
"2200","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Umayyah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying in a single garment, having crossed its two ends over his shoulders.""**"
"2201","Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""I saw my father [Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه] praying in a single garment, so I said: 'O my father, do you pray in a single garment while your other clothes are laid aside?' He replied:"" > **""O my dear daughter, the last prayer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ever prayed behind me was while wearing a single garment.""**"
"2202","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: ""One who saw the Prophet ﷺ informed me:"" > **""The Prophet ﷺ was praying in a single garment, having crossed its two ends over his shoulders.""**"
"2203","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I spent a night with the family of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to pray with one end of a quilt over him while the other end covered ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, who was menstruating and not praying.""**"
"2204","Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated: ""A Companion who saw the Prophet ﷺ informed me:"" > **""The Prophet ﷺ was seen praying in a single garment, having crossed its two ends over his opposite shoulders.""**"
"2205","Abū Naḍrah narrated: ""Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه said: 'Praying in a single garment is a Sunnah; we used to do so in the company of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and no fault was found with us.' However, Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said:"" > **""That was only when garments were scarce; but when Allah grants abundance, praying in two garments is purer and more commendable.""**"
"2206","Muḥammad ibn Abī Sufyān narrated that he heard Umm Ḥabībah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, say: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying while wearing a single garment.""**"
"2207","Umm al-Faḍl bint al-Ḥārith رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer in his house wrapped in a single garment (mutawashshiḥan).""**"
"2208","ʿAmmār [ibn Yāsir] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prayed in a single garment, wrapping it across his body.""**"
"2209","Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed in a single garment, having crossed its two ends over his shoulders.""**"
"2210","Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out during the illness in which he passed away, leaning upon Usāmah [ibn Zayd] رضي الله عنه and wrapped in a cotton garment, and led the people in prayer.""**"
"2211","Muʿāwiyah [ibn Abī Sufyān] رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I entered upon Umm Ḥabībah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and saw the Prophet ﷺ standing and praying in a single garment, having crossed its two ends over his shoulders. I asked: 'O Umm Ḥabībah, does the Prophet ﷺ pray in a single garment?' She replied:"" > **""Yes, and it is the very garment in which that which happens took place—meaning marital relations.""**"
"2212","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying when he felt the chill of the cold, so he said: 'O ʿĀʾishah, let down your woolen sheet over me.' I said: 'I am menstruating.' The Prophet ﷺ gently remarked at her hesitation, '[Is this an] excuse and stinginess?' and then said:"" > **""Your menses is not in your hand.""**"
"2213","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was standing and praying in a single garment. I stood on his left side, so he turned me around until he positioned me on his right side."" > **""The Prophet ﷺ was standing and praying in a single garment, and when I stood on his left, he turned me around until he placed me on his right side.""**"
"2214","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ caught sight of a man praying while letting his garment drape loosely from his shoulders (sadl), so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ approached him and gathered his garment securely around him.""**"
"2215","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer wrapped in a garment; when its two ends did not reach across to wrap securely, he knotted them together.""**"
"2216","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, the foster-father of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, narrated: > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ and ʿĀʾishah praying under a single garment, half of it over the Prophet ﷺ and half of it over ʿĀʾishah.""**"
"2217","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to us wrapped across his body in a cotton garment, holding a short spear (ʿanazah) in his hand while leaning upon Usāmah ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه; he planted the spear firmly in front of him and then prayed facing toward it.""**"
"2218","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""I entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying sitting with his legs drawn up against his chest wrapped in his garment (muḥtabiyan), with the buttons of his garment unfastened.""**"
"2219","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you pray, raise the hems of your trailing garments, for whatever part of your trailing garments touches the ground is in the Fire.""**"
"2220","ʿUbādah [ibn al-Ṣāmit] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about praying in a single garment, and he replied: > **""If it is wide and spacious, let him wrap it around his upper body; and if it is too small, let him tie it around his waist as an izār.""**"
"2221","Muʿādh [ibn Jabal] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed wearing a single garment tied around his waist (muʾtaziran).""**"
"2222","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer wearing a velvet cloak (qaṭīfah), having crossed its two ends over his shoulders.""**"
"2223","ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying, and I stood on his left side. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took hold of me and placed me on his right side. I was wearing a torn garment that did not adequately cover me, so whenever I prostrated, I held it together with my hand out of fear that my private parts might be exposed while women were praying behind me. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ completed the prayer, he sent for a garment, clothed me with it, and said:"" > **""Wear your worn-out cloth underneath like an under-shirt (and wear this garment over it).""**"
"2224","ʿAbdullāh ibn Sarjis رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Prophet of Allah ﷺ prayed one day wearing a striped woolen cloak (namirah). He then said to one of his Companions: 'Give me your cloak and take my cloak.' The man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, your cloak is of better quality than mine!' The Prophet ﷺ replied:"" > **""Yes, but it has a red stripe in it, and I feared that I might look at it and it would distract me from my prayer.""**"
"2225","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated: > **""I saw Khālid ibn al-Walīd رضي الله عنه leading the people in prayer in the army while wearing a single garment.""**"
"2226","ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb narrated from his father, from his maternal uncle, who said: > **""I came to the Prophet ﷺ during the winter and found them praying wearing hooded cloaks (barānis) and heavy woolen garments (aksiyah), with their hands kept inside them.""**"
"2227","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays, let him wear his two garments, for Allah is most deserving that one should beautify oneself for Him.""**"
"2228","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""If your waist-wrapper is narrow, tie it around your waist; and if it is wide, wrap it around your whole body—meaning during prayer.""**"
"2229","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade praying in trousers alone (without an upper garment).""**"
"2230","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah does not accept the prayer of a woman until she conceals her adornment, nor from a young girl who has reached puberty until she wears a head-veil (khimār).""**"
"2231","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O ʿAlī, instruct your women not to pray completely devoid of adornment (ʿuṭulan), even if by wearing a leather necklace around their necks.""**"
"2232","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaḥsh, the relative through marriage of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ passed by Maʿmar in the courtyard of the mosque while he was sitting with his thighs exposed. The Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""Cover your thigh, O Maʿmar, for indeed the thigh is ʿawrah (nakedness to be concealed).""**"
"2233","Jarhad رضي الله عنه and a group of honorable men from the tribe of Aslam narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by Jarhad while his thigh was uncovered in the mosque. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""O Jarhad, cover your thigh, for indeed the thigh is ʿawrah.""**"
"2234","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""Among the very first things revealed to the Prophet ﷺ was that it was said to him: 'Cover yourself,' and his private parts were never seen exposed after that.""**"
"2235","Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī [al-Bāqir] narrated: ""We said to ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar رضي الله عنهما: 'Narrate to us what you personally heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and saw from him, and do not narrate to us from anyone else even if they are trustworthy.' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:'"" > **""What is between the navel and the knee is ʿawrah.""**"
"2236","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at the marketplace area accompanied by Bilāl رضي الله عنه. He sat dangling his legs into the well and uncovered his thighs. Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه arrived seeking permission to enter, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'O Bilāl, permit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise.' Abū Bakr entered, sat to the right of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, dangled his legs into the well, and uncovered his thighs. Then ʿUmar رضي الله عنه arrived seeking permission to enter, and the Prophet ﷺ said: 'O Bilāl, permit him and give him glad tidings of Paradise.' ʿUmar entered, sat to the left of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, dangled his legs into the well, and uncovered his thighs. Then ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه arrived seeking permission to enter, and the Prophet ﷺ said:"" > **""Permit him, O Bilāl, and give him glad tidings of Paradise upon a calamity that shall afflict him.""**"
"2237","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There is no harm in a man examining a female servant if he intends to purchase her, except for her ʿawrah, which is what lies between her knees and where the waist-wrapper is tied.""**"
"2238","Mujammiʿ ibn Jāriyah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""He saw the Prophet ﷺ praying in his sandals.""**"
"2239","Mujammiʿ ibn Yaʿqūb narrated from a youth from the people of Qubāʾ who met him as an elder [ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ḥabībah رضي الله عنه], who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to us at Qubāʾ and sat at the entrance of the fortress, and the people gathered around him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked for water and was given a drink. He drank while I was sitting to his right—and I was the youngest of the gathering—so he handed the vessel to me and I drank. And I distinctly remember:"" > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer on that day wearing sandals, and he did not remove them.""**"
"2240","Ziyād al-Ḥārithī narrated: ""I heard a man ask Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه: 'Are you the one who forbids people from praying in their sandals?' Abū Hurayrah exclaimed: 'Nay, by the Lord of this Sacred Sanctuary! Nay, by the Lord of this Sacred Sanctuary! Nay, by the Lord of this Sacred Sanctuary!'"" > **""I indeed saw Muḥammad ﷺ praying facing this station while wearing his sandals, and then he departed while they were still upon his feet.""**"
"2241","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray standing and sitting, barefoot and wearing sandals, and he would turn to leave from his right and from his left.""**"
"2242","Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl al-ʿAdawī narrated that someone who heard a Bedouin told him: > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying while wearing two sandals made of cowhide. He spat to his left side and then rubbed the spot where he spat with his sandal.""**"
"2243","ʿAṭāʾ reported from an elderly man from Banū Shaybah: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying near this station [the Maqām] while wearing two tanned, hairless leather sandals (sibtiyyatān).""**"
"2244","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The adornment of prayer is footwear.""**"
"2245","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying in his sandals.""**"
"2246","Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Differ from the Jews and pray in your leather socks (khuffayn) and sandals, for they do not pray in their leather socks nor in their sandals.""**"
"2247","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prayed in sandals and in leather socks (khuffayn).""**"
"2248","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prayed while wearing his sandals.""**"
"2249","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Part of the completeness of prayer is praying in sandals.""**"
"2250","Fayrūz al-Daylamī رضي الله عنه reported that the delegation of Thaqīf arrived before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: > **""We saw him praying while wearing two matching, paired sandals.""**"
"2251","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed while there was a trace of mud on his sandals, and he was wearing a cloak; he carefully took care to prevent the mud from touching his cloak.""**"
"2252","Al-Hirmās ibn Ziyād al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying in his sandals.""**"
"2253","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer and took off his sandals, so we took off our sandals too. When he concluded the prayer, he asked: 'Why did you take off your sandals?' They replied: 'We saw you take them off, so we took ours off.' He said:"" > **""I merely grew uncomfortable and weary of them.""**"
"2254","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to pray while wearing his sandals.""**"
"2255","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays and removes his sandals, let him not place them to his right side lest he sin, nor behind him lest his companion behind him sin on account of them; rather, let him place them between his knees.""**"
"2256","Aws ibn Aws رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I stayed with the Prophet ﷺ for half a month, and I saw him praying wearing two matched sandals, and I saw him spit to his right and to his left.""**"
"2257","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drinking standing and sitting, praying shod and barefoot, and spitting to his right and to his left.""**"
"2258","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was leading the people in prayer when he removed his sandals. When the people sensed this, they removed their sandals too. When he finished his prayer, he turned to the people and said:"" > **""The angel came to me and informed me that there was filth on my sandals. Therefore, when one of you comes to the mosque, let him turn over his sandals and inspect them; if he sees any filth upon them, let him wipe them clean and then pray in them.""**"
"2259","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took off his sandals, so those behind him took off their sandals as well. He asked: 'What prompted you to take off your sandals?' They replied: 'We saw you take yours off, so we took ours off.' He said:"" > **""Indeed, Jibrīl informed me that there was impurity on them, so I took them off for that reason. Therefore, do not remove your sandals.""**"
"2260","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Prophet ﷺ never removed his sandals during prayer except once, whereupon the congregation also removed their sandals. The Prophet ﷺ asked: 'Why did you remove your sandals?' They replied: 'We saw you remove yours, so we removed ours.' He said:"" > **""Indeed, Jibrīl عليه السلام informed me that there was filth upon them.""**"
"2261","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer and removed his sandals while in prayer. The row directly behind him removed their sandals, and the row behind them also removed their sandals. When the Prophet ﷺ finished, he asked: 'Why did you remove your sandals?' They said: 'You removed yours, O Messenger of Allah, so the row behind you removed theirs, and we followed suit.' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Jibrīl عليه السلام came to me and mentioned that there was filth on my sandals, so I took them off. Therefore, pray in your sandals!""**"
"2262","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray upon a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah) and prostrate upon it.""**"
"2263","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray on a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah). He once said:"" > **""O ʿĀʾishah, put away your mat, for I fear that it might tempt or confuse the people.""**"
"2264","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray upon a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah).""**"
"2265","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ had a reed mat (ḥaṣīr) and a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah) upon which he used to pray.""**"
"2266","Umm Ḥabībah رضي الله عنها, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to pray upon a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah).""**"
"2267","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying and prostrating upon his garment.""**"
"2268","Jābir [ibn ʿAbdillāh] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to pray upon a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah).""**"
"2269","Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray upon a small palm-leaf mat (khumrah), and in another narration: and he would prostrate upon it.""**"
"2270","It is narrated that Shurayḥ asked ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها: ""Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ use to pray on a reed mat (*ḥaṣīr*), for I have heard in the Book of Allah: {And We have made Hell a prison (*ḥaṣīrā*) for the disbelievers} [al-Isrāʾ: 8]?"" She replied: > **""He did not use to pray upon it.""**"
"2271","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he would not place anything beneath his feet, except that one day it rained upon us, so he placed a leather mat (*niṭʿ*) beneath his feet.""**"
"2272","Abū ʿUbaydah narrated concerning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه: > **""Ibn Masʿūd would not pray—or would not prostrate—except directly upon the earth.""**"
"2273","It is narrated from Ibrāhīm [al-Nakhaʿī] that: > **""He used to stand upon the *baradā* and prostrate upon the earth.""** (They asked: ""What is the *baradā*?"" He replied: ""The reed mat."")"
"2274","Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about praying while carrying a bow and a quiver (*qarn*). He ﷺ said:"" > **""Pray with the bow, but cast aside the quiver.""** (Meaning: the quiver of arrows.)"
"2275","Ibn Sīrīn narrated: > **""Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه slaughtered a camel and became smeared with its blood and dung. When the call to begin prayer was proclaimed, he prayed and did not perform a new ablution.""**"
"2276","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray while carrying the daughter of Abū al-ʿĀṣ (Umāmah) upon his shoulder; whenever he bowed, he set her down, and whenever he stood up, he lifted her up.""**"
"2277","Sabrah ibn Maʿbad رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""An arrow serves as a sutrah for a man in his prayer; so when one of you prays, let him screen himself with an arrow.""**"
"2278","Saʿd al-Qaraẓ رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Negus sent three short javelins (*ʿanazāt*) to the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ kept one for himself, gave one to ʿAlī رضي الله عنه, and gave one to ʿUmar رضي الله عنه. Bilāl رضي الله عنه used to walk carrying it before him on the two ʿĪds, planting it firmly in the ground before him so that he ﷺ would pray facing toward it.""**"
"2279","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""A short spear (*ʿanazah*) used to be planted in the ground for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ so that he would pray facing toward it—and I believe he said: while women in camel-litters passed in front of him [beyond the sutrah].""**"
"2280","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a spear carried before him as he walked, and whenever he prayed, it was planted in the ground in front of him.""**"
"2281","Khabbāb رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I used to plant the short spear (*al-ʿanazah*) for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ [to serve as his sutrah].""**"
"2282","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""Before the mosque was built, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray facing a wooden post. When the mosque was built, a niche (*miḥrāb*) was constructed for him and he stepped forward to it; thereupon, the wooden post yearned and moaned with the groaning of a camel. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ placed his hand upon it, and it calmed down.""**"
"2283","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We were on a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the call to begin prayer was proclaimed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ faced the hump of a camel and stood praying towards it.""**"
"2284","Al-Miqdām رضي الله عنه narrated that Abū al-Dardāʾ and ʿUbādah [ibn al-Ṣāmit] رضي الله عنهما sat with al-Ḥārith ibn Muʿāwiyah. Abū al-Dardāʾ said: ""Which of you remembers when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer facing a camel from the spoils of war? When he completed the prayer, he plucked a single tuft of fur from the camel and said:"" > **""Nothing from what Allah has bestowed upon you of spoils is lawful for me, not even the likes of this tuft of fur, except for the fifth (khumus)—and the fifth is returned to you.""**"
"2285","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays facing a sutrah, let him draw close to it so that Satan does not interrupt his prayer.""** (In the narration of al-Ṭabarānī: ""Let him draw close to it so that Satan does not pass between him and it."")"
"2286","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays facing a sutrah, let him draw close to it so that Satan does not sever his prayer.""**"
"2287","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Draw close to the qiblah [and the sutrah].""**"
"2288","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays facing a sutrah, let him draw close to it so that Satan does not interrupt his prayer.""**"
"2289","It is narrated that Sahl ibn al-Ḥanẓaliyyah رضي الله عنه passed by a man praying far back from the qiblah wall. Sahl said to him: > **""Step forward to your place of prayer so that Satan does not sever your prayer; and I do not narrate to you except what I heard directly from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2290","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""None of you should ever pray while there is a wide gap—meaning an open space—between him and the qiblah [or sutrah].""**"
"2291","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Nothing interrupts the prayer of a Muslim except a donkey, a disbeliever, a dog, and a woman.""** (ʿĀʾishah responded: ""O Messenger of Allah! You have equated us with wretched beasts!"")"
"2292","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The prayer is severed by a dog, a donkey, and a woman.""**"
"2293","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAmr al-Ghifārī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer is severed by: the dog, the donkey, and the woman.""**"
"2294","ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd and Abū Bashīr al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was leading them in prayer one day when a woman was passing through the open ground (*al-Baṭḥāʾ*). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ gestured to her to step back, so she returned and waited until he finished praying, and only then did she pass.""**"
"2295","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at the upper part of the valley intending to pray, he stood up and we stood with him. Suddenly, a donkey emerged towards us from the mountain pass of Abū Dubb—the mountain pass of Abū Mūsā. The Prophet ﷺ paused and did not utter the takbīr, and Yaʿqūb ibn Zamʿah ran towards it until he turned it back.""**"
"2296","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, a sheep came rushing to pass in front of him. He hurried to forestall it until he pressed his stomach against the wall.""**"
"2297","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ hastened to block a cat from passing in front of him during the prayer.""**"
"2298","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying when a Bedouin passed by with a milking camel of his. The Prophet ﷺ gestured to him, but he did not understand. Thereupon, ʿUmar called out to him: 'O Bedouin, stay back!' When the Prophet ﷺ completed the prayer with the salam, he asked: 'Who was the speaker?' They replied: 'ʿUmar.' He ﷺ remarked:"" > **""This man possesses no understanding!""** (Al-Haythamī noted: ""This remark was said concerning the Bedouin, not concerning ʿUmar, according to what I understand, and Allah knows best."")"
"2299","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""We prayed an obligatory prayer with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he clasped his hand during the prayer. When he finished the prayer, we asked: 'O Messenger of Allah, has something new been ordained regarding the prayer?' He ﷺ replied:"" > **""No, except that Satan intended to pass in front of me, so I choked him until I felt the coolness of his tongue upon my hand. By Allah, had it not been for the supplication that my brother Sulaymān made before me, he would have been tied to one of the pillars of the mosque for the children of the people of Madīnah to circle around him.""**"
"2300","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""When anyone intends to pass in front of you while you are praying, do not let him pass, for indeed he takes away half of your prayer.""**"
"2301","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The one who deliberately passes in front of a person while he is praying will wish on the Day of Resurrection that he were a withered tree.""**"
"2302","Busr ibn Saʿīd narrated: ""Abū Juhaym sent me to Zayd ibn Khālid رضي الله عنه to ask him concerning the one who passes in front of a praying person. He replied: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:'"" > **""If the one who passes in front of a praying person knew what burden of sin was upon him, standing for forty autumns would be better for him than passing in front of him.""**"
"2303","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""If any of you is able to ensure that no one passes in front of him, let him do so; for indeed, the one who passes in front of a praying person incurs more deficiency than the one passed before.""**"
"2304","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man praying facing directly towards another man, so he commanded him to repeat the prayer. The man said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I prayed while you were looking at me!'""**"
"2305","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I have been prohibited from praying behind those who are conversing or those who are sleeping.""**"
"2306","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The sutrah of the imam is the sutrah of whoever is behind him.""**"
"2307","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying when a sheep attempted to pass in front of him. He hurried to ward it off until he pinned it against the wall. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Nothing cuts off the prayer, but ward off whatever passes before you to the best of your ability.""**"
"2308","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Nothing severs the prayer.""**"
"2309","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform voluntary night prayers while ʿĀʾishah lay stretched horizontally between him and the qiblah during his standing of the night.""**"
"2310","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ prayed while she was lying horizontally across before him, and he ﷺ said: > **""Are they not your mothers, your sisters, and your paternal aunts?""**"
"2311","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""My bed used to be spread opposite the place of prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he would pray while I was directly in front of him.""**"
"2312","Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf narrated: ""I was praying when a man tried to pass in front of me, so I tried to prevent him, but he refused. I then asked ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه about this, and he said:"" > **""It does not harm you, O son of my brother.""**"
"2313","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""A sheep passed in front of the Prophet ﷺ while he was in prayer, between him and the qiblah, and it did not cut off his prayer.""**"
"2314","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A cat does not interrupt the prayer; it is only part of the household furnishings.""**"
"2315","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prayed in an open expanse with nothing in front of him [as a sutrah].""**"
"2316","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""A boy from Banū Hāshim and I arrived riding upon a donkey, and we passed in front of the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying. We dismounted from it and left the donkey grazing on the vegetation of the earth—or he said: the herbs of the earth—and then we joined him in prayer."" A man asked: ""Was there a short spear (*ʿanazah*) before him?"" Ibn ʿAbbās replied: > **""No.""**"
"2317","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed while men and women were circumambulating in front of him without a sutrah, near the Black Stone.""**"
"2318","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The people should be led in prayer by the one among them who is most well-versed in the Qur'an.""**"
"2319","ʿAmr ibn Salamah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""Riders used to come to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and narrate to us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Let the one who knows the most Qur'an among you lead you in prayer.""**"
"2320","ʿAmr ibn Salamah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I went with my father to the Prophet ﷺ announcing the Islam of his people. Among the instructions he gave us was:"" > **""Let the one who knows the most Qur'an among you lead you in prayer.""** (ʿAmr added: ""I was the one who knew the most Qur'an among them, so they put me forward to lead them."")"
"2321","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The people are to be led in prayer by the one among them who is most well-versed in the Book of Allah.""**"
"2322","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you travel, let the one among you who knows the most Qur'an lead you in prayer, even if he is the youngest of you; and when he leads you, he is your leader (*amīr*).""**"
"2323","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever leads a people in prayer while among them is someone who is more well-versed in the Book of Allah than him will remain in abasement until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2324","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Form your rows, and let the most virtuous among you step forward to lead you in prayer; for indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, chooses messengers from among the angels and from among humankind.""**"
"2325","Marthad ibn Abī Marthad al-Ghanawī رضي الله عنه—who was a veteran of Badr—reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If it pleases you that your prayer be accepted, then let the best among you lead you; for indeed, they are your delegation in what is between you and your Lord, the Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"2326","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""Sālim, the freed slave of Abū Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه, used to lead the Emigrants (*Muhājirūn*) when they arrived in Madīnah—and among them was ʿUmar رضي الله عنه and others of the Emigrants—because he was the one among them who knew the most Qur'an.""**"
"2327","Qays ibn Zuhayr narrated: ""I went with Ḥanẓalah ibn al-Rabīʿ to the mosque of Furāt ibn Ḥayyān رضي الله عنهم. When the time for prayer arrived, Furāt said to Ḥanẓalah: 'Step forward.' Ḥanẓalah said: 'I will not step forward before you when you are older than me in years, earlier than me in migration, and the mosque is your mosque!' Furāt replied: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say something concerning you, so I will never step forward before you.' Ḥanẓalah asked: 'Were you present the day I came to him on the Day of al-Ṭāʾif and he sent me as a scout?' He said: 'Yes.' So Ḥanẓalah stepped forward and led them in prayer. Furāt then said: 'O my son, hasten; I only put this man forward because the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him as a scout to al-Ṭāʾif. When he returned and informed him of the news, he ﷺ said: ""You have spoken the truth; return to your dwelling, for you have stayed awake all night."" And when he turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to us:'"" > **""Follow this man and those like him in prayer.""**"
"2328","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ appointed Ibn Umm Maktūm رضي الله عنه as his deputy over Madīnah to lead the people in prayer.""** (In the narration of al-Ṭabarānī: ""Ibn Umm Maktūm was appointed as deputy over Madīnah twice to lead the people in prayer."")"
"2329","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed Ibn Umm Maktūm رضي الله عنه as his deputy over Madīnah to lead the people in prayer.""**"
"2330","ʿAbdullāh ibn Buḥaynah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""Whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ travelled, he appointed Ibn Umm Maktūm رضي الله عنه as his deputy over Madīnah; he would call the adhān, call the iqāmah, and lead them in prayer.""**"
"2331","It is narrated concerning ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmayr, the imam of Banū Khaṭmah رضي الله عنه: > **""He was the imam of Banū Khaṭmah during the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was blind, and he participated in battle alongside him ﷺ while he was blind.""**"
"2332","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥanẓalah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""We were in the house of Qays ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنه alongside a group of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. We said to him: 'Step forward [and lead].' He replied: 'I would never do so.' Then ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥanẓalah said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:'"" > **""A man has the greatest right to the head of his own mattress, the greatest right to the front of his riding animal, and the greatest right to lead the prayer in his own home.""** (Thereupon, Qays commanded a freed slave of his, who stepped forward and led the prayer.)"
"2333","Ibrāhīm narrated: ""ʿAbdullāh [ibn Masʿūd] visited Abū Mūsā [al-Ashʿarī] رضي الله عنهما and engaged in conversation with him. When the time for prayer arrived and the iqāmah was proclaimed, Abū Mūsā stepped back. ʿAbdullāh said to him: 'O Abū Mūsā, you surely know that it is part of the Sunnah for the master of the house to step forward.' But Abū Mūsā declined until a freed slave belonging to one of them stepped forward and led."" > **""You surely know that it is part of the Sunnah for the master of the house to step forward [to lead the prayer].""**"
"2334","ʿAlqamah narrated that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd came to Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنهما at his house. When the prayer was due, Abū Mūsā said: ""Step forward, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, for you are older in age and more knowledgeable."" ʿAbdullāh replied: ""Rather, you step forward, for we have come to you in your home and your place of prayer, so you have more right."" Abū Mūsā stepped forward and took off his sandals. When he completed the prayer with the salam, ʿAbdullāh said to him: > **""What made you take off your sandals? Are you in the sacred valley [of Ṭuwā]?!""**"
"2335","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever leads a people in prayer, let him fear Allah and know that he is a guarantor who will be held accountable for what he guaranteed. If he performs well, he shall receive a reward equal to the reward of everyone who prayed behind him, without anything being diminished from their rewards; and whatever shortcoming occurs, it shall be upon him alone.""**"
"2336","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The imam is a guarantor; so whatever he does, do likewise.""**"
"2337","An elder from the tribe of Ṭayyiʾ narrated: ""Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه passed by a mosque of ours. A man from among them stepped forward [to lead] and recited the Opening of the Book (al-Fātiḥah), and then recited: 'We make pilgrimage to the House of our Lord, and we pay the debt, and it is like sandgrouse descending.' ʿAbdullāh said: '{We never heard of this in the latest religion; this is nothing but a fabrication}' [Ṣād: 7]; and ʿAbdullāh turned away and left."" > **""We never heard of this in the latest religion; this is nothing but a fabrication!""**"
"2338","ʿUmar al-Anṣārī narrated: ""I asked Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه about praying behind a Qadarī (one who denies divine decree). He replied:"" > **""Do not pray behind him; as for me, had I prayed behind him, I would certainly repeat my prayer.""**"
"2339","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Obey every ruler, pray behind every imam, and never revile any of my Companions.""**"
"2340","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray the funeral prayer over whoever says: 'There is no god but Allah,' and pray behind whoever says: 'There is no god but Allah.'""**"
"2341","It is narrated concerning ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه: > **""He disliked leading the people in prayer from an elevated place.""**"
"2342","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day among a group of his Companions. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned to them and said: ""O people, do you not know that I am the Messenger of Allah to you?"" They replied: ""Yes, we bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah."" He asked: ""Do you not know that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed in His Book: {Whoever obeys the Messenger has obeyed Allah}?"" They replied: ""Yes, we bear witness that whoever obeys you has obeyed Allah, and that obeying you is part of obeying Allah."" He ﷺ said: > **""Verily, it is part of obedience to Allah that you obey me, and it is part of obedience to me that you obey your imams. Obey your imams: if they pray sitting, then pray sitting!""**"
"2343","Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to the people: > **""If the imam prays sitting, then pray sitting.""** (Al-Qāsim said: ""The people were amazed at the truthful precision of Muʿāwiyah."")"
"2344","It is narrated that Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh رضي الله عنه led a people in prayer. When he concluded the prayer, he said: ""I forgot to seek your permission before stepping forward; are you pleased with my leading the prayer?"" They replied: ""Yes, and who could possibly dislike that, O Disciple of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?!"" He said: ""Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:"" > **""Whichever man leads a people in prayer while they dislike him, his prayer does not rise past his ears.""**"
"2345","It is reported from Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه that he used to differ from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz in prayer, so ʿUmar said to him: ""What carries you to do this?"" He replied: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying a certain prayer; whenever you agree with it, I pray with you, and whenever you differ from it, I pray and return to my family."" > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying a certain prayer; whenever you agree with it, I pray with you, and whenever you differ from it, I pray and return to my family.""**"
"2346","It is reported from Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه that he used to differ from Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam in his prayer, so Marwān said to him: ""What carries you to do this?"" He said: ""I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying a certain prayer; if you agree with him, I agree with you, and if you differ from him, I pray and return to my family."" > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying a certain prayer; if you agree with him, I agree with you, and if you differ from him, I pray and return to my family.""**"
"2347","Abū ʿAlī al-Miṣrī said: ""We travelled with ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه, and the time of Ṣalāh came upon us, so we wanted him to step forward and lead us. He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:'"" > **""Whoever leads a people in prayer, if he completes it, then complete reward is for him and complete reward is for them; and if he does not complete it, then complete reward is for them and the sin is upon him.""**"
"2348","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه said: ""One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer and then left. Then he came back with water dripping from his head, and he led us in prayer. Then he said:"" > **""I had just now led you in prayer while I was junub, so whoever is afflicted with the like of what afflicted me, or finds a disturbance in his stomach, let him do the like of what I did.""**"
"2349","In another narration from him: ""While we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying, he suddenly left while we remained standing"" — then he mentioned the like of it, except that in al-Ṭabarānī's wording: > **""Let him leave and perform ghusl, then let him come and resume his prayer from the beginning.""**"
"2350","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ pronounced the takbīr for them in the Fajr prayer, then he gestured to them, then he went away. He returned with his head dripping, and he led them in prayer. Then he said: > **""I am only a human being like you; indeed, I was junub and I forgot.""**"
"2351","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered into his prayer, and they pronounced takbīr along with him. He then signalled to the people to remain as they were, and they stayed standing continuously until the Prophet of Allah ﷺ came back to them, having performed ghusl, with water dripping from his head. > **""Remain as you are.""**"
"2352","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""When the imām falters, do not prompt him, for indeed that is speech.""**"
"2353","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led the people in prayer and omitted a verse, then he said: > **""Which of you will correct me in anything of my recitation?""** Ubayy said: ""I will, O Messenger of Allah; you omitted such-and-such a verse."" So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I knew that if anyone would correct me, it would be you.""**"
"2354","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed Fajr and omitted a verse. When he had prayed, he said: > **""Is Ubayy ibn Kaʿb among the people?""** Ubayy said: ""O Messenger of Allah, has such-and-such a verse been abrogated, or were you made to forget it?"" He said: > **""I forgot it.""**"
"2355","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه said: ""One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer and dropped part of a sūrah from the Qurʾān. When he finished his prayer, Ubayy said: 'O Messenger of Allah, has such-and-such a verse been abrogated?' He said: 'No.' He said:"" > **""Then why did you not prompt me with it?""**"
"2356","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ hesitated over a verse in the Fajr prayer. When he finished the prayer, he looked at the faces of the people and said:"" > **""Did Ubayy ibn Kaʿb not pray with you?""** They said: ""No."" So the people thought he had asked about him only so that he could prompt him."
"2357","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer and became confused in it. When he finished, he said to Ubayy ibn Kaʿb: > **""Did you pray with us?""** He said: ""Yes."" He said: > **""Then what prevented you from prompting me?""**"
"2358","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in Fajr and omitted a verse. Ubayy then came, having missed part of the prayer. When he finished, he said: 'O Messenger of Allah, has this verse been abrogated, or were you made to forget it?' He said:"" > **""No, rather I was made to forget it.""**"
"2359","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: ""One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led his Companions in prayer. When he finished, he said:"" > **""What do you think of my prayer?""** They said: ""How excellent you prayed!"" He said: > **""I forgot a verse, and part of the excellence of a man's prayer is that he guards the recitation of the imām.""**"
"2360","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما reported that ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ was afflicted with janābah while he was commander of the army, so he abandoned ghusl because he said: ""If I perform ghusl, I shall die of cold."" He led those with him while junub. When he came to the Prophet ﷺ, he informed him of what he had done and told him of his excuse, so he approved it and remained silent."
"2361","Nāfiʿ ibn Sarjis said: ""We visited Abū Wāqid al-Kindī — and in one narration, Abū Wāqid al-Badrī — during his illness in which he died. He said:"" > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the briefest of people in prayer when leading people, and the longest of people in prayer when praying by himself.""**"
"2362","Mālik ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه said: > **""I went on a military expedition with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and I never prayed behind an imām whose prayer was briefer than his, along with completeness in rukūʿ and sujūd.""**"
"2363","Jābir رضي الله عنه reported: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the most lightening of people in prayer.""**"
"2364","From him in another narration: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was the briefest of people in prayer, along with completeness.""**"
"2365","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""One prostration of these people is longer than three prostrations of the Prophet ﷺ.""**"
"2366","Abū Khālid al-Wālibī said: ""I said to Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه: 'Was this how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to lead you in prayer?' He said: 'What is it you object to in my prayer?' I said: 'I only wanted to ask about that.' He said: 'Yes, and briefer. His standing was only as long as it takes the muʾadhdhin to come down from the minaret and reach the row.'"" > **""Yes, and briefer. His standing was only as long as it takes the muʾadhdhin to come down from the minaret and reach the row.""**"
"2367","In another narration from him: ""I saw Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه pray a prayer in which he was brief."""
"2368","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: > **""We used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a prayer so brief that if one of you prayed it today, you would find fault with him for it.""**"
"2369","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever leads us, let him complete the rukūʿ and the sujūd, for among us are the weak, the aged sick, the wayfarer, and the one with a need. This is how we used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2370","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: ""Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه used to lead his people in prayer, when Ḥarām entered while intending to water his palm trees. He entered the mosque to pray with the people, but when he saw Muʿādh prolonging, he shortened his own prayer and caught up with his palms to water them. When Muʿādh finished the prayer, it was said to him that Ḥarām had entered the mosque and, seeing him prolong, had shortened his prayer and gone to water his palms. He said: 'He is a hypocrite! Did he hasten away from his prayer for the sake of watering palms!' Ḥarām then came to the Prophet ﷺ, with Muʿādh in his presence, and said: 'O Prophet of Allah, I intended to water my palms, so I entered the mosque to pray with the people, but when he prolonged, I shortened and caught up with my palms to water them, yet he claims I am a hypocrite.' The Prophet ﷺ turned to Muʿādh and said:"" > **""Are you a trial, are you? Do not prolong for them. Recite 'Glorify the Name of your Lord' [al-Aʿlā], 'By the sun and its brightness' [al-Shams], and the like of them.""**"
"2371","Muʿādh ibn Rifāʿah reported from a man of Banū Salamah called Sulaym, who came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Muʿādh ibn Jabal comes to us after we have slept, for we work by day, and he calls for prayer, so we come out to him, but he prolongs for us."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Muʿādh ibn Jabal, do not be a trial. Either pray with me, or lighten for your people.""** Then he said: > **""O Sulaym, what do you have of the Qurʾān?""** He said: ""I ask Allah for Paradise and seek refuge with Him from the Fire; by Allah, I cannot do your humming nor the humming of Muʿādh well."" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""And is my humming and the humming of Muʿādh anything but that we ask Allah for Paradise and seek refuge with Him from the Fire?""** Sulaym said: ""You shall see tomorrow when the people meet, if Allah wills."" The people were then preparing for Uḥud, so he went out and was among the martyrs."
"2372","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما said: ""Ubayy used to lead the people of Qubāʾ in prayer, and he began a long sūrah. A youth of the Anṣār who had joined him in prayer, when he heard him begin a long sūrah, broke off from his prayer, for he wanted to treat a thirsty camel that gave him water. When Ubayy finished, the people said to him: 'So-and-so broke off from the prayer,' so Ubayy became angry and came to the Prophet ﷺ complaining of the youth. The youth also came complaining of him. The Prophet ﷺ became angry until anger was seen in his face, then he said:"" > **""Indeed, among you are those who repel. When you pray, be brief, for behind you are the weak, the aged, the sick, and the one with a need.""**"
"2373","In another narration from him: ""When Ubayy finished, he was told of that. Ubayy knew that the youth would complain to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and the youth was brought forward complaining of Ubayy. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Indeed, among you are those who repel. When you pray, then shorten""** — Abū Yaḥyā was uncertain whether he said ""awḥirū"" or ""awjizū"" — then he mentioned the ḥadīth similarly."
"2374","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما said: ""Ḥazm ibn Abī Kaʿb ibn Abī al-Qayn passed by Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه while he was leading his people in the night prayer (ʿatamah), and he had begun a long sūrah. Ḥazm had a thirsty camel with him, so he stepped back and prayed alone, praying well, then he went to his camel. He then came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and informed him, saying: 'O Messenger of Allah, it was more proper than what he did.' So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Do not ever be a trial — he said it three times — for behind you stand the aged, the weak, the one with a need, and the sick.""**"
"2375","Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported from his father: > **""I never prayed behind anyone whose prayer was lighter, along with completeness, than the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2376","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما said: > **""I never prayed behind anyone after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whose prayer was lighter, along with completeness, than the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2377","ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me when he sent me to Thaqīf:"" > **""Be brief in prayer, O ʿUthmān, and lead the people according to the weakest among them, for among them are the weak, the one with a need, the pregnant, and the nursing mother.""**"
"2378","Ibrāhīm ibn Yazīd al-Taymī said: ""My father had abandoned praying with us, so I said to him: 'O my father, why have you abandoned praying with us?' He said: 'You lighten too much.' I said: 'Then what of the saying of the Prophet ﷺ:'"" > **""Indeed, among you are the weak, the aged, and the one with a need?""** ""He said: 'I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه saying that, yet he used to remain in rukūʿ and sujūd three times as long as you pray.'"""
"2379","Abū Mālik al-Ashjaʿī reported from his father: ""I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and behind Abū Bakr, and behind ʿUmar, and behind ʿUthmān, and behind ʿAlī رضي الله عنهم, and none of them prayed lighter, along with completeness, than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" > **""None of them prayed lighter, along with completeness, than the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2380","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim رضي الله عنه went out to their gathering, and the prayer was established. Their imām stepped forward and prolonged the prayer in the sitting, so when he finished, ʿAdī said: ""Whoever among you leads us, let him complete the rukūʿ and the sujūd, for behind him are the young, the old, the sick, the wayfarer, and the one with a need."" When the next prayer came, ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim stepped forward; he completed the rukūʿ and the sujūd but was brief in the prayer. When he finished, he said: > **""This is how we used to pray behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2381","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Be brief in prayer, for behind you are the weak, the aged, and the one with a need.""**"
"2382","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Two rakʿahs of the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ are lighter than one rakʿah of your prayer.""**"
"2383","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ heard the voice of a child during prayer, so he shortened it."
"2384","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Indeed, I hear the voice of a child while I am in prayer, so I shorten it, fearing that his mother may be put to trial.""**"
"2385","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in Fajr with the two shortest sūrahs of the Qurʾān. When he finished his prayer, he turned to us with his face and said:"" > **""I hastened — or hurried — only so that the mother of the child may be free for her child.""** And he had heard the voice of the child."
"2386","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما reported from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, last night I did a deed!"" He said: > **""What is it?""** He said: ""There were women with me in the house who said: 'You recite and we do not recite, so lead us in prayer.' So I led them for eight rakʿahs and the witr."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ remained silent, and they took his silence as approval."
"2387","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما said: ""Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, something happened with me last night' — meaning in Ramaḍān. He said:"" > **""And what was that, O Ubayy?""** ""He said: 'There were women in my house who said: We do not recite the Qurʾān, so we shall pray with your prayer.' So I led them for eight rakʿahs and performed the witr."" This was thus an approved practice, for he did not say anything."
"2388","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه was once with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when the Prophet ﷺ went away for his need. The time of prayer overtook them, so ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf stepped forward and led them. The Prophet ﷺ then came and prayed with the people behind him. When he gave the salām, he said: > **""You have done well""** — or he said — > **""You have done right.""**"
"2389","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: ""When Bilāl رضي الله عنه wanted to proclaim the iqāmah for prayer, he would say:"" > **""Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Prayer, may Allah have mercy on you.""**"
"2390","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""When the prayer is established, do not stand until you see me.""**"
"2391","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه said: The iqāmah for prayer was proclaimed, then ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه stepped forward to a pillar in the mosque and prayed two rakʿahs, then he entered into the prayer."
"2392","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mūsā said: Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه came to us while the imām was praying the dawn prayer, so he prayed two rakʿahs by a pillar, for he had not yet prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr."
"2393","Abū Isḥāq reported that al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah sent to Ḥudhayfah and Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنهما asking them about the prayer on the day of ʿĪd, when the iqāmah for the Fajr prayer was proclaimed, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2394","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man praying the two rakʿahs of the morning when the muʾadhdhin had begun the iqāmah, so the Prophet ﷺ nudged his shoulder and said: > **""Could this not have been before that?""**"
"2395","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: ""The dawn prayer was established, so I stood up to pray the two rakʿahs before dawn, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took my hand and pulled me, saying:"" > **""Would you pray the dawn prayer as four rakʿahs?""**"
"2396","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:"" > **""There is no prayer for one who enters the mosque while the imām is standing in prayer; let him not pray alone by himself, but let him enter into the prayer with the imām.""**"
"2397","Anas رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out when the prayer had been established, and he saw people praying the two rakʿahs of Fajr, so he said:"" > **""Two prayers together?!""** And he forbade that they be prayed once the prayer has been established."
"2398","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: ""The Prophet ﷺ entered while Bilāl was proclaiming the iqāmah for prayer, and he saw a man praying the two rakʿahs of Fajr, so he said to him:"" > **""Two prayers together?!""**"
"2399","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه bowed before reaching the row, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""May Allah increase you in eagerness, but do not repeat it. Pray what you catch, and make up what preceded you.""**"
"2400","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said concerning one who misses part of the prayer with the imām: > **""Let him make what he catches with the imām the end of his prayer.""**"
"2401","It is reported from Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that Jundub and Masrūq each caught one rakʿah — meaning of the Maghrib prayer. Jundub recited while Masrūq did not recite behind the imām. When the imām gave the salām, they stood to make up what they had missed: Masrūq sat in the second and the third, while Jundub stood in the second and did not sit. When they finished, they discussed that, then they came to Ibn Masʿūd, who said: > **""Each has done correctly — or each has done well — but do as Masrūq does.""**"
"2402","ʿAlī and Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Whoever does not catch the rakʿah shall not count the prostration.""**"
"2403","Qatādah reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه caught a people sitting at the end of their prayer, so he said: > **""You have caught it, if Allah wills.""**"
"2404","Zayd ibn Wahb said: ""Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه and I entered the mosque while the imām was bowing, so we bowed and then walked forward until we stood level with the row. When the imām finished, I stood to make up, but he said:"" > **""You have already caught it.""**"
"2405","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""When one of you bows and then walks to the row, if he enters the row before they raise their heads, then he shall count it; but if they raise their heads before he reaches the row, then he shall not count it.""**"
"2406","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه said: ""A man prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and he would bow before he bowed and rise before he rose. When the Prophet ﷺ finished the prayer, he said:"" > **""Who did this?""** ""He said: 'I did, O Messenger of Allah. I wanted to know whether you would know that or not.' He said:"" > **""Beware of the defective prayer. When the imām bows, then bow, and when he rises, then rise.""**"
"2407","Ibn Masʿadah, the commander of the army, رضي الله عنه said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying:"" > **""I have become heavy-bodied, so whoever misses my rukūʿ will catch it up in the slowness of my standing.""**"
"2408","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: > **""One of us would keep his back straight in prayer behind the Prophet ﷺ until the Prophet ﷺ had settled into prostration — or he said: reached the ground — and only then would he prostrate.""**"
"2409","Anas رضي الله عنه reported: > **""When the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, none of us would prostrate until he saw him prostrate.""**"
"2410","al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنهما said: ""When we prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ and he said, 'Allah hears the one who praises Him,' none of us would bend his back until he saw the Prophet ﷺ prostrate."" > **""None of us would bend his back until he saw the Prophet ﷺ prostrate.""**"
"2411","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""I have become heavy-bodied, so do not race me in standing in prayer, nor in rukūʿ, nor in sujūd.""**"
"2412","Samurah رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Do not precede your imām in rukūʿ, for you will catch him up in what he precedes you in.""**"
"2413","Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you stand in prayer, do not precede your reciter in rukūʿ, sujūd, and standing; rather, let your reciter precede you. You will catch up with whatever he precedes you in, when he raises his head in rukūʿ, sujūd, and standing before you, for you will then catch up with your reciter in it.""**"
"2414","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""When the imām pronounces takbīr, then pronounce takbīr; when he bows, then bow; when he prostrates, then prostrate; when he raises his head from rukūʿ, then raise; and when he prays sitting, then pray sitting.""**"
"2415","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The one who lowers and raises before the imām — his forelock is in the hand of a devil.""**"
"2416","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said:"" > **""Does none of you feel secure, when he raises his head before the imām, that Allah may turn his head into the head of a dog?""**"
"2417","From Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه with him also: > **""The one who raises his head before the imām and lowers it.""**"
"2418","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""The imām is appointed only to be followed. When he bows, then bow; when he rises, then rise. Do not precede him when he bows, nor when he rises, nor when he prostrates. If your only concern is to catch up with what he precedes you in, then he prostrates before you and rises before you, so you will catch up with that.""**"
"2419","Abū al-Aḥwaṣ reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""When you are behind the imām, do not bow until he bows, nor prostrate until he prostrates, nor raise your head before him. When the imām has finished and has neither stood nor turned away, and you have a need, then go and leave him, for your prayer is complete.""**"
"2420","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""What makes the one who raises his head before the Imam feel secure that his head will not be turned into the head of a dog? People must either desist from looking up toward the sky [during prayer] or their sight will surely be snatched away.""**"
"2421","It is narrated that ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه was leading the people in prayer here, and the people were lowering their heads [into prostration] before he lowered his head, and raising their heads before he raised his head. When he finished the prayer, he turned to them and said: > **""O people! Why do you appoint an Imam and take someone as a leader? I prayed with you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without falling short in any way.""**"
"2422","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""He used to pray with the Prophet ﷺ, and then he would go to his people and lead them in prayer.""**"
"2423","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""None of you should come to the prayer while suppressing urine [or stool], nor should any of you lead people in prayer and single out himself for supplication to their exclusion; for if he does so, he has betrayed them.""**"
"2424","Muʿādh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: > **""Indeed, the one who laughs during prayer, the one who looks around, and the one who cracks his fingers are all in the same position [of deficiency].""**"
"2425","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""My beloved friend ﷺ advised me with three things and forbade me from three things: > **""He forbade me from pecking [in prostration] like the pecking of a rooster, squatting [in sitting] like the squatting of a dog, and glancing around [in prayer] like the glancing of a fox.""**"
"2426","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When a man stands up in prayer, Allah turns toward him with His Face. If he looks away, Allah says: 'O son of Adam, toward whom are you turning? Toward someone who is better for you than Me? Turn toward Me!' If he looks away a second time, He says the same. But if he looks away a third time, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, turns His Face away from him.""**"
"2427","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When a servant stands for prayer—I think he said—he is before the Most Merciful, Blessed and Exalted. If he turns away, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, says: 'Toward whom are you turning? Toward someone better than Me? Turn toward Me, O son of Adam, for I am better than that toward which you are turning!'""**"
"2428","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When one of you stands for prayer, let him attend fully to it until he finishes it. Beware of looking around in prayer, for indeed one of you is in intimate converse with his Lord as long as he is in prayer.""**"
"2429","ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not look around during your prayer, for indeed there is no prayer for the one who looks around.""**"
"2430","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to glance right and left during prayer, until Allah revealed: {Successful indeed are the believers, those who are humble and reverent in their prayers} [al-Muʾminūn: 1–2]. Thereafter, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ maintained deep reverence (*khushūʿ*) and would not glance to the right or to the left.""**"
"2431","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever performs ablution and perfects his ablution, then prays two units of prayer and supplicates to his Lord, his supplication will surely be answered, whether granted sooner or later. Beware of looking around in prayer, for indeed there is no prayer for the one who looks around. If you are overcome in voluntary prayer, do not let yourselves be overcome in the obligatory prayer.""**"
"2432","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever stands in prayer and turns his gaze around, Allah rejects his prayer back upon him.""**"
"2433","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Allah remains turned toward the servant with His Face as long as he does not look around or break his ablution.""**"
"2434","Khawwāt ibn Jubayr رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I was praying when a man behind me said: > **""Make it brief, for we have a need of you.""** So I turned around, and behold, it was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"2435","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that a man greeted the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with peace while he was praying, so the Prophet ﷺ returned the greeting with a gesture. When he finished the prayer, the Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""We used to return the greeting of peace during our prayer, but we were subsequently forbidden from doing so.""**"
"2436","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""Formerly, when a man entered the mosque and found the people praying, he would ask the person beside him, and he would inform him of what he had missed; so he would make up what he missed, and then join them in prayer. This continued until one day Muʿādh [ibn Jabal] arrived, and they gestured to him that he had missed such and such [rakʿahs], but he refused to do that; instead, he joined them in prayer and then made up what he had missed afterward. When this was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said: > **""Muʿādh has done well, and you should do as he has done.""**"
"2437","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ spoke during prayer out of forgetfulness, so he built upon what he had already prayed.""**"
"2438","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying and greeted him with peace, but he did not return the greeting to me.""**"
"2439","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and greeted him with peace, so he gestured back to me.""**"
"2440","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Smiling does not break the prayer, but laughing aloud breaks it.""**"
"2441","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""While the Prophet ﷺ was praying ʿAṣr during the Battle of Badr, he smiled. When he concluded the prayer, it was said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, you smiled during the prayer?' He ﷺ replied: > **""Mīkāʾīl passed by me with dust upon his wing, and he smiled at me, so I smiled back at him.""**"
"2442","Jābir ibn Riʾāb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Jibrīl عليه السلام passed by me while I was praying and smiled at me, so I smiled back at him.""**"
"2443","Abū Mūsā [al-Ashʿarī] رضي الله عنه narrated: ""While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, a man entered and fell into a pit that was in the mosque, as he had impaired vision. Many of the people laughed while they were in prayer. Thereupon, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded: > **""Whoever laughed must repeat the ablution and repeat the prayer.""**"
"2444","Jābir رضي الله عنه was asked about a man who laughs during prayer. He replied: > **""He must repeat the prayer, but he does not repeat the ablution.""**"
"2445","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We used to pray behind the Prophet ﷺ and look toward the darkness of dawn (*al-sadaf*) [in the sky].""**"
"2446","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you is praying, let him not raise his gaze toward the sky, lest it be snatched away.""**"
"2447","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not raise your eyes toward the sky lest they be snatched away—meaning: during prayer.""**"
"2448","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""People must either desist from raising their eyes toward the sky [during prayer] or their sight will surely be snatched away.""**"
"2449","Ibrāhīm [al-Nakhaʿī] narrated that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه saw a man raising his hands toward the sky, supplicating while he was in his prayer. ʿAbdullāh remarked: > **""Perhaps his sight will be snatched away before it returns to him.""**"
"2450","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you stands for prayer, let him not close his eyes.""**"
"2451","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""None of you should pray while his garment is over his nose, for indeed that is the muzzle of Satan.""**"
"2452","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade blowing [away dust] during prostration and blowing into a drink.""**"
"2453","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When one of you stands for prayer, let him prepare his place of prostration beforehand and not leave it neglected, such that when he drops down to prostrate he blows on it and then prostrates. For one of you to prostrate upon a burning ember would be better for him than to prostrate after having blown [upon the ground].""**"
"2454","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three things are from coarse behavior: that a man urinates while standing, that he wipes his forehead before he concludes his prayer, or that he blows [away dust] in his prostration.""**"
"2455","Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه reported in a raised narration that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Three things are from coarse behavior: that a man blows in his prostration, or that he wipes his forehead before concluding his prayer...""** *(The third was forgotten by the narrator.)*"
"2456","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A man should not wipe the dust from his forehead until he has completed the prayer, though there is no harm in wiping sweat from his temples; for indeed the angels continue to pray for him as long as the mark of prostration remains upon him.""**"
"2457","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ would not wipe his face during the prayer.""**"
"2458","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to wipe sweat from his face during prayer.""**"
"2459","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying, so he stood at his side and followed his prayer. Then a scorpion approached until it reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but it left him and moved toward ʿAlī. ʿAlī struck it with his sandal until he killed it, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw no harm in killing it.""**"
"2460","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day while he was standing in prayer in the mosque, and the door toward the Qiblah was closed, slightly to the side of the prayer area. I asked for it to be opened, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ heard my voice, he reached out with his hand and opened the door, and then continued with his prayer.""**"
"2461","Abū Mūsā [al-Ashʿarī] and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not recite the Qurʾan while in a state of major ritual impurity (*junub*), nor while bowing or prostrating; do not squat like a dog; do not pray with your hair tied back in a knot; do not spread your forearms [on the ground] like a predatory beast; do not wear striped Egyptian silk (*al-qassī*); do not wear a gold signet ring; and do not wear your ring on either of these two fingers: the index finger or the middle finger.""**"
"2462","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Resting the hands on the hips during prayer is the resting posture of the people of the Fire.""**"
"2463","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to touch his beard in prayer without idling or fidgeting.""**"
"2464","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ touch his beard during prayer.""**"
"2465","ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ would sometimes touch his beard during prayer.""**"
"2466","Al-Ḥasan [al-Baṣrī] رحمه الله narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to touch his beard during prayer.""**"
"2467","Anas [ibn Mālik] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade squatting (*al-iqʿāʾ*) and resting on the hip (*al-tawarruk*) during prayer.""**"
"2468","Samurah [ibn Jundub] رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade resting on the hip (*al-tawarruk*), squatting (*al-iqʿāʾ*), and sitting poised restlessly on our heels (*al-istifāz*) in our prayer.""**"
"2469","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade a man from praying while his hair is braided or tied up in a knot.""**"
"2470","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ disliked yawning during prayer.""**"
"2471","Abū al-Yaqẓān reported from his father, from his grandfather, in a raised narration that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Sneezing, drowsiness, nosebleeds, menstruation, vomiting, and yawning during prayer are from Satan.""**"
"2472","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Yawning and sneezing during prayer are from Satan.""**"
"2473","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about smoothing the pebbles [for prostration]. He ﷺ replied: > **""Once, and for you to refrain from doing so is better than a hundred she-camels, all with black eyes.""**"
"2474","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about everything, even about smoothing the pebbles. He ﷺ replied: > **""Once, or leave them alone.""**"
"2475","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man moving pebbles while he was in prayer. When he finished, he ﷺ said to the man: > **""That is your only share from your prayer.""**"
"2476","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""We were with the Prophet ﷺ in prayer, and a man was turning pebbles over with his hand. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concluded the prayer, he turned to us and asked: 'Which of you was turning the pebbles over with his hand?' A man stood up and said: 'I was, O Messenger of Allah.' He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, that is your share from your prayer.""**"
"2477","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man behind him turning over pebbles while he was in prayer. He ﷺ asked: 'Who was turning over the pebbles?' A man replied: 'I was.' He ﷺ said: > **""That is your share from your prayer.""**"
"2478","Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about smoothing the pebbles—meaning in prayer—and he ﷺ said: > **""A single wipe [only].""**"
"2479","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the morning prayer, and he began reaching out with his hand. When he finished, the people asked him about it, and he ﷺ said: > **""Satan was throwing sparks of fire at me to distract me from the prayer, so I reached out for him. Had I seized him, he would not have escaped me until he was tied to one of the pillars of the mosque for the children of Madīnah to gaze at.""**"
"2480","In another narration from him [Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه]: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer and began rebuffing something in front of him.""**"
"2481","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood and led the morning prayer, with Abū Saʿīd behind him, and the recitation became obscure to him. When he finished his prayer, he ﷺ said: > **""If only you had seen me and Iblīs when I reached out with my hand and kept choking him until I felt the coolness of his saliva between these two fingers of mine—the thumb and the one next to it! Had it not been for the supplication of my brother Sulaymān [عليه السلام], he would have found himself tied to one of the pillars of the mosque for the boys of Madīnah to play with.""**"
"2482","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""While we were standing in rows behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr prayer, we saw him reaching forward for something during prayer to take it, and reaching again to take it, but a barrier was placed before him. Then he stepped back, so we stepped back; then he stepped back a second time, so we stepped back. When he said the salām, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb asked: 'O Messenger of Allah, today we saw you do something in your prayer that you had never done before.' He ﷺ replied: > **""Paradise was displayed before me with all its splendor and radiance, so I reached out to pluck a cluster of fruit from it to bring to you; had I taken it, everyone between the heavens and the earth could have eaten from it without diminishing it, but a barrier was placed between me and it. Then the Fire was displayed before me, and when I felt the heat of its blaze, I stepped back. The majority of those I saw in it were women who, if entrusted with a secret, disclose it; if asked, are stingy; if they ask, are persistent and importunate; and if given, are ungrateful. And I saw in it Liḥy [ʿAmr ibn Luḥayy] dragging his entrails, and the person who resembled him most was Maʿbad ibn Aktham.""** Maʿbad asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, is any harm feared for me due to resembling him, as he is an ancestor?"" He ﷺ replied: > **""No, you are a believer while he was a disbeliever, and he was the first to unite the Arabs upon the worship of idols.""**"
"2483","It is narrated from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ said the like of it: > **""Paradise and the Fire were displayed before me during the prayer...""** *(Narrating the like of the previous hadith).*"
"2484","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ one day and he prolonged the standing, whereas he usually lightened the prayer when leading us. I saw him reach out with his hand as though to take something, and then he bowed. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said the salām, he sat and we sat around him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'I knew that the length of my prayer and standing startled you.' We said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah, and we heard you say: ""O Lord, while I am among them?""' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, there is nothing that you have been promised in the Hereafter except that it was displayed before me in this standing of mine, to the point that the Fire was displayed before me. A portion of it advanced toward me until it was level with this tent of mine, and I feared that it would engulf you, so I said: 'O Lord, while I am among them?' Thereupon, Allah turned it away from you, and it retreated in pieces like carpets. I cast a glance into it and saw ʿImrān ibn Khurthān ibn al-Ḥārith, one of Banū Ghifār, reclining in Hell upon his bow; and I saw in it the woman of Ḥimyar, the owner of the cat that she had tied up—neither feeding it nor giving it water [until it died of starvation].""**"
"2485","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""We came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying, and he gestured to us with his hand that we should sit down, so we sat.""**"
"2486","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would spend the night, then Bilāl would call him with the adhān; he would stand, perform ghusl—and I would see the water trickling down his cheek and hair—and then go out and pray, and I would hear his weeping.""**"
"2487","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""If one of you feels a disturbance [in his bowels or bladder] while he is in his prayer, let him depart and perform ablution.""**"
"2488","Al-Miswar ibn Makhramah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""None of you should ever pray while experiencing any bodily discomfort—meaning the need to relieve feces or urine.""**"
"2489","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would never pray while feeling any discomfort of bodily waste [urine or feces].""**"
"2490","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever bears witness that I am the Messenger of Allah must not attend the prayer while suppressing the urge to urinate [or defecate], until he has relieved himself.""**"
"2491","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, part of the perfection of the prayer is the proper establishment of the rows.""**"
"2492","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Establish the rows well in prayer.""**"
"2493","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I see from behind me just as I see in front of me; so straighten your rows and perform your bowing and prostrating well.""**"
"2494","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""I remember our situation [in the time of the Prophet ﷺ] where the prayer would not begin until our rows were fully formed and complete.""**"
"2495","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""You shall surely straighten the rows, or faces will surely be effaced, and you shall surely lower your gazes, or your sights will surely be snatched away.""**"
"2496","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I have been favoured with four qualities: I and my Ummah have been made in prayer as the angels line up, the earth has been made for me a means of ablution, the earth has been made for me a mosque, and the spoils of war have been made lawful for me.""**"
"2497","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Line up as the angels line up before their Lord."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, how do the angels line up before their Lord? He said: > **""They straighten the rows and join their shoulders together.""**"
"2498","Bilāl رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to straighten our shoulders in prayer.""**"
"2499","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Stand straight so that your hearts become straight, and stay joined together so that you show mercy to one another.""**"
"2500","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Straighten your rows, for Satan slips through them like the ḥadhaf, or like the young of the ḥadhaf.""**"
"2501","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of you are those with the softest shoulders in prayer, and there is no step of greater reward than the step a man takes to a gap in the row and fills it.""**"
"2502","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fills a gap in a row, Allah raises him a degree thereby and builds for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"2503","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever fills a gap in the row, he is forgiven.""**"
"2504","ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the rows.""**"
"2505","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Beware of gaps, meaning in prayer.""**"
"2506","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Straighten your rows, for Satan slips through them like the ḥadhaf, or like the young of the ḥadhaf.""**"
"2507","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Keep the rows tightly joined, for I see the devils slipping through among you as though they were the young of the ḥadhaf.""**"
"2508","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who join the rows, and no servant joins a row except that Allah raises him a degree thereby and the angels shower goodness upon him.""**"
"2509","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, and upon the second? He said: ""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, and upon the second? He said: ""And upon the second."" And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Straighten your rows, align your shoulders, be gentle in the hands of your brothers, and close the gaps, for Satan enters among you in the likeness of the ḥadhaf, meaning the small young of sheep.""**"
"2510","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon the first row, or upon the first rows.""**"
"2511","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Indeed Allah, Blessed and Exalted, and His angels send blessings upon the first row.""**"
"2512","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sought forgiveness for the first row three times, for the second row twice, and for the third row once.""**"
"2513","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Hold to the first row, and hold to its right side, and beware of forming a row between pillars.""**"
"2514","Abū Burdah al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If you are able to stand behind the imām, do so; otherwise stand to his right.""**"
"2515","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUmayr رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The first row has excellence over the other rows.""**"
"2516","ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ narrated that ʿĀmir ibn Masʿūd al-Qurashī related to him, having crowded with him at Makkah in the days of Ibn al-Zubayr, near the Station [of Ibrāhīm], in the first row. He said: I said to him: Is it said that there is good in the first row? He said: Yes indeed; by Allah, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If people knew what is in the first row, they would not line up in it except by drawing lots.""**"
"2517","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said, meaning the foremost first row: > **""Indeed Allah and His angels send blessings upon those who advance to the front in their prayer.""**"
"2518","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah expiates sins and increases good deeds?"" They said: Yes indeed, O Messenger of Allah. He said: ""Performing ablution thoroughly despite hardship, taking many steps to the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another. There is none among you who leaves his house in a state of purity and prays with the Muslims, then sits in the gathering waiting for the next prayer, except that the angels say: O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy on him. And when you stand for prayer, straighten your rows and establish them and close the gaps, for I see you from behind my back. When your imām says: Allāhu Akbar, then say: Allāhu Akbar; when he bows, then bow; when he says: Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah, then say: O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise. And the best of the rows of men is the foremost, and the worst of them is the rearmost; and the best of the rows of women is the rearmost, and the worst of them is the foremost. O company of women, when the men prostrate, lower your gazes so that you do not see the private parts of the men, due to the tightness of their waist-wrappers."" > **""Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah expiates sins and increases good deeds? Performing ablution thoroughly despite hardship, taking many steps to the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another.""**"
"2519","Abū Saʿīd, meaning al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of the rows of men is the foremost, and the worst of them is the rearmost; and the best of the rows of women is the rearmost, and the worst of them is the foremost.""**"
"2520","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of the rows of men is the first of them, and the worst of them is the last of them; and the worst of the rows of women is the first of them, and the best of them is the last of them.""**"
"2521","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The best of the rows of men is the first of them, and the worst of them is the last of them; and the best of the rows of women is the last of them, and the worst of them is the first of them.""**"
"2522","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of the rows of men is the first of them, and the worst of them is the last of them; and the best of the rows of women is the last of them, and the worst of them is the first of them.""**"
"2523","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of the rows of men is the first of them, and the worst of them is the last of them; and the best of the rows of women is the last of them, and the worst of them is the first of them.""**"
"2524","ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Let those of you with mature understanding and intellect be closest to me, then those who come next after them, then those who come next after them.""**"
"2525","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command the Emigrants to advance and to be in the front of the rows, saying: > **""They know more about prayer than the ignorant and the Bedouins, and I do not like that the Bedouins should be in front of them while they do not know how prayer is performed.""**"
"2526","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Let the Bedouins stand behind the Emigrants and the Helpers, so that they may follow them in prayer.""**"
"2527","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه said: > **""It is from the Sunnah that a man stands, with two men behind him, and a woman behind the two of them.""**"
"2528","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever keeps alive the left side of the mosque due to the scarcity of its people, for him is a double reward.""**"
"2529","Jābir ibn Ṣakhr, one of Banū Salimah رضي الله عنه, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while on the road to Makkah, said: ""Who will go ahead of us to al-Uthāyah?"" Abū Uways said: That was when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had sent us forth. He said: ""So that he may plaster its basin and prepare it and fill it with water until we come to him?"" Jabbār said: So I stood up and said: I will. He said: ""Go."" So I went and came to al-Uthāyah, and I plastered it and prepared it and filled it with water. Then my eyes overcame me and I slept, and I did not wake except due to a man whose camel was pulling toward the water while he held it back from it. He said: O keeper of the basin, bring your basin for watering. And behold, it was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: Yes. So he brought his camel to drink, then turned away and made it kneel. Then he said: ""Follow me with the water-skin."" So I followed him with water, and he performed ablution and performed it excellently, and I performed ablution along with him. Then he stood to pray, and I stood to his left, so he took my hand and moved me to his right, and we prayed, and it was not long before the people came to us. > **""Who will go ahead of us to al-Uthāyah, so that he may plaster its basin and prepare it and fill it with water until we come to him?""**"
"2530","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and he placed me on his right.""**"
"2531","ʿAbdullāh ibn Unays رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying, and I stood to his left, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took hold of me and placed me on his right.""**"
"2532","Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ performed ablution and wiped over his socks and prayed, and he placed me on his right.""**"
"2533","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Do not line up between pillars, and do not face people in prayer while they are engaged in conversation.""**"
"2534","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""I disliked prayer between pillars for one man alone and for two.""**"
"2535","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever sees a gap in a row, let him fill it himself; if he does not do so, then if a passer-by passes, let him step over his neck, for he has no sanctity.""**"
"2536","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever leaves the first row for fear of harming anyone, Allah doubles for him the reward of the first row.""**"
"2537","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you reaches the row and it is already complete, let him pull a man toward himself and make him stand beside him.""**"
"2538","Wābiṣah ibn Maʿbad رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prayer while a man was praying behind the people alone, and he said: > **""O you who prays alone, did you not join a row and enter along with them, or pull a man toward yourself if the place was too narrow for you? Repeat your prayer, for there is no prayer for you.""**"
"2539","ʿAṭāʾ narrated that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما saying upon the pulpit: ""When one of you enters the mosque while the people are bowing, let him bow when he enters, then advance while bowing until he enters the row, for that is the Sunnah."" ʿAṭāʾ said: I saw him doing that, and Ibn Jurayj said: I saw ʿAṭāʾ doing that. > **""When one of you enters the mosque while the people are bowing, let him bow when he enters, then advance while bowing until he enters the row, for that is the Sunnah.""**"
"2540","Qatādah narrated that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""There is no harm in bowing before reaching the row.""**"
"2541","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Prophet ﷺ saw a man praying alone behind the row, so he commanded him to repeat the prayer.""**"
"2542","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man praying alone behind the rows and said: > **""Repeat the prayer.""**"
"2543","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه and ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwāk at every prayer.""**"
"2544","Umm Ḥabībah رضي الله عنها narrated that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwāk at every prayer.""**"
"2545","Zaynab bint Jaḥsh رضي الله عنها narrated that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwāk at every prayer, just as they perform ablution.""**"
"2546","A man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated, meaning from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: > **""Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwāk with every prayer.""**"
"2547","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command the use of the siwāk.""**"
"2548","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwāk at every prayer.""**"
"2549","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were it not that you would become too weak, I would have commanded you to use the siwāk at every prayer.""**"
"2550","Al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه narrated that people used to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ without having used the siwāk, so he said: > **""You enter upon me with yellowed teeth; use the siwāk. Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have made the siwāk obligatory upon them at every prayer, just as I made ablution obligatory upon them.""**"
"2551","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my Ummah, I would have commanded them to use the siwāk at every prayer.""**"
"2552","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Were it not that it would become an established practice, I would have commanded the use of the siwāk at every prayer.""**"
"2553","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: ""We used to place the siwāk of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ along with his water for purification. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, you never leave the siwāk? He said: > **""Yes indeed; if I were able to use it at every pair of rakʿahs of my prayer, I would do so.""**"
"2554","ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ رضي الله عنها, narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The excellence of prayer with the siwāk over prayer without the siwāk is seventy prayers.""**"
"2555","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Two rakʿahs with the siwāk are better than seventy rakʿahs without the siwāk.""**"
"2556","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""I was commanded to use the siwāk until I thought that revelation or the Qurʾan would come down to me concerning it.""**"
"2557","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I was commanded to use the siwāk until I feared that revelation would come to me concerning it.""**"
"2558","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I was indeed commanded to use the siwāk until I feared for my teeth.""**"
"2559","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I was commanded to use the siwāk until I feared that it would be made obligatory upon me.""**"
"2560","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not sleep except with the siwāk beside him, and when he awoke he would begin with the siwāk; and in some wordings: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not wake at any hour of the night except that he would pass the siwāk over his mouth: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not sleep except with the siwāk beside him, and when he awoke he would begin with the siwāk.""**"
"2561","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I was commanded to use the siwāk until I feared toothlessness, or until I feared for my gums and my teeth.""**"
"2562","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Gabriel commanded me to use the siwāk until I thought I would become toothless.""**"
"2563","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Gabriel kept enjoining me to use the siwāk until I feared for my molar teeth.""**"
"2564","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه commanded the use of the siwāk and said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When a servant uses the siwāk and then stands to pray, an angel stands behind him and listens to his recitation, then draws near to him until he places his mouth upon his mouth, so nothing of the Qurʾan leaves his mouth except that it enters the belly of the angel. So purify your mouths for the Qurʾan.""**"
"2565","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to use the siwāk and said: > **""What an excellent thing it is.""**"
"2566","Malīḥ ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Khaṭmī, from his father, from his grandfather, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Five are from the Sunan of the Messengers: modesty, forbearance, cupping, the siwāk, and applying perfume.""**"
"2567","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I adhered to the siwāk until I feared it would wear away my teeth.""**"
"2568","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not sleep a night nor wake except that he cleaned his teeth with the siwāk.""**"
"2569","Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not leave his house for any of the prayers until he had used the siwāk.""**"
"2570","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to use the siwak repeatedly during the night."
"2571","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: Sometimes the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would use the siwak four times during the night."
"2572","Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm narrated from Maymūnah رضي الله عنها, and he had been raised as an orphan under her care, that a siwak would constantly remain in a vessel, and if work or prayer did not occupy her, she would take the siwak and clean her teeth with it."
"2573","Bahz رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to use the siwak widthwise [across the teeth]."
"2574","ʿĀ'ishah رضي الله عنها said: I said, O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, should a man whose mouth has gone [whose teeth have fallen out] use the siwak? He said: > **""Yes.""** I said, How should he do it? He said: > **""He should insert his finger into his mouth and rub it.""**"
"2575","Abū Khayrah al-Ṣubāḥī رضي الله عنه said: I was among the delegation that came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he provisioned us with arak branches to use as siwak. We said, O Messenger of Allah, we have palm stalks with us, but we accept your honour and your gift. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Allah, forgive ʿAbd al-Qays, for they embraced Islam willingly without compulsion, when other people sat back and did not embrace Islam except in disgrace and under duress.""**"
"2576","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""How excellent is the siwak of olive, from a blessed tree. It sweetens the mouth and removes tartar, and it is my siwak and the siwak of the Prophets before me.""**"
"2577","Kathīr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf al-Muzanī narrated from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The fingers take the place of the siwak when there is no siwak.""**"
"2578","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: Accustom yourselves to goodness, for goodness comes only through habit, and guard your intentions in Ṣalāh."
"2579","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: When the prayer has been commenced, do not leave it for something else."
"2580","Shaqīq said: ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه said: Whoever emigrates seeking something, it shall be for him. He said: And a man emigrated in order to marry a woman called Umm Qays, so he came to be called the emigrant of Umm Qays."
"2581","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, and they would not raise their hands except at the opening of the prayer. And on one occasion he said: They would not raise their hands after the first takbīr."
"2582","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ open the prayer and raise his hands until he brought them beyond his ears."
"2583","Al-Dhayyāl ibn Ḥarmalah said: I asked Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما, How many were you on the Day of the Tree? He said: We were one thousand four hundred. He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to raise his hands at every takbīr of the prayer."
"2584","Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl said: One who had heard a Bedouin narrate to me that he said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying. He said: He raised his head from rukūʿ and raised his palms until they were level with, or reached, the lobes of his ears."
"2585","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to raise his hands in rukūʿ and sujūd."
"2586","Anas رضي الله عنه said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raise his hands when he opened the prayer, when he bowed, and when he raised his head from rukūʿ."
"2587","Qatādah said: I said to Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه, Show us how the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was. So he stood and prayed, and he would raise his hands with every takbīr."
"2588","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and behind Abū Bakr and ʿUmar; all of them would raise their hands when they opened the prayer, when they said the takbīr for rukūʿ, and when they raised the head pronouncing the takbīr for sujūd."
"2589","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you opens the prayer, let him raise his hands and face the Qiblah with their insides, for indeed Allah is before him.""**"
"2590","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to raise his hands at the takbīr for rukūʿ and at the takbīr when going down in prostration."
"2591","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in his prayer, he would raise his hands opposite his ears, and when he pronounced the takbīr he would let them down. And sometimes I saw him place his right hand over his left. When he finished the Opening of the Book he would pause, then raise his hands opposite his ears and pronounce the takbīr and bow, and we would not bow until we saw him bowing. Then he would stand straight up from his rukūʿ until every bone returned to its place, then he would raise his hands opposite his ears."
"2592","Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUmayr رضي الله عنه said: He ﷺ used to teach us: > **""When you stand for prayer, raise your hands so that they do not go beyond your ears, then say: Allāhu Akbar, glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You, blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god but You. And if you add nothing beyond the takbīr, it will suffice you.""**"
"2593","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The hands are not raised except in seven places: when opening the prayer, and when entering al-Masjid al-Ḥarām.""**"
"2594","Wā'il ibn Ḥujr رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to me: > **""O Wā'il ibn Ḥujr, when you pray, place your hands level with your ears, and a woman should place her hands level with her breasts.""**"
"2595","Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهم narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The hands are raised in seven places: at the opening of the prayer, when facing the House, at al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah, at the two standings, and at the Stone.""**"
"2596","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه said: Indeed there is recorded for every gesture a man makes with his hand during prayer, for every finger, a good deed or a degree."
"2597","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه said: When Bilāl رضي الله عنه said, Qad qāmat al-Ṣalāh, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would rise with the takbīr."
"2598","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""For everything there is a choicest part, and the choicest part of prayer is the first takbīr.""**"
"2599","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed for everything there is a prime, and indeed the prime of prayer is the first takbīr, so guard it well.""**"
"2600","Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥārith said: Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه fell ill — or was absent — so Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه led us in prayer. He pronounced the takbīr aloud when he opened the prayer, when he bowed, when he said, Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah, when he raised his head from prostration, when he prostrated, and when he stood up from the two rakʿahs, continuing thus until he completed his prayer. When he had prayed, it was said to him, The people have differed over your prayer. So he went out and stood upon the minbar and said, O people, by Allah I do not care whether your prayer differs or does not differ; thus did I see the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray."
"2601","Al-Barā' رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pronounce the takbīr at every lowering and rising."
"2602","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""The key of prayer is purification, its inviolability begins with the takbīr, and its release is with the taslīm.""**"
"2603","ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The key of prayer is purification, its inviolability begins with the takbīr, and its release is with the taslīm.""**"
"2604","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The inviolability of prayer begins with the takbīr and its release is with the taslīm, and when you have given the salām, if some need hastens you, then go forth before turning to face [the people]."
"2605","Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that a man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was sitting, and he prayed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered him to repeat it, so he repeated it two or three times. He said, O Messenger of Allah, I have done my utmost after two or three times. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed the prayer of none among the people is complete until he performs ablution, placing ablution in its proper places, then says: Allāhu Akbar.""**"
"2606","Al-Ḥārith ibn Ghuṭayf — or Ghuṭayf ibn al-Ḥārith — رضي الله عنه said: Of the things I have not forgotten, I have not forgotten that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placing his right hand over his left in prayer."
"2607","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a man who was praying with his left hand placed over his right, so he pulled it away and placed the right over the left."
"2608","Shaddād ibn Shuraḥbīl رضي الله عنه said: I have not forgotten, and I shall not forget, that I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing with his right hand over his left hand, grasping it, meaning in prayer."
"2609","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: I heard the Prophet of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed we, the company of the Prophets, have been commanded to hasten our breaking of the fast, to delay our suḥūr, and to place our right hands over our left in prayer.""**"
"2610","Yaʿlā ibn Murrah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Three things Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves: hastening the breaking of the fast, delaying the suḥūr, and striking the hands, one upon the other, in prayer.""**"
"2611","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه, raising it [to the Prophet ﷺ], said: > **""Three are from the character of prophethood: hastening the breaking of the fast, delaying the suḥūr, and placing the right over the left in prayer.""**"
"2612","ʿUqbah ibn Abī ʿĀ'ishah said: I saw ʿAbdullāh ibn Jābir al-Bayāḍī, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, placing one of his hands over his forearm in prayer."
"2613","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated that a man said one day when he entered prayer: Praise be to Allah filling the heaven, and he glorified and supplicated. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Who said them?""** He said: I did. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I indeed saw the angels passing it among themselves.""**"
"2614","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه said: A man came while we were in the row behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he entered the row and said: Allāhu Akbaru kabīrā, wa subḥāna Allāhi bukratan wa aṣīlā. He said: The Muslims raised their heads and disapproved of the man, and they said, Who is it that raised his voice above the voice of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he said: > **""Who is this one with the loud voice?""** It was said: Here he is, O Messenger of Allah. He said: > **""By Allah, I indeed saw your speech ascending into the heaven until a door was opened and it entered therein.""**"
"2615","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to us: > **""When one of you prays, let him say: O Allah, place distance between me and my sins as You have placed distance between the east and the west. O Allah, I seek refuge in You lest You turn Your Face away from me on the Day of Resurrection. O Allah, purify me of my sins as the white garment is purified of filth. O Allah, grant me life as a Muslim and cause me to die as a Muslim.""**"
"2616","Samurah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: > **""O Allah, place distance between me and my sin as You have placed distance between the east and the west, and purify me of my sin as You have purified the white garment of filth.""**"
"2617","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us that when we opened the prayer we should say: > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You, blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god but You.""** And ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه used to teach us and say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say it."
"2618","Ibn Jurayj said: One whom I deem truthful narrated to me from Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and ʿUthmān and from Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that when they opened [the prayer] they would say before the recitation: > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You, blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god but You.""**"
"2619","Wāthilah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he opened the prayer, would say: > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You, blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god but You.""**"
"2620","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ opened the prayer, he would say: > **""I have turned my face toward the One Who originated the heavens and the earth, upright as a Muslim, and I am not among the polytheists. Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You, blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god but You. Indeed my prayer and my sacrifice and my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, Who has no partner, and with that I have been commanded, and I am among the Muslims.""**"
"2621","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه said: A writing reached me containing the opening [supplication] of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he pronounced the takbīr he would say: > **""Indeed I have turned my face toward the One Who originated the heavens and the earth, upright, and I am not among the polytheists. Indeed my prayer and my sacrifice and my living and my dying are for Allah, Lord of the worlds, Who has no partner, and with that I have been commanded, and I am among the Muslims. O Allah, You are the Sovereign, there is no god but You, glory be to You and praise be to You. You are my Lord and I am Your servant, You have no partner. I have wronged myself and I confess my sin, so forgive me all my sins, for none forgives sins but You. At Your service and aided by Your felicity, and all good is in Your Hands. There is no refuge nor escape from You except to You. I seek Your forgiveness and I repent to You.""** Then he would recite."
"2622","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that when he pronounced the takbīr, he would raise his hands until they were level with his ears, saying: > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and praise be to You, blessed is Your Name, exalted is Your Majesty, and there is no god but You.""**"
"2623","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ opened the prayer, he would say, All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds, then he would pause briefly."
"2624","Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān رضي الله عنهما said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ one night, and he performed ablution and stood to pray. I came to him and stood at his left, so he placed me at his right. Then he said: > **""Glory be to Allah, Possessor of dominion, compulsion, grandeur, and greatness.""**"
"2625","Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī رضي الله عنه said: While the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was praying, he heard a man supplicate: Praise be to Allah, abundant, good, blessed praise therein, as befits the nobility of the Face of our Lord, Mighty and Majestic. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he said: > **""Who is the one who said such and such? I indeed saw twelve angels racing toward it.""** Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fixed his gaze until they were concealed by the veil. He said: > **""It is yours with its seal on the Day of Resurrection, and the like thereof.""**"
"2626","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما was asked about reciting Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm aloud, and he said: We used to say it is the recitation of the Bedouins."
"2627","Saʿīd ibn Yazīd said: I asked Anas رضي الله عنه, Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recite, In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, or, All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds? He said: Indeed you are asking me about something no one before you has asked me about."
"2628","One of the wives of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — Nāfiʿ said: I think it was Ḥafṣah رضي الله عنها — was asked about the recitation of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and she said: Indeed you are not able to do it. He said: It was said, Inform us of it. He said: So she recited a recitation in which she proceeded deliberately. He said: Then Ibn Abī Mulaykah demonstrated for us: All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds, then he paused; the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, then he paused; Master of the Day of Judgment."
"2629","Ibrāhīm al-Ṣā'igh said: I asked Maṭar al-Warrāq, saying, Do you recite Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm and seek refuge from Satan, the accursed, in every rakʿah and at the opening of every sūrah? He said: Qatādah informed me from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn and Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنهما, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: > **""They are the two pauses, done silently to oneself: when he opens the prayer and when he rises from sitting after the two rakʿahs.""**"
"2630","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm, the polytheists would mock him and say, Muḥammad mentions the god of Yamāmah, for Musaylimah used to call himself al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm. So when this verse was revealed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that it not be recited aloud."
"2631","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, used to recite Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm silently."
"2632","Abū Wā'il said: ʿAlī and ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما would not recite Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm aloud, nor the seeking of refuge, nor the āmīn."
"2633","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm aloud in prayer."
"2634","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Prophet ﷺ would not know the end of a sūrah until Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm was revealed. When Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm was revealed, he knew that the sūrah had been sealed and another — or a new sūrah had begun."
"2635","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to say: > **""All praise is due to Allah, Lord of the worlds, is seven verses, one of them being Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm, and it is the Seven Oft-Repeated and the Magnificent Qur'an, and it is the Mother of the Qur'an and the Opening of the Book.""**"
"2636","ʿAlī and ʿAmmār رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm aloud."
"2637","Nāfiʿ narrated that Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, when he opened the prayer, would begin with Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm in the Mother of the Qur'an and in the sūrah that follows it, and he would mention that he heard that from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"2638","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not leave the mosque until I teach you a verse from a sūrah that was not revealed to anyone before me except Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd.""** Then the Prophet ﷺ went out until he reached the threshold of the door. He said: > **""With what do you open your prayer and your recitation?""** I said: With Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm. He said: > **""It is it.""** Then he brought out his other foot."
"2639","ʿAbdullāh ibn Buḥaynah رضي الله عنه, who was among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Did any one of you recite with me just now?""** They said: Yes. He said: > **""Indeed I say, what is wrong with me that the Qur'an is being contended with me?""** So the people refrained from reciting along with him when he said that."
"2640","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: They used to recite behind the Prophet ﷺ, so he said: > **""You have confused the Qur'an for me.""**"
"2641","Ibn Buḥaynah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ prayed a prayer in which recitation is aloud, and when he finished he said: > **""Do you recite behind me?""** Some of them said: Indeed we do so. He said: > **""Do not do so. Indeed I say, what is wrong with me that the Qur'an is being contended with me?""** He said: So the people refrained from reciting in that in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited aloud."
"2642","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led his Companions in prayer, and when he completed his prayer he turned to them with his face and said: > **""Do you recite in your prayer behind the imām while the imām is reciting?""** They fell silent. He said it three times. Then one speaker — or speakers — said: Indeed we do so. He said: > **""Then do not do so; let one of you recite the Opening of the Book to himself.""**"
"2643","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما said: We prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he finished he said to us: > **""Do you recite along with me when you are in prayer?""** We said: Yes. He said: > **""Then do not do so, except with the Mother of the Qur'an.""**"
"2644","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه said: A man asked the Prophet ﷺ, saying, O Messenger of Allah, is there recitation in every prayer? He said: > **""Yes.""** A man among the people said: Has this become obligatory? The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""I do not consider the imām, when he recites, but that it suffices.""**"
"2645","Jahr narrated: I recited behind the Prophet ﷺ, and when he finished the prayer he said: > **""O Jahr! Let your Lord hear you, and do not let me hear you.""**"
"2646","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: O so-and-so, do not recite behind the imām, unless he is an imām who does not recite."
"2647","Abū Wāʾil narrated: A man came to Ibn Masʿūd and said: O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, shall I recite behind the imām? He said: Listen attentively to the Qurʾān, for in the Ṣalāh there is occupation, and the imām will suffice you for that."
"2648","Ibrāhīm narrated that Ibn Masʿūd would not recite behind the imām, and Ibrāhīm used to adopt this practice, and when Ibn Masʿūd was the imām he would recite in the first two rakʿahs and would not recite anything in the last two."
"2649","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever has an imām, then the recitation of the imām is recitation for him.""**"
"2650","Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl narrated: Hishām ibn ʿĀmir came to the Ṣalāh and hastened his walking, and he entered the Ṣalāh while his breath was labored, so he recited aloud behind the imām. When he finished his Ṣalāh it was said to him: Do you recite behind the imām? He said: We indeed do so."
"2651","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites behind the imām, let him recite the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb.""**"
"2652","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Every Ṣalāh in which the Umm al-Qurʾān is not recited is deficient, it is deficient, it is deficient.""**"
"2653","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Every Ṣalāh in which the Umm al-Qurʾān is not recited is incomplete, incomplete, incomplete.""**"
"2654","Mihrān narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever does not recite the Umm al-Kitāb in his Ṣalāh, then it is deficient.""**"
"2655","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do you recite behind me?""** They said: Yes. He said: > **""Then do not do so, except with the Umm al-Qurʾān.""**"
"2656","A man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Perhaps you recite while the imām is reciting?""** He said it three times. They said: Indeed we do that. He said: > **""Then do not do so, except that one of you recite the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb silently to himself.""**"
"2657","A man from the desert people narrated from his father, and his father had been a captive with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: I heard Muḥammad ﷺ say: > **""No Ṣalāh is accepted in which the Umm al-Kitāb is not recited.""**"
"2658","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه narrated: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb, then he said: > **""Your Lord says: O son of Adam, I have sent down upon you seven verses; three are for Me, and three are for you, and one is between Me and you. As for that which is for Me, then it is: All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful, Master of the Day of Judgment, You alone we worship and You alone we seek help from. The worship is from you, and the help is upon Me. And as for that which is for you: Guide us along the Straight Path, the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor, not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray.""**"
"2659","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to open the recitation with All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and ʿUthmān رضي الله عنهم did the same."
"2660","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ used to open the Ṣalāh with All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2661","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to open his Ṣalāh with All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"2662","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: While I was with the Prophet ﷺ, a Jewish man sought permission to enter, so he gave him permission, and he said: Death be upon you. The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""And upon you.""** Then she mentioned the ḥadīth up to where he said: > **""Indeed, they do not envy us for anything as they envy us for the Friday to which Allah guided us while they went astray from it, and for the Qiblah to which Allah guided us while they went astray from it, and for our saying Amīn behind the imām.""**"
"2663","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ was sitting in the house of one of his wives, with ʿĀʾishah beside him, when a group of Jews entered upon him and said: Death be upon you, O Muḥammad. He said: > **""And upon you.""** They sat and conversed, and ʿĀʾishah had understood the greeting with which they had greeted the Prophet ﷺ, so she gathered her anger and watched, and she could not restrain her rage, so she said: Rather, death be upon you, along with the anger of Allah and His curse; is this how you greet the Prophet of Allah ﷺ? Then they left, so the Prophet ﷺ said to her: > **""What carried you to say what you said?""** She said: Did you not hear how they greeted you, O Messenger of Allah? By Allah, I could not control myself when I heard their greeting to you. So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There is no harm; did you not see how I answered them? Indeed, the Jews are a people weary of their religion, and they are a people of envy, and they have not envied the Muslims for anything better than three things: returning the salām, establishing the rows, and their saying Amīn behind their imām in the prescribed prayer.""**"
"2664","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the imām says: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, those behind him say Amīn, and the Amīn of the people of the heaven and the Amīn of the people of the earth coincide, so Allah forgives the servant for what has preceded of his sins. And the example of the one who does not say Amīn is like the example of a man who went to battle with a people, so they cast lots and their arrows came out while his arrow did not come out, so he said: What is wrong with my arrow that it did not come out? It is said: Indeed, you did not say Amīn.""**"
"2665","Sulaymān narrated that Bilāl رضي الله عنه said to the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Do not precede me with Amīn.""**"
"2666","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""When the imām says: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, then say Amīn, and Allah will answer you.""**"
"2667","Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr رضي الله عنه narrated: I saw the Prophet ﷺ enter the Ṣalāh, and when he finished the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb he said Amīn three times."
"2668","Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when He said: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray, say: > **""My Lord, forgive me, Amīn.""**"
"2669","Umm al-Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنها narrated that she used to pray behind the Prophet ﷺ in the row of women, and she heard him say: All praise belongs to Allah, Lord of the worlds, the Most Merciful, the Especially Merciful, Master of the Day of Judgment, until he reached: Not of those who earned anger nor of those who went astray. He said: > **""Amīn.""** So that I heard it while I was in the row of women. And he used to say the takbīr when he prostrated and when he raised his head."
"2670","Al-Agharr, from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated: I prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and he recited Sūrat ar-Rūm."
"2671","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: There is no sūrah from the Mufaṣṣal, small or large, except that I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting all of it in the Ṣalāh."
"2672","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ counting the verses in the Ṣalāh."
"2673","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ used to recite the Qurʾān from its beginning to its end in the obligatory prayers."
"2674","Abū al-ʿĀliyah narrated: One who had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say the following informed me: > **""For every sūrah is its share of the rukūʿ and the sujūd.""** He said: Then I met him afterwards and said: Indeed, Ibn ʿUmar used to recite several sūrahs in a single rakʿah, so do you know who narrated this ḥadīth to you? He said: Indeed, I know him, and I know since when he narrated it to me; he narrated it to me fifty years ago."
"2675","Nāfiʿ narrated: Sometimes Ibn ʿUmar رحمه الله would lead us with two or three sūrahs in the obligatory prayer."
"2676","Abū al-ʿĀliyah narrated: Thirty from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ gathered and said: As for what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recites aloud in, we have known it, and what he does not recite aloud in, we will not measure by what he recites aloud in. He said: So they gathered, and no two among them differed, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Ẓuhr prayer about thirty verses in the first two rakʿahs, in each rakʿah, and in the last two rakʿahs about half of that, and he would recite in the ʿAṣr about half of his recitation in the two rakʿahs of Ẓuhr, and in the last two about half of that."
"2677","Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr رضي الله عنهما narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying toward the Corner, before he proclaimed openly what he was commanded, while the polytheists were listening: > **""So which of the favors of your Lord will you both deny?""**"
"2678","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""There is no Ṣalāh except with the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb and two verses along with it.""**"
"2679","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Sunnah of recitation in the Ṣalāh is to recite in the first two rakʿahs the Umm al-Qurʾān and a sūrah, and in the last two rakʿahs the Umm al-Qurʾān."
"2680","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: Recitation is Sunnah; do not oppose the people with your own opinion."
"2681","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and prayed two rakʿahs, and he did not recite in them except the Umm al-Kitāb."
"2682","Al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated: They disputed about the recitation in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, so they sent me to Khārijah ibn Zayd, and he said: Ubayy said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to prolong the standing and move his lips, so I know well that that was only for recitation, and I do the same."
"2683","One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated: The recitation of the Prophet ﷺ in Ẓuhr used to be recognized by the movement of his beard."
"2684","Yazīd ibn al-Barāʾ narrated: Ubayy said: Gather together so that I may show you how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution, and how he used to pray, for indeed I do not know how much longer my companionship with you will last. He said: So he gathered his sons and his household, and he called for water for ablution, and he rinsed his mouth, and cleaned his nose, and washed his face three times, and washed his right hand three times, and washed this one three times, meaning the left. Then he said: Like this, I have not fallen short in showing you how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution. Then he entered his house and prayed a Ṣalāh, we do not know what it was. Then he came out and ordered the Ṣalāh, so it was established, and he led us in Ẓuhr, and I believe I heard verses from Yā-Sīn from him. Then he prayed the ʿAṣr, then he led us in Maghrib, then he led us in ʿIshāʾ, then he said: I have not fallen short in showing you how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform ablution and how he used to pray."
"2685","Al-Barāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Ẓuhr, and we thought that he had recited the Tanzīl as-Sajdah."
"2686","ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Sukayn narrated: I came to Anas ibn Mālik and said: Inform me about the Ṣalāh of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So he led the people of his house and prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr with us, and he recited with us in a whispered recitation, reciting al-Mursalāt, and an-Nāziʿāt, and ʿAmma yatasāʾalūn, and similar sūrahs."
"2687","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."
"2688","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with them the midday prayer and raised his voice, reciting: By the sun and its brightness, and: By the night when it covers. So Ubayy ibn Kaʿb said to him: O Messenger of Allah, were you commanded something in this Ṣalāh? He said: > **""No, but I wanted to set the measure for you.""**"
"2689","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: The recitation of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to be recognized in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr by the movement of his beard."
"2690","Abū Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in all of them, meaning the four rakʿahs of Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr."
"2691","ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim رضي الله عنه led them in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr and recited about: When the sky splits open. When he finished the Ṣalāh he said: I have not fallen short with you from the Ṣalāh of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"2692","ʿIkrimah said: There is no recitation in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr except with the Umm al-Kitāb. So Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to recite, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed what was revealed to him from the messages of his Lord."
"2693","Ibn Sīrīn narrated that Ibn Masʿūd used to recite in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, in the first two rakʿahs the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb and a sūrah in each rakʿah, and in the last two the Fātiḥat al-Kitāb."
"2694","ʿAlqamah narrated: I prayed beside ʿAbdullāh, and I did not know that he recited anything until I heard him say: My Lord, increase me in knowledge. So I knew that he was reciting Ṭā-Hā."
"2695","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ziyād narrated: I heard the recitation of ʿAbdullāh in one of the two daytime prayers."
"2696","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ziyād narrated: I stood beside ʿAbdullāh in Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, and I heard him recite."
"2697","Ḥumayd and ʿUthmān al-Battī narrated: We prayed Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr behind Anas ibn Mālik, and we heard him recite: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High."
"2698","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه narrated: It was said: O Messenger of Allah, indeed here there are people who recite aloud in the daytime prayer? So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them: > **""Will you not pelt them with dung?""**"
"2699","From Abū Ayyūb, or from Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنهما, that the Prophet ﷺ recited in Maghrib al-Aʿrāf, dividing it over the two rakʿahs."
"2700","Marwān narrated: Zayd ibn Thābit said to me: Why is it that I see you reciting the short Mufaṣṣal in the Ṣalāh, when I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting the two longest? I said: And what are the two longest? He said: Al-Aʿrāf and Yūnus."
"2701","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه used to recite in the two rakʿahs of Maghrib Sūrat al-Anfāl."
"2702","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in Maghrib al-Anfāl."
"2703","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite with them in Maghrib: Those who disbelieved and barred from the path of Allah."
"2704","ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ recited in Maghrib: By the fig and the olive."
"2705","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib رضي الله عنه narrated: The last Ṣalāh the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed was Maghrib, and he recited in the first rakʿah: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and in the second: Say, O disbelievers."
"2706","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the last ʿIshāʾ: By the sky containing great stars, and: By the sky and the night comer."
"2707","In another narration from him, that the Prophet ﷺ commanded that the heavenly sūrahs be recited in ʿIshāʾ."
"2708","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated that Muʿādh ibn Jabal led his companions in the ʿIshāʾ prayer and recited in it: The Hour has drawn near. Then a man stood up before he finished, prayed alone, and left. So Muʿādh spoke harsh words to him. The man came to the Prophet ﷺ and excused himself to him, saying: Indeed, I was working among the date palms and I feared for the water. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Pray with: By the sun and its brightness, and similar sūrahs.""**"
"2709","ʿAbd ar-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd narrated: Ibn Masʿūd prayed the last ʿIshāʾ and opened with Sūrat al-Anfāl until he reached: How excellent a Patron and how excellent a Helper. Then he bowed, then he recited in the second rakʿah two sūrahs from the Mufaṣṣal, and in one narration: a sūrah from the Mufaṣṣal."
"2710","Simāk ibn Ḥarb narrated from a man from the people of Madīnah that he prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ and heard him recite in the Fajr prayer: Qāf, by the Glorious Qurʾān."
"2711","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the morning prayer Yā-Sīn."
"2712","In another narration from him, that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the morning prayer al-Wāqiʿah and similar sūrahs."
"2713","Al-Agharr al-Muzanī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited in the morning prayer Sūrat ar-Rūm."
"2714","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: I arrived in Madīnah while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was at Khaybar, and a man from Banū Ghifār was leading the people, and he recited in the first rakʿah Sūrat Maryam, and in the second: Woe to the defrauders. I believe he said: In the Fajr prayer."
"2715","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that there be recited in the morning prayer: By the night when it covers, and: By the sun and its brightness."
"2716","Rifāʿah al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Do not recite in the morning prayer less than ten verses, and do not recite in ʿIshāʾ less than ten verses.""**"
"2717","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه prayed Fajr in one of the mosques of Banū Asad, and their imām led them with the two longest sūrahs of the Mufaṣṣal according to the arrangement of ʿAbdullāh. When he finished the Ṣalāh he said: Do I not see that you are a young man, reciting these two sūrahs in this Ṣalāh while you are young."
"2718","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: The Prophet ﷺ prayed the Fajr prayer on a journey and recited: Say, O disbelievers, and: Say, He is Allah, the One. Then he said: > **""I have recited for you a third of the Qurʾān and a quarter of it.""**"
"2719","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The worst thieves among people is the one who steals from his Ṣalāh.""** They said: O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal from his Ṣalāh? He said: > **""He does not complete its rukūʿ and its sujūd, or he does not straighten his back in the rukūʿ nor in the sujūd.""**"
"2720","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the worst of people in theft is the one who steals his prayer."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal it?"" He ﷺ said: ""He does not complete its rukūʿ nor its sujūd.""**"
"2721","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the worst of people in theft is the one who steals from his prayer."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal from his prayer?"" He ﷺ said: ""He does not complete its rukūʿ nor its sujūd.""**"
"2722","ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The most thieving of people is the one who steals his prayer."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, how does he steal his prayer?"" He ﷺ said: ""He does not complete its rukūʿ nor its sujūd, and the most miserly of people is the one who is miserly with the greeting of peace.""**"
"2723","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah does not look at the prayer of a man who does not straighten his backbone in what is between his rukūʿ and his sujūd.""**"
"2724","Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥanafī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, does not look at the prayer of a servant who does not straighten his backbone in what is between its rukūʿ and its sujūd.""**"
"2725","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out and saw a man in the mosque who was not completing his rukūʿ nor his sujūd, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer of a man who does not complete his rukūʿ nor his sujūd is not accepted.""**"
"2726","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade that a man pray a prayer in which he does not complete its rukūʿ nor its sujūd.""**"
"2727","Hānī ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Ṣadafī narrated: ""I performed Hajj in the time of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه and sat in the mosque of the Prophet ﷺ, and there was a man narrating to them. He said: 'We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man came forward and prayed at this pillar, hastening before completing his prayer, then he left. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, if this one were to die, he would die having nothing of the religion. A man should lighten his prayer yet complete it.""** He said: ""So I asked about the man, who he was, and it was said to me: ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه."""
"2728","Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man who was not completing his rukūʿ and was pecking in his sujūd while he was praying, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If he were to die upon this state of his, he would die upon other than the religion of Muḥammad ﷺ.""** Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The parable of the one who does not complete his rukūʿ and pecks in his sujūd is the parable of the hungry one who eats one date or two dates; they avail him nothing.""** Abū Ṣāliḥ said: ""I said to Abū ʿAbdillāh: 'Who narrated this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' He said: 'The commanders of the armies—ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, Khālid ibn al-Walīd, and Shuraḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah—heard it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'"""
"2729","Bilāl رضي الله عنه saw a man who was not completing the rukūʿ nor the sujūd, so he said: > **""If this one were to die, he would die upon other than the religion of Muḥammad ﷺ.""**"
"2730","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to his Companions, while I was present: > **""If one of you owned this pillar, he would dislike being cheated regarding it, so how is it that one of you acts and then cheats in his prayer, which belongs to Allah? Complete your prayers, for indeed Allah accepts only what is complete.""**"
"2731","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade me from reciting while bowing, and he ﷺ said: > **""O ʿAlī, the parable of the one who does not straighten his backbone in his prayer is like the parable of a pregnant woman who carries, then when her time of delivery draws near she miscarries, so she is neither one with a pregnancy nor one with a child.""**"
"2732","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, when the servant prays and does not complete its humility nor its rukūʿ, and looks around a great deal, it is not accepted from him; and whoever drags his garment in arrogance, Allah will not look at him on the Day of Resurrection, even if he is noble before Allah.""**"
"2733","From Qatādah or another than him: Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه saw two men praying, one of them trailing his lower garment and the other not completing his rukūʿ nor his sujūd, so he laughed. They said: ""What makes you laugh, O Abā ʿAbd al-Raḥmān?"" He said: > **""I am amazed at these two men. As for the one trailing his lower garment, Allah will not look at him; and as for the other, Allah will not accept his prayer.""**"
"2734","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the servant performs ablution and perfects the ablution, then stands for prayer and completes its rukūʿ and its sujūd and the recitation therein, it says: 'May Allah preserve you as you have preserved me.' Then it is taken up with it to the heaven, while it has radiance and light, and the doors of heaven are opened for it. But when the servant does not perfect the ablution, nor complete the rukūʿ and sujūd and recitation, it says: 'May Allah waste you as you have wasted me.' Then it is taken up with it to the heaven while darkness is upon it, and the doors of heaven are locked; then it is wrapped up as a worn-out garment is wrapped, then the face of its doer is struck with it.""**"
"2735","Zayd ibn Jubayr narrated that Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما saw a youth praying, having lengthened his prayer and drawn it out at length, so he said: ""Who knows this one?"" A man said: ""I do."" So ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""Had I known him, I would have commanded him to lengthen the rukūʿ and the sujūd, for indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Indeed, when the servant stands to pray, his sins are brought and placed upon his head and his shoulders; every time he bows and prostrates, they fall away.""**"
"2736","ʿAlqamah narrated: ""I entered the mosque and found ʿAbdullāh praying, so he bowed, and I began Sūrat al-Aʿrāf and finished it before he prostrated."""
"2737","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bowed, he kept level, such that if water were poured upon his back it would settle.""**"
"2738","Abū Barzah al-Aslamī رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bowed, if water were poured upon his back it would settle.""**"
"2739","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""When the Prophet ﷺ bowed, if a cup of water were placed upon his back it would not spill.""**"
"2740","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated: > **""When the Prophet ﷺ bowed, if a cup of water were placed upon him it would settle, due to his straightness.""**"
"2741","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that when he ﷺ said ""Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,"" he would say: > **""O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs praise filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling what is between them and filling whatever You will of anything thereafter; O Possessor of praise and grandeur and glory. There is none to withhold what You have given, and the striving of the striver does not avail him against You.""**"
"2742","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""When the imām says 'Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,' let whoever is behind him say: 'Rabbanā laka al-ḥamd.'""**"
"2743","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""One day the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in prayer, and when he raised his head from rukūʿ he said: 'Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah.' So a man behind him said: 'Rabbanā wa laka al-ḥamd, kathīran ṭayyiban mubārakan fīh.' When the Prophet ﷺ finished, he said three times: > **""Who was the one speaking just now?""** The man said: ""I was, O Messenger of Allah."" He ﷺ said: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I saw more than thirty angels hastening toward it, which of them would record it first.""**"
"2744","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: > **""The imām is appointed only to be followed, so when he says the takbīr, say the takbīr; and when he bows, bow; and when he says 'Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,' say: 'Al-ḥamdu lillāh.'""**"
"2745","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed a prayer, and when he said 'Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,' a man behind him said: 'Allāhumma Rabbanā laka al-ḥamd, kathīran ṭayyiban mubārakan fīh.' When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, he ﷺ said: > **""Who is the one who said the word?""** The man said: ""I did, O Messenger of Allah."" He ﷺ said: > **""I saw a group of the angels surrounding it, then ascending with it until it disappeared from me.""**"
"2746","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: > **""As for me, I prostrate upon seven bones, and I do not tuck up hair nor garment.""**"
"2747","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""We were commanded to prostrate upon seven bones and not to tuck up hair nor garment.""**"
"2748","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه said: > **""The servant was commanded to prostrate upon seven parts of himself: his face, his two palms, his two knees, and his two feet; whichever of them he fails to place, then there is a deficiency to that extent.""**"
"2749","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Prostration is upon seven limbs.""**"
"2750","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I saw the whiteness of the side of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was prostrating."""
"2751","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, he kept his arms apart until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."""
"2752","Al-Barāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to prostrate upon the fleshy parts of the palms."""
"2753","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""It is as though I am still looking at the whiteness of the armpits of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he prostrated."""
"2754","ʿAdī ibn ʿUmayrah al-Ḥaḍramī رضي الله عنه narrated: ""When the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits could be seen; then when he gave the salām, he turned with his face to his right until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen, and to his left."""
"2755","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to prostrate upon his forehead along with the forelock of his hair."""
"2756","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrating upon the fold of the turban."""
"2757","ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Aqram narrated from his father from his grandfather, who said: ""I was herding sheep at al-Qāʿ in Namirah when I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ alight there. He established the prayer and prayed with his Companions, so I prayed with them, and it is as though I am still looking at the redness of what was beneath the shoulders of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was prostrating."""
"2758","Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated: ""One who saw Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated to me, saying: 'It is as though I am still looking at him while he was prostrating, keeping his elbows apart until I could almost see the whiteness of his armpits.'"""
"2759","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه passed by a man prostrating while his head was tied in a knot, so he untied it. When the man finished, ʿAbdullāh said to him: > **""Do not tie it up, for indeed the hair prostrates, and for you there is a reward for every hair."" He said: ""I only tied it so that it would not become dusty."" He said: ""That it become dusty is better for you.""**"
"2760","Kathīr ibn Sulaym narrated: ""I saw Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه prostrating upon his turban."""
"2761","Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""A man should not wipe his forehead until he finishes his prayer, and there is no harm in wiping sweat from his temples; for indeed the angels invoke blessings upon him as long as the trace of prostration remains between his eyes.""**"
"2762","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he ﷺ said: > **""Whoever does not press his nose to the ground along with his forehead when he prostrates, his prayer is not valid.""**"
"2763","Umm ʿAṭiyyah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah does not accept the prayer of one whose nose does not touch the ground.""**"
"2764","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prostrates, let him place his palms directly upon the ground; perhaps Allah will free him on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"2765","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated on a rainy day, such that I was indeed looking at the trace of that upon his forehead and the tip of his nose."""
"2766","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pressing his nose firmly to the ground just as he pressed his forehead."""
"2767","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you pray, do not spread your forearms as the beast of prey spreads them; rest upon your two palms and keep your elbows away from your sides.""**"
"2768","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to be balanced in sujūd and not to squat upon the heels with the feet erect."""
"2769","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: ""When one of you prostrates, let him not prostrate sprawled out nor sitting in tawarruk, for indeed when he perfects the sujūd, every limb in him prostrates."""
"2770","Al-Aʿmash narrated: ""I saw Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه praying in Makkah, and when he prostrated he kept apart until I saw the folds of his armpit."""
"2771","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: > **""The closest the servant is to his Lord is when he is prostrating.""**"
"2772","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: ""When there was revealed upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: {When the help of Allah comes and the victory} [al-Naṣr: 1], whenever he recited it he would say the takbīr and bow, and he would say: > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise. O Allah, forgive me; indeed, You are the Most Relenting, the Most Merciful.""**"
"2773","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, I have been forbidden from reciting in rukūʿ and sujūd; so when you bow, magnify Allah, and when you prostrate, strive in asking, for it is worthy that you be answered.""**"
"2774","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that she missed the Prophet ﷺ from his resting place, so she felt for him with her hand, and it fell upon him while he was prostrating and saying: > **""My Lord, grant my soul its piety and purify it; You are the best of those who purify it. You are its Guardian and its Master.""**"
"2775","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated: ""It was my night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he slipped away, and I thought he had slipped away to one of his wives, so I went out in jealousy—and behold, there he was, prostrating like a cast-off garment. I heard him saying: > **""My dark form and my shadow have prostrated to You, my heart has believed in You. My Lord, here is my hand and what I have committed against myself. O Tremendous One, hoped in for every tremendous matter, forgive the tremendous sin.""** She said: ""Then he raised his head and said: > **""What has brought you out?""** She said: 'A thought that I thought.' He ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, some thought is sin, so seek Allah's forgiveness. Jibrīl came to me and commanded me to say these words that you have heard, so say them in your prostration; for whoever says them will not raise his head until he is forgiven.""**"
"2776","Jubayr ibn Muṭʿim رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to say in his rukūʿ three times: > **""Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous,""** and in his sujūd three times: > **""Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.""**"
"2777","Abū Bakrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to glorify in his rukūʿ three times: > **""Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous,""** and in his sujūd three times: > **""Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.""**"
"2778","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, part of the Sunnah is that a man say in his rukūʿ three times: 'Glory be to my Lord, the Tremendous,' and in his sujūd three times: 'Glory be to my Lord, the Most High.'""**"
"2779","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: ""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated, he would say in his sujūd: > **""My dark form and my shadow have prostrated to You, my heart has believed in You. I acknowledge Your blessing upon me; here is my hand and what I have committed against myself.""**"
"2780","Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, and when he bowed he said three times: > **""Glory be to Allah and with His praise,""** then he raised his head."
"2781","Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه narrated: ""I observed the Prophet ﷺ closely and listened to him, and most of his prayer consisted of saying: > **""Glory be to the Lord of the worlds.""**"
"2782","ʿAbdullāh ibn Ziyād al-Asadī narrated that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه saying while bowing: > **""There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"2783","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd narrated: ""ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه would level the pebbles with his hand a single time when he intended to prostrate, and he would say in his sujūd: > **""Here I am at Your service, and blessedness is from You.""**"
"2784","Abū al-Aswad and Shaddād ibn al-Azmaʿ narrated from Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه: They differed, for Abū al-Aswad said: ""ʿAbdullāh would say in his sujūd: > **""Glory be to You, O Allah; there is no Lord but You,""** while Shaddād said: ""He would say: > **""Glory be to You; there is no god but You.""**"
"2785","Abū Mālik narrated from his father from the Prophet ﷺ that he ﷺ said: > **""There is no servant who prostrates and says: 'My Lord, forgive me,' three times, except that he is forgiven before he raises his head.""**"
"2786","ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say: > **""Place your needs upon the prescribed prayer.""**"
"2787","Abū Khālid, a man from among the companions of ʿAbdullāh, narrated: ""A man came to ʿAbdullāh and said: 'O Abā ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, so-and-so recites the Qurʾān while bowing and recites while prostrating.' So ʿAbdullāh said: > **""There are men who recite the Qurʾān and it does not pass beyond their collarbones; but when it enters the heart and becomes firmly rooted therein, it benefits.""**"
"2788","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ghanm narrated that Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه gathered his people and said: ""O company of Ashʿarīs, gather together, and gather your women and your children; I shall teach you the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ."" So they gathered, and they gathered their women and their children, and he showed them how he performed ablution, accounting for the ablution in its places, until when the shade declined and the shadow broke, he stood and called the adhān. He arranged the men in the nearest row, arranged the boys behind them, and arranged the women behind the boys. Then he established the prayer, stepped forward, raised his hands, and said the takbīr. He recited the Fātiḥah of the Book and a sūrah, saying them quietly. Then he said the takbīr and bowed, saying three times: ""Glory be to Allah and with His praise."" Then he said: ""Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,"" and stood upright. Then he said the takbīr and fell prostrating. Then he said the takbīr and raised his head. Then he said the takbīr and prostrated. Then he said the takbīr and rose standing. His takbīr in the first rakʿah was six takbīrs, and he said the takbīr when he stood for the second rakʿah. When he finished his prayer, he turned to his people with his face and said: > **""Memorize my takbīr and learn my rukūʿ and my sujūd, for indeed it is the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with which he used to pray for us at this hour of the day.""**"
"2789","In a narration from him: ""He prayed the Ẓuhr prayer, recited the Fātiḥah of the Book, and said twenty-two takbīrs."""
"2790","In another narration from him, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: He used to equalize the four rakʿahs in recitation and standing, and he would make the first rakʿah the longest of them so that the people might join; he would say the takbīr whenever he prostrated and whenever he bowed, and he would say the takbīr whenever he rose between the two rakʿahs after sitting."
"2791","Ibn al-Qāsim narrated: ""We sat with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā رضي الله عنه, and he said: 'Shall I not show you the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ?' He said: 'So we said: Yes indeed.' Then he stood, said the takbīr, recited, then bowed, placing his hands upon his knees until every bone took its place. Then he rose until every bone took its place. Then he prostrated until every bone took its place. Then he raised until every bone took its place. Then he prostrated until every bone took its place. Then he rose and did in the second rakʿah as he had done in the first rakʿah. Then he said: > **""Such is the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"2792","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated: ""A man asked the Prophet ﷺ about something concerning the prayer, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: > **""Interlace the fingers of your hands and your feet""—meaning the completion of ablution—and among what he said to him was: ""When you bow, place your palms upon your knees until you are tranquil, and when you prostrate, press your forehead firmly to the ground until you feel the hardness of the ground.""**"
"2793","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to say the takbīr whenever he lowered himself and whenever he rose."""
"2794","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated: ""The first to reduce the takbīr was al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah."" So ʿAbdullāh said: ""They reduced it; may Allah reduce them. > **""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying the takbīr whenever he bowed, whenever he prostrated, and whenever he rose.""**"
"2795","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه said: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reminded us of a prayer which we used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, which we had not forgotten — or which we had not abandoned. He would pronounce the takbīr when he bowed and when he raised his head from rukūʿ. > **""ʿAlī reminded us of a prayer which we used to pray with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ; he would pronounce the takbīr when he bowed and when he raised his head from rukūʿ.""**"
"2796","It was narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas — of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy, a trustworthy narrator — narrated to me, from Abū al-Qāsim Miqsam, the freedman of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal, who said: A man of the people of Madinah narrated to me, saying: I prayed in the mosque of Banū Ghifār, and when I sat in my prayer I spread out my left leg and sat, placed my left hand upon my left thigh, raised the front of my right foot upright, placed my right hand upon my right thigh, and raised my forefinger in indication. Khufāf ibn Īmāʾ ibn Raḥaḍah رضي الله عنه, who had companionship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saw me doing that, and when I finished my prayer he said: O my son, why did you raise your finger like this? I said to him: I saw the people doing that. He said: You have done rightly, for indeed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do that, while the polytheists used to say that Muḥammad works magic with his finger — and they lied; rather the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did that declaring the Oneness of his Lord, Mighty and Majestic, thereby. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to spread his left thigh in the middle and at the end of the prayer, sit upon his left hip, place his left hand upon his left thigh, keep his right foot upright, place his right hand upon his right thigh, and then raise his forefinger, declaring thereby the Oneness of his Lord, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"2797","Abū al-Zubayr said: I asked Jābir رضي الله عنه about prostration, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanding that we be upright and balanced in sujūd, and that a man should not prostrate while spreading out his forearms. > **""Be upright and balanced in sujūd, and let no man prostrate while spreading out his forearms.""**"
"2798","Samurah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to be balanced in sujūd and not to sit perched up on the toes. > **""Be balanced in sujūd and do not sit perched up.""**"
"2799","Aḥmad said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, saying: The people of Makkah say: Ibn Jurayj took the prayer from ʿAṭāʾ, ʿAṭāʾ took it from Ibn al-Zubayr, Ibn al-Zubayr took it from Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه, and Abū Bakr took it from the Prophet ﷺ. ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: I never saw anyone pray more beautifully than Ibn Jurayj."
"2800","ʿAdī ibn ʿUmayrah رضي الله عنه said: When the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits could be seen. Then when he gave the salām, he would turn with his face to his right until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen, then give salām to his left, turning with his face until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen from his left. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ prostrated, the whiteness of his armpits could be seen, and when he gave the salām he turned his face to the right and to the left until the whiteness of his cheek could be seen.""**"
"2801","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O Buraydah, when you begin the prayer, say: 'Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise; there is no power nor strength except through Allah; there is no god but You; I have wronged myself, so forgive me, for none forgives sins but You.' Then recite whatever is easy for you of the Qurʾān. When you bow, say 'Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great' three times. When you rise from rukūʿ, say: 'Allah hears the one who praises Him; O Allah, our Lord, to You belongs all praise, filling the heavens and filling the earth and filling whatever else You will after that.' When you prostrate, say 'Glory be to my Lord, the Most High' three times, and: 'My face has prostrated to the One who created it and fashioned its hearing and sight; blessed is Allah, the Best of creators.' When you rise from sujūd, say: 'My Lord, forgive me, have mercy upon me, guide me, and provide for me; indeed I am in need of whatever good You send down to me.' When you sit in your prayer, do not omit in the tashahhud: 'There is no god but Allah and I am the Messenger of Allah,' and sending blessings upon me and upon all the prophets of Allah, and peace upon the righteous servants of Allah.""**"
"2802","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنهما said: Muʿādh رضي الله عنه used to remain behind with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he came he would lead his people in prayer. A man of Banū Salamah called Sulaym used to pray with Muʿādh, but one night Muʿādh was detained from them, so Sulaym prayed alone and left. When Muʿādh came, he was told that Sulaym had prayed alone and departed, and Muʿādh reported that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent for Sulaym and asked him about it, and he said: I am a man who works through the day until evening comes and I grow drowsy; Muʿādh comes to us, though he had been delayed for us, and when he was detained from me I prayed and returned to my family. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked: > **""What did you do when you prayed?""** He said: I recited the Opening of the Book and a sūrah, then I sat, recited the tashahhud, asked for Paradise, sought refuge from the Fire, sent blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and then departed; I do not do well your murmured supplication nor the murmured supplication of Muʿādh. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ laughed and said: > **""Do I and Muʿādh murmur our supplication for anything but to enter Paradise and to be protected from the Fire?""** Then he sent word to Muʿādh: > **""Do not be a source of trial, putting the people to trial. Return to them and lead them in prayer before they fall asleep.""** Then Sulaym said: O Muʿādh, you shall see tomorrow, when we meet the enemy, how you fare and how I fare. On the day of Uḥud, Sulaym passed by brandishing his sword and said: O Muʿādh, advance! But Muʿādh did not advance, while Sulaym advanced and fought until he was killed. Whenever he was mentioned in the presence of Muʿādh, Muʿādh would say: Sulaym was true to Allah, and Muʿādh lied."
"2803","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ used to lead us in Ẓuhr when the sun had declined — even if one were to place his sides upon the scorching sand it would cook them — and he would prolong the first rakʿah, remaining standing and reciting for as long as the shuffling of the people's sandals could be heard. Then he would bow, then stand for the second and bow a rakʿah shorter than the first, then make the third rakʿah shorter than the second and the fourth shorter than the third. Then he would pray ʿAṣr while the sun was still white and clear, with time enough for a traveler to journey two or three farsakhs; he would prolong the first rakʿah of ʿAṣr and make the second shorter than the first. He would pray Maghrib when one might still be saying: Has the sun set or not? — prolonging the first rakʿah of Maghrib, making the second shorter than the first and the third shorter than the second. And he would delay the last ʿIshāʾ somewhat. > **""The Prophet ﷺ would prolong the first rakʿah of each prayer and shorten what followed it, pray ʿAṣr while the sun was still white and clear, pray Maghrib just after sunset, and delay the ʿIshāʾ somewhat.""**"
"2804","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Shall I not guide you to that by which Allah expiates sins and increases good deeds? Perfecting ablution despite hardship, taking many steps to the mosques, and waiting for one prayer after another. Indeed the angels say: 'O Allah, forgive him; O Allah, have mercy upon him.' When you stand for prayer, straighten your rows and establish them and close the gaps, for indeed I see you from behind my back. When the imām says 'Allāhu Akbar,' say 'Allāhu Akbar'; when he bows, bow; and when he says 'Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,' say 'Allāhumma Rabbanā laka al-ḥamd.'""** And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best rows of the men are the foremost of them and the worst are the rearmost, while the best rows of the women are the rearmost of them and the worst are the foremost.""**"
"2805","Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr رضي الله عنه said: I witnessed the Prophet ﷺ when a vessel containing water was brought to him. He poured water over his right hand three times, then immersed his right hand in the vessel and washed his left hand with it three times. Then he put his right hand into the water and scooped a handful, rinsed his mouth and sniffed water into his nose three times and blew it out three times. Then he put both his hands into the vessel, raised them to his face, and washed his face three times; he washed the inner part of his ears, inserting his fingers inside, and wiped the outer part of his neck and the underside of his beard three times. Then he put his right hand into the vessel and washed his right forearm with it beyond the elbow three times, then washed his left forearm with his right hand beyond the elbow three times. Then he wiped his head three times, the outer part of his ears three times, and the outer part of his neck — and I think he said — the outer part of his beard three times. Then he washed his right foot with his right hand three times, separating between the toes, raising the water beyond the ankle and up the shin, then did the same with the left. Then he took a handful of water, filled his hand with it, placed it upon his head until the water ran down from its sides, and said: > **""This is the complete ablution.""** Wāʾil said: I did not see him drying himself with a cloth. Then he rose toward the mosque and entered the miḥrāb — meaning the place of the miḥrāb — and the people formed rows behind him and to his right and left. He raised his hands until they were level with the lobes of his ears, then placed his right hand over his left at his chest, then began the recitation, reciting ""al-Ḥamd"" aloud. When he finished Sūrat al-Ḥamd he said ""Āmīn"" until those behind him heard, then he recited another sūrah. Then he raised his hands with the takbīr until they were level with the lobes of his ears, then bowed, placing his hands upon his knees, spreading his fingers, and paused in rukūʿ until he was balanced, so that his back — had a cup of water been placed upon it — would not spill. Then he raised his head ﷺ with reverence and said ""Samiʿa Allāhu liman ḥamidah,"" then raised his hands until they were level with the lobes of his ears, then went down into sujūd with the takbīr, raising his hands until they were level with the lobes of his ears. He fixed his forehead firmly upon the ground until I could see his nose in the dust, arched his forearms and head, spread his left thigh, kept the right upright as he fixed the toes of his feet, and did not linger in sujūd. He raised his head, raising his hands with the takbīr until they were level with the lobes of his ears, sat a light sitting, placing his right palm upon his knee and part of his thigh, forming a circle with his finger. Then he went down prostrating again likewise, then raised his head with the takbīr, his hands rising until they were level with the lobes of his ears, until he stood upright and every bone returned to its place. Then he prayed four rakʿahs doing in them as he had done in this one, then sat for the tashahhud likewise, then gave salām to his right until the whiteness of his left cheek could be seen, and to his left until the whiteness of his right cheek could be seen."
"2806","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in his prayer, he would raise his hands opposite his ears; when he pronounced the takbīr he would let them fall, then remain silent. Sometimes I saw him place his right hand over his left. When he finished the Opening of the Book he would pause, and when he completed the sūrah he would pause. Then he would raise his hands opposite his ears, pronounce the takbīr, and bow — and we would not bow until we saw him bowing. Then he would stand straight from rukūʿ until every limb returned to its place. Then he would raise his hands opposite his ears, pronounce the takbīr, and fall prostrate, pressing his forehead and nose firmly upon the ground. Then he would rise as though he were an arrow, not leaning upon his hands. When he sat at the end of his prayer, he would rest upon his left thigh, with his right hand upon his right thigh, gesturing with his finger when he supplicated. And when he gave the salām, he would rise swiftly. > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in his prayer, he would raise his hands opposite his ears, pause after the recitation, stand straight from rukūʿ until every limb returned to its place, press his forehead and nose firmly upon the ground in sujūd, rest upon his left thigh at the end of his prayer gesturing with his finger, and rise swiftly after the salām.""**"
"2807","Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bowed he would spread his fingers apart, and when he prostrated he would join his fingers together. > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bowed he spread his fingers apart, and when he prostrated he joined them together.""**"
"2808","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he would spread his fingers wide. > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he would spread his fingers wide.""**"
"2809","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: I never saw anyone whose prayer more resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than that of the son of Umm Sulaym — meaning Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه. > **""I never saw anyone whose prayer more resembled the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than that of Anas ibn Mālik.""**"
"2810","Samurah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us, when we were in prayer and raised our heads from sujūd, to settle ourselves seated upon the ground and not to remain perched upon the tips of the feet. > **""When you raise your heads from sujūd, settle yourselves seated upon the ground and do not remain perched upon the tips of the feet.""**"
"2811","Samurah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade sitting in the manner of iqʿāʾ during prayer. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade iqʿāʾ in prayer.""**"
"2812","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd said: I observed ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه in prayer and saw him rise without sitting — he would rise upon the fronts of his feet in the first and third rakʿahs."
"2813","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The first thing to be removed from this Ummah is reverent humility [khushūʿ], until you will see no humble person among them.""**"
"2814","Shaddād ibn Aws رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The first thing to be removed from the people is reverent humility.""**"
"2815","Abū ʿUbaydah said: ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه, when he stood for prayer, would lower his voice, his hands, and his gaze therein."
"2816","Al-Aʿmash said: When ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه prayed, he was as though he were a garment cast down."
"2817","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: Observe dignity in the prayer — that is, be still and tranquil."
"2818","ʿAṭāʾ said: When Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما prayed, he was as though he were a solid block, utterly still."
"2819","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never recited the qunūt in any of the prayers at all except in the witr. But when he was at war, he would recite the qunūt in all the prayers, supplicating against the polytheists. Neither Abū Bakr, nor ʿUmar, nor ʿUthmān رضي الله عنهم ever recited the qunūt until they died. Nor did ʿAlī رضي الله عنه recite the qunūt until he fought the people of Syria, when he would recite the qunūt in all the prayers, while Muʿāwiyah would supplicate against him likewise — each of the two supplicating against the other. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never recited the qunūt in any of the prayers except in the witr, but when he was at war he would recite the qunūt in all of them, supplicating against the polytheists.""**"
"2820","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the qunūt for a month, supplicating against ʿUṣayyah and Dhakwān; then, when he prevailed over them, he abandoned the qunūt. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the qunūt for a month supplicating against ʿUṣayyah and Dhakwān, then abandoned it when he prevailed over them.""**"
"2821","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: Do you suppose that your standing when the imām finishes the sūrah is the qunūt? By Allah, it is an innovation. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never did it except for a single month, then abandoned it. And do you suppose that your raising your hands in prayer is correct? By Allah, it is an innovation. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never raised beyond this — and he raised his hands to the level of his shoulders. > **""The qunūt after the sūrah is an innovation; the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did it only for a month and then abandoned it. And raising the hands beyond the shoulders in prayer is an innovation.""**"
"2822","Abū Mijlaz said: I prayed behind Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما and he did not recite the qunūt. I said: What prevented you from the qunūt? He said: I do not recall it from any of my companions."
"2823","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه would not recite the qunūt in the dawn prayer, and when he recited the qunūt in the witr he would recite it before the rakʿah of bowing."
"2824","And in a narration from him also: ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه would not recite the qunūt in any of the prayers except in the witr, before the rukūʿ."
"2825","ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه would pronounce the takbīr when he finished the recitation, and when he finished the qunūt he would pronounce the takbīr and bow."
"2826","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the qunūt in the Fajr prayer, supplicating against one people and supplicating for another. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the qunūt in the Fajr prayer, supplicating against one people and supplicating for another.""**"
"2827","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Bakr said: ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah, Salamah ibn Hishām, and al-Walīd ibn al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah fled from the polytheists toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — ʿAyyāsh and Salamah riding together upon one camel with al-Walīd driving them on — when the finger of al-Walīd was wounded, and he said: ""Are you but a finger that has bled, and what you met was in the path of Allah."" The Prophet ﷺ came to know of their setting out toward him and of their affair before we knew, so he prayed the morning prayer, bowed the first rakʿah of it, and when he raised his head he supplicated for them before prostrating, saying: > **""O Allah, save ʿAyyāsh ibn Abī Rabīʿah; O Allah, save Salamah ibn Hishām; O Allah, save al-Walīd ibn al-Walīd; O Allah, save the oppressed among the believers; O Allah, make Your grip severe upon Muḍar, and make those years upon them like the years of Yūsuf.""**"
"2828","Khufāf ibn Īmāʾ ibn Raḥaḍah al-Ghifārī رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ led us in Fajr, and when he raised his head from the last rakʿah he said: > **""O Allah, curse Liḥyān, Riʿl, Dhakwān, and ʿUṣayyah, who disobeyed Allah and His Messenger. Aslam — may Allah grant it peace — and Ghifār — may Allah forgive it.""** Then he fell prostrate. When he completed the prayer, he turned to the people with his face and said: > **""O people, it is not I who said this; rather Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said it.""**"
"2829","Al-Barāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would not pray any prescribed prayer without reciting the qunūt therein. > **""The Prophet ﷺ would not pray any prescribed prayer without reciting the qunūt therein.""**"
"2830","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I recite the qunūt only so that you may supplicate to your Lord and ask Him for your needs.""**"
"2831","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say in his supplication: > **""O Allah, guide me among those You have guided, grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, take me into Your care among those You have taken into Your care, bless for me what You have granted me, and shield me from the evil of what You have decreed. For indeed You decree and none decrees against You, and indeed none whom You befriend is ever humiliated. Blessed are You, our Lord, and Exalted.""**"
"2832","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade the qunūt in the ʿIshāʾ prayer. > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade the qunūt in the ʿIshāʾ prayer.""**"
"2833","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ would pray the witr as three rakʿahs, placing the qunūt before the rukūʿ. > **""The Prophet ﷺ would pray the witr as three rakʿahs, placing the qunūt before the rukūʿ.""**"
"2834","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited the qunūt in the morning prayer after the rukūʿ. He said: I heard him supplicating in his qunūt against the disbelievers, and I heard him saying: > **""Make their hearts like the hearts of disbelieving women.""**"
"2835","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued to recite the qunūt in Fajr until he departed from this world. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ continued to recite the qunūt in Fajr until he departed from this world.""**"
"2836","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the qunūt until he died, and Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه until he died, and ʿUmar رضي الله عنه until he died. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the qunūt until he died, and Abū Bakr until he died, and ʿUmar until he died.""**"
"2837","Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the polytheists in prayer, he would begin with Quraysh and then follow them with many tribes of the Arabs. It was said to him: Curse the disbelievers of Quraysh. So whenever the Prophet ﷺ intended to curse a tribe, he would say: > **""O Allah, curse the disbelievers of Banū so-and-so.""**"
"2838","Abū al-Zubayr reported from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach them the tashahhud just as he would teach them a sūrah of the Qurʾān. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us the tashahhud just as he would teach us a sūrah of the Qurʾān.""**"
"2839","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""In every two rakʿahs there is a tashahhud, and salutations of peace upon the messengers and upon those righteous servants of Allah who followed them.""**"
"2840","Maymūnah رضي الله عنها said: When the Prophet ﷺ sat, he would settle himself firmly upon his left thigh. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ sat, he would settle himself firmly upon his left thigh.""**"
"2841","ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه — said: For one of you to sit upon two hot stones is better for him than to sit cross-legged in prayer. ʿAbd al-Razzāq said, explaining: When one prays standing, he should not sit cross-legged for the tashahhud; but when he prays sitting, let him sit cross-legged."
"2842","Usāmah ibn Ḥārithah رضي الله عنه said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ placing his hand — as I recall, upon his thigh — gesturing with his finger. > **""I saw the Prophet ﷺ placing his hand upon his thigh, gesturing with his finger.""**"
"2843","Khufāf ibn Īmāʾ ibn Raḥaḍah al-Ghifārī رضي الله عنه said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat at the end of his prayer, he would gesture with his forefinger; the polytheists used to say that he works magic with it — and they lied; rather it is the declaration of Oneness. > **""When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat at the end of his prayer, he would gesture with his forefinger; it is the declaration of Oneness, not magic.""**"
"2844","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه said: When the polytheists entered Makkah they would say to their idols: ""Greetings and goodness to you."" So Allah revealed to His Prophet ﷺ: Say: ""Greetings belong to Allah, and good and pure things belong to Allah."" > **""Say: Greetings belong to Allah, and good and pure things belong to Allah.""**"
"2845","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Prophet ﷺ used to teach the people the tashahhud from the minbar, just as a teacher teaches young boys. > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to teach the people the tashahhud from the minbar, just as a teacher teaches young boys.""**"
"2846","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ used to do thus in his prayer — and he gestured with his forefinger. > **""The Prophet ﷺ used to gesture with his forefinger in his prayer.""**"
"2847","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā reported from his father رضي الله عنه that when the Prophet ﷺ supplicated in prayer, he would place his hand upon his thigh and then signal with his finger thus, lowering his little finger and the one next to it. > **""When the Prophet ﷺ supplicated in prayer, he would place his hand upon his thigh and signal with his finger, lowering his little finger and the one next to it.""**"
"2848","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There is no prayer for one who has no tashahhud.""**"
"2849","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ used to teach us the tashahhud just as he would teach us a sūrah of the Qurʾān, and he would say: > **""Learn it, for there is no prayer without the tashahhud.""**"
"2850","Nāfiʿ reported that Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, when he prayed, would gesture with his finger and follow it with his gaze, and he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is indeed harder upon Satan than iron.""**"
"2851","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite the tashahhud in prayer. The narrators said: We asked: Do you preserve it from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as you preserve the letters of the Qurʾān — its wāws and alifs — when he sat upon his left hip? > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite the tashahhud in prayer when he sat upon his left hip, and we preserved it from him as we preserve the letters of the Qurʾān.""**"
"2852","Al-Aswad said: ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنه used to teach us the tashahhud in prayer, holding us to the exact alif and wāw."
"2853","Jarīr ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us the tashahhud and the takbīr just as he would teach us a sūrah of the Qurʾān. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us the tashahhud and the takbīr just as he would teach us a sūrah of the Qurʾān.""**"
"2854","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you is in the mosque, let him not raise his voice to another, but gesture with his finger toward his Lord, Blessed and Exalted.""**"
"2855","Khālid al-Ḥadhdhāʾ said: I taught Ibn Sīrīn the tashahhud, narrating it to him from Abū Naḍrah from Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه; so he adopted my tashahhud and abandoned his own."
"2856","ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught him: ""Greetings, prayers, good and pure and blessed things belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger."" > **""Greetings, prayers, good and pure and blessed things belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger.""**"
"2857","Al-Bahzī said: I asked al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنه about the tashahhud of ʿAlī رضي الله عنه. He said: It is the tashahhud of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. I said: Then what of the tashahhud of ʿAbdullāh? He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved to lighten matters for his Ummah. I said: How did ʿAlī recite the tashahhud of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ? He said: > **""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good, pure, ever-recurring, wholesome, blessed, clean things, and abundant, ample favours — all belong to Allah.""**"
"2858","Abū al-Ward reported that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما say: The tashahhud of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ which he would recite was: ""In the name of Allah and through Allah, the best of names. Greetings, good and pure things, and prayers belong to Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger, whom He sent with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner; and that the Hour is coming, no doubt therein. Peace be upon you, O noble Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. O Allah, forgive me and guide me"" — in the first two rakʿahs. > **""In the name of Allah and through Allah, the best of names. Greetings, good and pure things, and prayers belong to Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger, whom He sent with the truth as a bearer of glad tidings and a warner; and that the Hour is coming, no doubt therein. Peace be upon you, O noble Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. O Allah, forgive me and guide me.""**"
"2859","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not add anything beyond the tashahhud in the first two rakʿahs. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not add anything beyond the tashahhud in the first two rakʿahs.""**"
"2860","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me the tashahhud in the middle of the prayer and at its end. When he sat in the middle of the prayer and at its end upon his left hip, he would say: ""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good and pure things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger."" Then, if he was in the middle of the prayer, he would rise when he finished his tashahhud; and if he was at the end of it, he would supplicate after his tashahhud with whatever supplication Allah willed, and then give the salām. > **""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good and pure things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger.""**"
"2861","And he narrated it with another chain, adding after the words ""and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger"": When you have completed this — or he said: when you have done this — you have completed your prayer; then if you wish, stand, and if you wish, remain seated. Al-Ṭabarānī clarified that these words, ""when you finish this you have completed your prayer,"" are the statement of Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه himself. > **""When you have completed this, you have completed your prayer; then if you wish, stand, and if you wish, remain seated.""**"
"2862","Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr said: Abū ʿUbaydah ibn ʿAbdillāh wrote to me: To proceed, I inform you of the guidance of Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه in his word and deed in prayer. He said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was granted the most comprehensive of speech, and he would teach us what to say in prayer when we sit: ""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good and pure things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger. Then ask thereafter for whatever occurs to you, seeking His mercy and forgiveness with brief words, and do not prolong the sitting thereby."" And he would say: I love that your asking of Allah, when one of you sits in prayer and completes the salutation, be that he say thereafter: Glory be to You; there is no god besides You. Forgive my sin and set right my deeds, for indeed You forgive sins for whom You will, and You are the Most Forgiving, the Most Merciful. O Most Forgiving, forgive me; O Most Relenting, relent toward me; O Most Merciful, have mercy upon me; O Pardoner, pardon me; O Most Kind, show kindness to me. My Lord, inspire me to give thanks for Your favour which You bestowed upon me, and gird me with excellence in Your worship. My Lord, I ask You for all good and seek refuge in You from all evil. My Lord, open for me with good, seal for me with good, and grant me longing to meet You without harmful affliction or misleading trial. Shield me from evil deeds, for whomever You shield from evil deeds on that Day, You have indeed had mercy upon him — and that is the supreme triumph. Then whatever your supplication be, let it be in humility and sincerity, for indeed He loves the humble entreaty of His servant to Him. > **""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good and pure things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger.""**"
"2863","Al-Shaʿbī said: Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, after saying ""Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings,"" would say: Peace upon us from our Lord."
"2864","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: Among the supplications of the Prophet ﷺ after the tashahhud in the obligatory prayer was: ""O Allah, we ask You for all good, its sooner and its later, what we know thereof and what we do not know; and we seek refuge in You from all evil, its sooner and its later, what we know thereof and what we do not know. O Allah, we ask You for whatever Your righteous servants asked You, and we seek refuge in You from whatever Your righteous servants sought refuge from. Our Lord, grant us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and shield us from the punishment of the Fire. Our Lord, we have believed, so forgive us our sins, expiate our misdeeds, and cause us to die with the righteous. Our Lord, grant us what You promised us through Your messengers, and do not disgrace us on the Day of Resurrection; indeed You never fail the promise."" Then he would give salām to his right and to his left. > **""O Allah, we ask You for all good, its sooner and its later, what we know thereof and what we do not know; and we seek refuge in You from all evil, its sooner and its later, what we know thereof and what we do not know. O Allah, we ask You for whatever Your righteous servants asked You, and we seek refuge in You from whatever Your righteous servants sought refuge from. Our Lord, grant us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and shield us from the punishment of the Fire. Our Lord, we have believed, so forgive us our sins, expiate our misdeeds, and cause us to die with the righteous. Our Lord, grant us what You promised us through Your messengers, and do not disgrace us on the Day of Resurrection; indeed You never fail the promise.""**"
"2865","Abū Rāshid said: I asked Salmān al-Fārisī رضي الله عنه about the tashahhud, and he said: I shall teach you just as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught it to me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught me the tashahhud letter by letter: ""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good and pure things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger."" > **""Greetings belong to Allah, as do prayers and good and pure things. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, without partner, and I bear witness that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger.""**"
"2866","ʿAbdullāh ibn Bābay said: I prayed beside Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, and when he had prayed he struck my thigh with his hand and said: Shall I not teach you the greeting of the prayer just as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to teach us? Then he recited these words: ""Greetings, prayers, and good and pure things belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah"" — and he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth. > **""Greetings, prayers, and good and pure things belong to Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah.""**"
"2867","Anas رضي الله عنه said: ""I bear witness that Allah is True, that meeting Him is true, that Paradise is true and the Fire is true. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the trial of the Dajjāl, from the trial of life and death, from the punishment of the grave and the punishment of Hell."" Abū Khaythamah said: As though he meant it from the Prophet ﷺ. > **""I bear witness that Allah is True, that meeting Him is true, that Paradise is true and the Fire is true. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the trial of the Dajjāl, from the trial of life and death, from the punishment of the grave and the punishment of Hell.""**"
"2868","A man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ used to say: ""O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad, upon the people of his house, upon his wives and his offspring, as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrāhīm; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muḥammad, the people of his house, his wives and his offspring, as You blessed the family of Ibrāhīm; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious."" Ibn Ṭāwūs said: And my father used to say the same. > **""O Allah, send blessings upon Muḥammad, upon the people of his house, upon his wives and his offspring, as You sent blessings upon the family of Ibrāhīm; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And bless Muḥammad, the people of his house, his wives and his offspring, as You blessed the family of Ibrāhīm; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.""**"
"2869","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, we have learned how to greet you with peace, so how do we send blessings upon you? He said: > **""Say: O Allah, bestow Your prayers, Your mercy, and Your blessings upon Muḥammad and upon the family of Muḥammad, as You bestowed them upon the family of Ibrāhīm; indeed You are Praiseworthy, Glorious.""**"
"2870","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that they asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ how they should send Ṣalāh upon him, and he said: > **""Say: O Allah, send Ṣalāh upon Muḥammad, and bless Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, as You sent Ṣalāh upon and blessed the family of Ibrāhīm. Indeed, You are Praiseworthy, Glorious. And the salām is as you already know.""**"
"2871","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Send abundant Ṣalāh upon me, for indeed it is a purification for you.""**"
"2872","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught him: > **""At-taḥiyyātu lillāhi waṣ-ṣalawātu waṭ-ṭayyibāt. As-salāmu ʿalayka ayyuhā an-nabiyyu wa raḥmatullāhi wa barakātuh. As-salāmu ʿalaynā wa ʿalā ʿibādillāhiṣ-ṣāliḥīn. Ashhadu an lā ilāha illallāh, wa ashhadu anna Muḥammadan ʿabduhū wa rasūluh. Allāhumma ṣalli ʿalā Muḥammadin wa ahli baytihī kamā ṣallayta ʿalā Ibrāhīm, innaka ḥamīdun majīd. Allāhumma ṣalli ʿalaynā maʿahum. Allāhumma bārik ʿalā Muḥammadin wa ʿalā ahli baytihī kamā bārakta ʿalā Ibrāhīm, innaka ḥamīdun majīd. Allāhumma bārik ʿalaynā maʿahum. Ṣalawātullāhi wa ṣalawātul-muʾminīna ʿalā Muḥammadin an-nabiyyi al-ummiyy. As-salāmu ʿalayhi wa raḥmatullāhi wa barakātuh.""**"
"2873","Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give salām in his Ṣalāh to his right and to his left, until the whiteness of his cheeks could be seen."
"2874","Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to give salām to his right and to his left, until the whiteness of his right cheek and the whiteness of his left cheek could be seen."
"2875","A Bedouin narrated from his father that he prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and he gave two salāms, to his right and to his left."
"2876","Bisṭām narrated from a Bedouin who had stayed with them as a guest that he prayed with the Prophet ﷺ, and he gave two salāms."
"2877","Wāsiʿ ibn Ḥabbān narrated that he was standing praying in the mosque while Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما was facing him, leaning his back against the Qiblah wall of the mosque. When Wāsiʿ finished, he left from his left toward Ibn ʿUmar and sat beside him, so Ibn ʿUmar said to him, ""What prevented you from leaving to your right?"" He said, ""Nothing, except that I saw you, so I came toward you."" Ibn ʿUmar then said, ""You have done well. Some people say: when you pray and then leave, leave to your right."" Ibn ʿUmar said: > **""When you pray and then leave, leave, if you wish, to your right, and if you wish, to your left.""**"
"2878","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ, and Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما, used to begin the recitation aloud with ""Al-ḥamdu lillāhi rabbi l-ʿālamīn,"" and they would give one salām."
"2879","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to give salām to his right and to his left: > **""As-salāmu ʿalaykum wa raḥmatullāh, as-salāmu ʿalaykum wa raḥmatullāh.""**"
"2880","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to give two salāms."
"2881","Abū Rimthah رضي الله عنه narrated that he witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray and then give salām to his right and to his left, until the brightness of his cheeks was seen."
"2882","Al-ʿAbbās ibn Sahl ibn Saʿd narrated that he was in a gathering in which his father was present, along with Abū Hurayrah, Abū Usayd, and Abū Ḥumayd رضي الله عنهم, and they recalled the Ṣalāh of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and they mentioned that he gave salām to his right and to his left."
"2883","Aws ibn Aws رضي الله عنه narrated that they stayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for half a month, and he saw him turn away to his right and saw him turn away to his left, and he saw that his sandals had two straps."
"2884","Asmāʾ ibn Ḥārithah رضي الله عنه narrated that he saw the Prophet ﷺ leave from his left toward his house."
"2885","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه narrated that when the Prophet ﷺ gave salām to them at the end of the Ṣalāh, they would say: > **""Wa ʿalaykumu as-salām wa raḥmatullāhi wa barakātuh.""**"
"2886","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that he prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and as soon as he gave salām he would stand up. Then he prayed with Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه, and when he gave salām he leapt up as though rising from hot embers."
"2887","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that when the imām gives salām and a man has some need, he should not wait for him to turn to face the people after giving salām, for indeed the separation of the Ṣalāh is the taslīm. And ʿAbdullāh himself, when he gave salām, would not remain seated but would stand up, or move from his place, or face the people."
"2888","Ghālib ibn Farqad narrated that Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه used to give salām to his right and to his left: ""As-salāmu ʿalaykum wa raḥmatullāh."""
"2889","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: > **""I only accept the Ṣalāh of one who humbles himself through it before My greatness, and does not exalt himself over My creation, and does not persist overnight in disobedience to Me, and spends his day in My remembrance, and shows mercy to the poor, the wayfarer, the widow, and the afflicted. His light is like the light of the sun. I guard him with My might and entrust him to My angels. I make for him light in darkness and forbearance in ignorance, and his likeness among My creation is like the likeness of al-Firdaws in Paradise.""**"
"2890","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The Ṣalāh is three thirds: purification is a third, rukūʿ is a third, and sujūd is a third. Whoever performs it with its due right, it is accepted from him, and the rest of his deeds are accepted from him. And whoever has his Ṣalāh rejected, the rest of his deeds are rejected along with it.""**"
"2891","Abū Hārūn narrated that they asked Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه whether he had memorized anything the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say after giving salām, and he said yes, he used to say: > **""Subḥāna rabbika rabbi l-ʿizzati ʿammā yaṣifūn, wa salāmun ʿalā al-mursalīn, wa al-ḥamdu lillāhi rabbi l-ʿālamīn.""**"
"2892","Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites Āyat al-Kursī after the prescribed Ṣalāh will remain under the protection of Allah until the next Ṣalāh.""**"
"2893","Abū Umāmah al-Bāhilī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When the Ṣalāh is established, the gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered. When one who has finished the Ṣalāh leaves it without saying: O Allah, save me from the Fire, admit me into Paradise, and marry me to the ḥūr al-ʿīn, the Fire says: Woe to this one, was he unable to seek refuge with Allah from Hell? And Paradise says: Woe to this one, was he unable to ask Allah for Paradise? And the ḥūr al-ʿīn say: Was he unable to ask Allah to marry him to the ḥūr al-ʿīn?""**"
"2894","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a sick man, and he was with him, and he saw him praying and prostrating upon a pillow, so he forbade him and said: > **""If you are able to prostrate upon the ground, then prostrate. Otherwise, gesture with a gesture, and make the sujūd lower than the rukūʿ.""**"
"2895","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a man among his Companions who was ill, and he was with him. He entered upon him while he was praying upon a stick, placing his forehead upon the stick, so he gestured to him, and he threw the stick aside and took a pillow. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then said: > **""Leave it aside. If you are able to prostrate upon the ground, then do so. Otherwise, gesture with a gesture, and make your sujūd lower than your rukūʿ.""**"
"2896","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever among you is able to prostrate, let him prostrate. And whoever is not able, let him not raise anything to his forehead to prostrate upon it. Rather, his rukūʿ and his sujūd are to be done with a gesture.""**"
"2897","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The sick person prays standing. If hardship befalls him, he prays sitting. If hardship befalls him, he prays lying down, gesturing with his head. And if hardship befalls him, he glorifies Allah.""**"
"2898","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed the prescribed prayer seated upon the ground, and he sat for the supererogatory prayer upon the ground, gesturing with gestures."
"2899","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه entered upon his brother ʿUtbah while he was praying upon a siwāk stick which he would raise to his face, so he took it and threw it aside, then said, ""Gesture with a gesture, and let your bowing be higher than your prostration."""
"2900","Ibrāhīm narrated that ʿAlqamah and al-Aswad entered upon ʿAbdullāh and said that the mother of al-Aswad is seated for prayer and a fan-handle stick is fixed upright for her upon which she prostrates, so what is his view? He said, ""I consider Satan to be presenting himself through the stick. Let her prostrate upon the ground if she is able; otherwise, let her gesture with a gesture."""
"2901","Al-Mukhtār narrated that he asked Anas رضي الله عنه about the prayer of the sick person, and he said that he bows and prostrates while seated in the prescribed prayer."
"2902","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""The prayer of one seated is half of the prayer of one standing.""**"
"2903","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated in marfūʿ form: > **""The prayer of one seated is half of the prayer of one standing.""**"
"2904","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Sāʾib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer of one sitting is half of the prayer of one standing.""**"
"2905","Al-Muṭṭalib ibn Abī Wadāʿah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man praying seated, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer of one seated is half of the prayer of one standing.""** So the people undertook standing with effort."
"2906","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr رضي الله عنه narrated that he came to the Prophet ﷺ while he was praying, sometimes seated and sometimes standing."
"2907","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه narrated that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I prayed and did not know whether I prayed an even number or an odd number."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Beware lest Satan play with you in your Ṣalāh. Whoever among you prays and does not know whether he prayed an even number or an odd number, let him prostrate twice, for they complete his Ṣalāh.""**"
"2908","ʿAṭāʾ narrated that Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما prayed Maghrib and gave salām after two rakʿahs, then rose to touch the Black Stone, so the people said ""Subḥān Allāh."" He said, ""What is the matter with you?"" Then he prayed what remained and prostrated twice. That was mentioned to Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما, and he said, ""He did not deviate from the Sunnah of his Prophet ﷺ."""
"2909","Maʿdī ibn Sulaymān narrated that he came to Muṭayr to ask him about the ḥadīth of Dhū al-Yadayn. He came to him and asked him, but he was a very old man who could not convey the ḥadīth fully due to old age. His son Shuʿayb said, ""Yes, father, you told me that you met Dhū al-Yadayn at Dhū Khushub, and he told you that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with them one of the two evening prayers — and it was ʿAṣr — two rakʿahs, then gave salām. The hasty people went out, and among the people were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما. Dhū al-Yadayn said, 'Has the Ṣalāh been shortened, or did you forget?' He said, 'It has neither been shortened nor have I forgotten.' Then he turned to Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما and said, 'What does Dhū al-Yadayn say?' They said, 'He has spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah.' So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned, the people gathered, he prayed two rakʿahs with them, then gave salām, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."""
"2910","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated that Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه led them in prayer and rose after two rakʿahs, so they said ""Subḥān Allāh"" to him, but he continued standing. He went on in his standing until he finished, then said, ""Did you think I should sit? I only did as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do."""
"2911","Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated that Saʿd ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه led them in prayer, then he narrated something similar to the ḥadīth of Abū Muʿāwiyah, but he did not mention the Prophet ﷺ."
"2912","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The two prostrations of forgetfulness suffice for every addition and omission.""**"
"2913","Abū al-Malīḥ ibn Usāmah narrated from his father that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I complain to you of a whispering I find in my chest. I enter my Ṣalāh and do not know whether I finish upon an even number or upon an odd number."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you find that, raise your right forefinger and strike it into your left thigh, and say: Bismillāh, for indeed it is a knife to Satan.""**"
"2914","Maymūnah bint Saʿd رضي الله عنها narrated that she said, ""O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling concerning a man who forgets in his Ṣalāh and does not know how much he has prayed."" He said: > **""Let him not finish, but let him remain standing in his Ṣalāh until he knows how much he has prayed, for that is only whispering that presents itself and makes him forgetful of his Ṣalāh.""**"
"2915","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ prayed with them the ʿAṣr or Ẓuhr prayer and stood up after two rakʿahs, so they said ""Subḥān Allāh"" to him, but he continued in his Ṣalāh. When he completed the Ṣalāh, he prostrated twice and then gave salām."
"2916","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed ʿAṣr with them as three rakʿahs, then entered upon one of his wives. A man among his Companions called Dhū al-Shimālayn entered upon him and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, has the Ṣalāh been decreased?"" He said: > **""What is that?""** He said, ""You prayed three."" So he stood, took him by his hand, and went out to the people who had prayed with him, and said: > **""Is what Dhū al-Yadayn says true?""** They said, ""What is that, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: > **""He claims that I prayed three.""** They said, ""He has spoken the truth; we thought you had been commanded with some command concerning it."" So he prayed the rakʿah with them and prostrated twice after the tashahhud."
"2917","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed three rakʿahs with them, then gave salām. Dhū al-Shimālayn said to him, ""Has the Ṣalāh been decreased, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: > **""Is it so, O Dhā al-Yadayn?""** He said, ""Yes."" So he performed a rakʿah and two prostrations, and two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2918","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed ʿAṣr as five rakʿahs, then prostrated the two prostrations of error while seated."
"2919","Abū Khaldah narrated that he asked Ibn Sīrīn, saying, ""I pray and do not know whether I prayed two rakʿahs or four?"" He said that Abū al-ʿUryān told him that the Prophet ﷺ prayed one day and then entered the house. Among the people was a long-handed man whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to call Dhā al-Yadayn. Dhū al-Yadayn said, ""O Messenger of Allah, has the Ṣalāh been shortened, or did you forget?"" He said: > **""It has neither been shortened nor have I forgotten.""** He said, ""Rather, you forgot the prayer."" So he went forward and prayed two rakʿahs with them, then gave salām, then said takbīr and prostrated like his usual prostration or longer, then said takbīr and raised his head. Muḥammad did not remember whether he gave salām afterward or not."
"2920","ʿAbdullāh, meaning Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه, narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed with them, then entered his house. Some of the people said, ""Has something been added to the Ṣalāh?"" He said: > **""What is that?""** He said, ""You prayed five."" So he took him by his hand and went out to the mosque, where there was a circle in which were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما. He said: > **""Is what Dhū al-Yadayn says true?""** They said, ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" So he faced the Qiblah and then prostrated twice."
"2921","Ibn Masʿadah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr and gave salām after two rakʿahs. Dhū al-Yadayn said to him, ""Has the Ṣalāh been shortened, or did you forget?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""What does Dhū al-Yadayn say?""** They said, ""He has spoken the truth."" So he completed the two rakʿahs with them, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness while seated after giving salām."
"2922","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ did not prostrate on the day of Dhū al-Yadayn."
"2923","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about a man who forgets in his Ṣalāh and does not know how much he has prayed. He said: > **""Let him repeat his Ṣalāh, and let him prostrate twice while seated.""**"
"2924","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that she complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about forgetfulness in Ṣalāh. He said: > **""When you pray and see that you have completed your Ṣalāh while you are in doubt, then recite the tashahhud and finish, then prostrate twice while seated, then recite the tashahhud between them, and then finish.""**"
"2925","Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī narrated that Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه and his companions left Makkah, and he prayed Maghrib with them as two rakʿahs, then gave salām. Then he stood, recited three verses from al-Nisāʾ, then bowed, prostrated, and gave salām, attributing it to the Prophet ﷺ."
"2926","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه stood in his Ṣalāh when sitting was due upon him, so the people said, ""Subḥān Allāh, Subḥān Allāh."" He understood what they intended, but when he completed his Ṣalāh he prostrated twice while seated, then said, ""I heard you saying Subḥān Allāh so that I would sit, but that is not the Sunnah. The Sunnah is only what I did."""
"2927","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ forgot before completion, so he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness before giving salām, and he said: > **""Whoever forgets before completion prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness before giving salām, and when one forgets after completion, he prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness after giving salām.""**"
"2928","Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās narrated that he prayed behind Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه a prayer in which he forgot, so he prostrated after the salām. Then he turned to them and said, ""I only did as I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ do."""
"2929","Qatādah narrated that Anas رضي الله عنه recited aloud in Ẓuhr or ʿAṣr, and he did not prostrate."
"2930","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""There is no forgetfulness prostration in the prayer of fear.""**"
"2931","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Ṣalāh was made obligatory as two rakʿahs each, except Maghrib as three, because it is witr. She said that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ traveled, he prayed the original prayer, except Maghrib; and when he was resident, he added two rakʿahs with every two rakʿahs, except Maghrib because it is witr, and Fajr because the recitation therein is prolonged."
"2932","In another narration from her, ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said that the Ṣalāh was made obligatory in Makkah as two rakʿahs each, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to Madinah, he added two rakʿahs with every two rakʿahs, and she narrated similarly."
"2933","Also from Aḥmad, from her, ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها said that the first thing Allah made obligatory upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of the Ṣalāh was two rakʿahs each, except Maghrib, for it was three, and he mentioned its meaning."
"2934","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said, ""O people, indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, made the Ṣalāh obligatory upon the tongue of your Prophet ﷺ as four in residence and two in travel."""
"2935","Abū al-Kanūd narrated that he asked Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما about the travel prayer, and he said, ""Two rakʿahs that descended from heaven. If you wish, then return them."""
"2936","Mawriq narrated that he asked Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما about the prayer in travel, and he said, ""Two rakʿahs each. Whoever opposes the Sunnah has disbelieved."""
"2937","Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd al-Kindī, the nephew of al-Namir, narrated that the Ṣalāh was made obligatory as two rakʿahs each, then increase was made in the residence prayer, while the travel prayer was left as it was."
"2938","Ibrāhīm narrated that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said, ""Whoever prays four in travel repeats the prayer."""
"2939","Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهم both said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prescribed the prayer in travel as two rakʿahs, and it is complete; and the witr in travel is a Sunnah."
"2940","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that he prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the prayer of fear as two rakʿahs, except Maghrib as three, and he prayed with him in travel as two rakʿahs, except Maghrib as three."
"2941","Khalaf ibn Ḥafṣ narrated from Anas رضي الله عنه that they were taken to Syria to ʿAbd al-Malik, forty men of the Anṣār, so that he might assign stipends for them. When he returned, while they were at Fajj al-Nāqah, he prayed Ẓuhr with them as two rakʿahs, then entered his tent. The people stood and added two more rakʿahs to their two rakʿahs, so he said, ""May Allah disgrace the faces. By Allah, they neither hit the Sunnah nor accepted the concession. I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say:"" > **""A people will go deep into the religion and pass through it as the arrow passes through the game.""**"
"2942","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he traveled, prayed two rakʿahs each, and when he was resident, he prayed four. He said that Ibn ʿAbbās said, ""Whoever prays four in travel is like one who prays two in residence."" He said that Ibn ʿAbbās also said, ""The Ṣalāh was shortened only once, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs and the people prayed one rakʿah each."""
"2943","Abū Naḍrah narrated that a youth of Aslam asked ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنه about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he said, ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never traveled except that he prayed two rakʿahs, except Maghrib."""
"2944","Abū Laylā al-Kindī narrated that Salmān رضي الله عنه came forward with twelve or thirteen riders among the Companions of Muḥammad ﷺ. When the time of Ṣalāh came, they said, ""Come forward, O Abā ʿAbdillāh."" He said, ""We do not lead you, nor do we marry your women. Indeed, Allah guided us through you."" So a man from the people came forward and prayed four rakʿahs. When he gave salām, Salmān said, ""What have we to do with the fourfold? Half of the fourfold would have sufficed us, and we are more in need of the concession,"" meaning travel."
"2945","Salmān رضي الله عنه said: Prayer was ordained as two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed it in Makkah in that manner until he came to Madinah, and he prayed it in Madinah for as long as Allah willed. Then two rakʿahs were added in the prayer of the resident, while the prayer in travel was left as it was."
"2946","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: I travelled with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. All of them prayed, from the time they set out from Madinah until they returned to it, two rakʿahs during the journey and during the stay in Makkah."
"2947","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would travel from Makkah to Madinah fearing none but Allah, praying two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs."
"2948","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb narrated that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه prayed four rakʿahs in Minā, and the people objected to him for it. He said: O people, I took a family in Makkah since I arrived, and I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever takes a family in a land, let him pray the prayer of a resident.""**"
"2949","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""When a traveller takes a family in a land, he is counted among its people; he prays the prayer of a resident, four rakʿahs.""** He said: And I took a family in it since I arrived here, and for that reason I have led you in four rakʿahs."
"2950","ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Zubayr said: When Muʿāwiyah came to us performing Ḥajj, we came to Makkah. He led us in Ẓuhr prayer as two rakʿahs, then he departed to Dār al-Nadwah. He said: And ʿUthmān, when he completed the prayer upon arriving in Makkah, would pray Ẓuhr, ʿAṣr, and the last ʿIshāʾ there as four rakʿahs each. When he went out to Minā and ʿArafāt, he would shorten the prayer. When he finished Ḥajj and stayed in Minā, he would complete the prayer until he departed. When Muʿāwiyah led us in Ẓuhr as two rakʿahs, Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān rose toward him and said to him: No one has criticized your cousin with anything uglier than that with which you have criticized him. He said to them: Woe to you both! Was there anything for me to do other than what I did? I prayed them both with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with Abū Bakr and with ʿUmar. They said: But your cousin had completed them, and your differing from him is a criticism of him. He said: Then Muʿāwiyah went out to ʿAṣr and prayed it with us as four rakʿahs. > **""I prayed them both with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and with Abū Bakr and with ʿUmar.""**"
"2951","A man said: We had carried something for Abū Dharr, intending to give it to him. We came to al-Rabadhah and asked about him but did not find him. It was said: He sought permission for Ḥajj and was permitted. So we came to him in the town — meaning Minā — and while we were with him, it was said to him: ʿUthmān has prayed four rakʿahs. That bore heavily upon Abū Dharr and he spoke stern words. He said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he prayed two rakʿahs, and I prayed with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar. Then Abū Dharr stood and prayed four rakʿahs. It was said to him: You criticized the Commander of the Believers for something and then you do it yourself? He said: Disagreement is worse. > **""I prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he prayed two rakʿahs, and I prayed with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar.""**"
"2952","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ, when travelling, would sometimes complete the prayer and sometimes shorten it."
"2953","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The best of my Ummah are those who, when they do wrong, seek forgiveness; and when they do good, rejoice; and when they travel, shorten the prayer and break the fast.""**"
"2954","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O people of Makkah, do not shorten the prayer at less than four buruds, from Makkah to ʿUsfān.""**"
"2955","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to shorten the prayer at al-ʿAqīq."
"2956","Al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Do not shorten the prayer except in Ḥajj or jihād.""**"
"2957","Ziyād ibn Abī Maryam narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that he said: Do not curtail any of your prayers in your nearby outings nor in your pastures, and do not travel through the villages of the Sawād for your needs and then say: We are travellers. The traveller is only one who goes from one horizon to another. > **""The traveller is only one who goes from one horizon to another.""**"
"2958","Thumāmah ibn Shuraḥbīl said: I went out to Ibn ʿUmar and said: What is the prayer of the traveller? He said: Two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs, except Maghrib prayer, which is three. I said: What is your view if we are at Dhū al-Majāz? He said: And what is Dhū al-Majāz? I said: A place where we gather and trade, and where we remain for twenty nights or fifteen nights. He said: O people, I was in Azerbaijan — I do not recall whether he said four months or two months — and I saw them praying it as two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs. And I saw the Prophet ﷺ with my own eyes praying it as two rakʿahs. Then he cited this verse: 'Indeed, in the Messenger of Allah you have an excellent example.' > **""Two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs, except Maghrib prayer, which is three.""**"
"2959","Al-Ḥasan narrated that he stayed with Anas in Naysābūr for two years, and he would pray two rakʿahs, two rakʿahs."
"2960","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed in Tabūk for twenty nights, shortening the prayer."
"2961","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined two prayers on the day he raided Banū al-Muṣṭaliq."
"2962","In another narration, the Prophet ﷺ combined two prayers while travelling."
"2963","From him also, that the Prophet ﷺ would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ when the journey pressed him hard."
"2964","Abū al-Zubayr said: I asked Jābir رضي الله عنه: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ? He said: > **""Yes, in the year we raided Banū al-Muṣṭaliq.""**"
"2965","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ, while travelling, would delay Ẓuhr and hasten ʿAṣr, and delay Maghrib and hasten ʿIshāʾ."
"2966","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would combine two prayers while travelling."
"2967","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he would combine two prayers while travelling."
"2968","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would combine Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ, delaying this one to the end of its time and hastening this one at the beginning of its time."
"2969","Khuzaymah ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ prayed Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ as three and two rakʿahs with a single iqāmah."
"2970","ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Anṣārī narrated from Khuzaymah ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه that he said: I prayed with the Prophet ﷺ at Muzdalifah with a single iqāmah."
"2971","Abū Saʿīd — meaning al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه — said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, and Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. He delayed Maghrib and hastened ʿIshāʾ, praying them together."
"2972","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey and the march pressed him, and he mounted before the sun declined, he would delay Ẓuhr until the first time of ʿAṣr entered. Then he would dismount and pray them together. Then he would delay Maghrib until the disappearance of the twilight became apparent. Then he would dismount and pray them together, Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ."
"2973","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ, when on a journey, if the sun had declined before he set out, would pray Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together. And if he set out before the sun declined, he would combine them at the beginning of the time of ʿAṣr. He would do the same for Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ."
"2974","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that when he wished to combine two prayers while travelling, he would delay Ẓuhr to the end of its time and pray it, and pray ʿAṣr at the beginning of its time. And he would pray Maghrib at the end of its time and pray ʿIshāʾ at the beginning of its time. And he would say: > **""Such was how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would combine two prayers while travelling.""**"
"2975","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the expedition of Tabūk. He would combine Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr, praying Ẓuhr at the end of its time and praying ʿAṣr at the beginning of its time. Then he would move on, and pray Maghrib when its time was about to exit, before the twilight had disappeared, and pray ʿIshāʾ at the beginning of its time, when the twilight had disappeared. Then, when he drew near, he said: > **""We shall camp tomorrow, if Allah wills, at Tabūk, so let none of you precede us to the water.""** Muʿādh said: I was the first to hasten to the water, yet two men had already reached it before me. They had drawn water into their two waterskins with them, and the water was murky. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and said: > **""Did I not forbid you both that none should precede us to the water?""** Then he called for the two waterskins and they were poured into the water, and he supplicated to Allah, and the water gushed forth. Then he said: > **""O Muʿādh, it is as if, should your life be prolonged, you will see what is here filled with gardens.""**"
"2976","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stayed at Khaybar for six months, praying Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr together, and Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ together."
"2977","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined the first and ʿAṣr, and Maghrib and ʿIshāʾ. He was asked about that, and he said: > **""I did this so that my Ummah would not be put in hardship.""**"
"2978","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ combined two prayers in Madinah without fear."
"2979","Yaʿlā ibn Umayyah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was on a journey when rain befell us. There was wetness beneath us and sky above us, and we were in a narrow pass. The time of prayer came, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded Bilāl, and he called the adhān and the iqāmah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stepped forward and prayed upon his mount, and the people prayed upon their mounts, gesturing with gestures, making sujūd lower than rukūʿ."
"2980","ʿAlqamah ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Muzanī narrated from his father رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you are among reeds, or snow, or mud, and the time of prayer comes, then gesture with gestures.""**"
"2981","ʿAmr ibn Yaʿlā رضي الله عنه said: I attended the prescribed prayer while we were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ upon our mounts. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us, moving ahead of us, then led us, and we prayed upon our mounts."
"2982","Anas ibn Sīrīn said: We came with Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه from al-Kūfah, until when we were at Aṭīṭ, morning came upon us while the ground was clay and water. So he prayed the prescribed prayer upon his beast. Then he said: > **""I never prayed the prescribed prayer upon my beast before today.""**"
"2983","ʿAzzah, who was among the early women, said: Abū Bakr addressed us and said: > **""Do not pray upon packsaddles.""**"
"2984","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Prayer on the back of a beast while travelling is like this, and like this, and like this, and like this.""**"
"2985","Al-Hirmās ibn Ziyād رضي الله عنه said: I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying upon a camel facing toward Syria."
"2986","Abū Saʿīd and Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ would pray voluntary prayers upon his mount, wherever it faced, gesturing with gestures, making sujūd lower than rukūʿ."
"2987","Saʿīd ibn Jubayr narrated that Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما would pray voluntary prayers upon his mount, but when he wished to pray witr, he would dismount and pray witr upon the ground."
"2988","Shuqrān, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, said: I saw with my own eyes the Prophet ﷺ heading toward Khaybar upon a donkey, praying upon it with gestures."
"2989","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه said: I saw the Prophet ﷺ praying the supererogatory prayer upon his mount, wherever it faced, but he would not do that for the prescribed prayer."
"2990","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray witr upon his camel."
"2991","Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to pray in a ship standing, unless he feared drowning."
"2992","Anas ibn Sīrīn said: I went out with Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه to a land at Shaqq Sirrīn, until when we were at the Tigris, Ẓuhr came. He led us seated upon a mat in a ship, while the ship was being towed along with us."
"2993","Qatādah narrated that Ibn Masʿūd and ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنهما would pray voluntary prayers in travel before the prayer and after it."
"2994","Thawbān رضي الله عنه said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and he said: > **""Indeed, this journey is exertion and hardship. So when one of you prays witr, let him bow two rakʿahs. If he wakes, well and good; otherwise, they shall count for him.""**"
"2995","Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah رضي الله عنه narrated that a man from the Anṣār came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: Inform us about Friday, what good is in it? He said: > **""In it are five qualities: in it Ādam was created, and in it Ādam was sent down, and in it Allah caused Ādam to die, and in it is an hour in which no servant asks Allah for anything except that He grants it to him, so long as he does not ask for a sin or severing of kinship, and in it the Hour will come. There is no angel brought near, nor heaven, nor earth, nor mountain, nor stone, except that it feels apprehension from Friday.""**"
"2996","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: Friday was presented to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Jibrīl came to him with something like a white mirror in his palm, in whose midst was like a black spot. He said: What is this, O Jibrīl? He said: > **""This is Friday. Your Lord presents it to you so that it may be a festival for you and for your people after you. For you therein is good. You are the first, and the Jews and Christians are after you. And therein is an hour in which no one supplicates his Lord for good allotted to him except that He grants it, or seeks refuge from evil except that He wards off from him what is greater than it. And we call it in the Hereafter the Day of Increase.""** Then he mentioned the hadith in full."
"2997","In another narration from Anas رضي الله عنه, he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The days were presented to me, and among them Friday was presented to me. It was like a white mirror, and in its midst was a black spot. I said: What is this? It was said: The Hour.""**"
"2998","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, does not leave any of the Muslims on Friday except that He forgives him.""**"
"2999","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Good deeds are multiplied on Friday.""**"
"3000","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: It was said to the Prophet ﷺ: Why was Friday named? He said: > **""Because in it the clay of your father Ādam was fashioned, and in it shall be the stunning blast and the resurrection, and in it shall be the seizure. And in the last three hours of it is an hour in which whoever supplicates Allah therein shall be answered.""**"
"3001","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""The sun never rises nor sets upon a day more excellent — or greater — than Friday.""** Then he mentioned the like thereof."
"3002","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The master of days with Allah is Friday. In it your father Ādam was created, and in it he entered Paradise, and in it he came out, and in it the Hour will come.""**"
"3003","The like thereof has been narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām رضي الله عنه in a long hadith."
"3004","Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The days shall be raised in their form, and Friday shall be raised radiant and luminous. Its people shall throng around it like a bride led to her chamber, illuminating for them as they walk in its light. Their complexions shall be white as snow and their fragrance as musk. They shall wade through hills of camphor, while men and jinn gaze at them, never blinking in wonder, until they enter Paradise. None shall mingle with them except the muʾadhdhins who acted for reward.""**"
"3005","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Shall I not inform you of the best of angels? Jibrīl, peace be upon him. And the best of Prophets? Ādam. And the best of days? Friday. And the best of months? Ramaḍān. And the best of nights? Laylat al-Qadr. And the best of women? Maryam bint ʿImrān.""**"
"3006","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that when Rajab entered, the Prophet ﷺ would say: > **""O Allah, bless us in Rajab and Shaʿbān, and bring us to Ramaḍān.""** And when Friday night came, he would say: > **""This is a radiant night and a resplendent day.""**"
"3007","Abū Hurayrah and Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Allah, Blessed and Exalted, led astray from Friday those who came before us. For the Jews is Saturday and for the Christians is Sunday. We are the last in this world and the first on the Day of Resurrection, those forgiven before all creation.""**"
"3008","Anas رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Friday and the night of Friday comprise twenty-four hours, in none of which is there an hour except that Allah has therein six hundred freed from the Fire.""** He said: Then we went out from him and entered upon al-Ḥasan, and mentioned to him the hadith of Thābit. He said: I heard it, and he added therein: All of them having already deserved the Fire."
"3009","Abū Saʿīd and Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, in Friday is an hour which no Muslim servant coincides with, asking Allah, Mighty and Majestic, therein for good, except that He grants it to him. And it is after ʿAṣr.""**"
"3010","Abū Salamah said: I heard Abū Hurayrah and Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنهما mention from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Indeed, in Friday is an hour which no servant coincides with while praying, asking Allah therein for anything, except that He grants it to him.""** He said: And ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām mentions from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: Yes, it is the last hour. I said: But he said 'while praying,' and that is not a time of prayer. He said: Did you not hear — or did it not reach you — that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever awaits the prayer is in prayer.""**"
"3011","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, in Friday is an hour which no Muslim coincides with, asking Allah therein for good, except that He grants it to him.""**"
"3012","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Seek the hour hoped for in Friday between ʿAṣr and the setting of the sun, and it is but this much — meaning a handful.""**"
"3013","Fāṭimah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ رضي الله عنها, narrated from her father, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he said: > **""Indeed, in Friday is an hour which no Muslim servant coincides with, asking Allah therein for good, except that He grants it to him.""**"
"3014","Abū Salamah said: Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه used to narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Indeed, in Friday is an hour which no Muslim coincides with while praying, asking Allah for good, except that He grants it to him.""** He said: And Abū Hurayrah indicated with his hand how brief it is. He said: When Abū Hurayrah passed away, I said: By Allah, I shall surely go to Abū Saʿīd and ask him about this hour, whether he has any knowledge of it. So I came to him and found him straightening palm stalks. I said: O Abū Saʿīd, what are these stalks that I see you straightening? He said: These are stalks in which Allah has placed blessing for us. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved them and would lean upon them, so we would straighten them and bring them to him. Then he saw spittle in the Qiblah of the mosque, while in his hand was a stalk from those stalks, so he scraped it off and said: > **""When one of you is in his prayer, let him not spit in front of him, for his Lord is in front of him. Let him spit to his left or beneath his foot.""** He said: Then Sarīḥ added: If he finds no spittoon, then in his garment or his sandal. He said: Then the sky poured rain that night. When the Prophet ﷺ came out for the last ʿIshāʾ prayer, lightning flashed and he saw Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān. He said: What is this night journey, O Qatādah? He said: I knew, O Messenger of Allah, that those attending the prayer were few, so I wished to attend it. He said: When you have prayed, remain until I pass by you. When he finished, he gave him the stalk and said: > **""Take this, and it shall illuminate ten ahead of you and ten behind you. When you enter the house and see darkness in the corner of the house, strike it before you speak, for it is a devil.""** He said: So he did, and for that reason we love these stalks. He said: I said: O Abū Saʿīd, Abū Hurayrah narrated to us about the hour that is in Friday — do you have any knowledge of it? He said: We asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about it, and he said: > **""I had been informed of it, then I was made to forget it, as Laylat al-Qadr was made forgotten.""** Then I went out from him — meaning from Abū Saʿīd — until I came to the house of a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. I said: This is a man who has read the Torah and accompanied the Prophet ﷺ. He said: So I entered upon him and said: Inform me about this hour which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to speak of regarding Friday? He said: Yes. Allah created Ādam on Friday, and settled him in Paradise on Friday, and sent him down to the earth on Friday, and caused him to die on Friday. It is the day in which the Hour will come, and it is the last hour of Friday. He said: I said: Do you not know that the Prophet ﷺ said 'in prayer?' He said: Do you not know that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever awaits a prayer is in prayer.""**"
"3015","Maymūnah bint Saʿd رضي الله عنها said: She said: Give us a ruling, O Messenger of Allah, about Friday prayer. He said: > **""In it is an hour in which no servant supplicates his Lord therein except that He answers him.""** I said: Which hour is it, O Messenger of Allah? He said: > **""That is when the imām stands.""**"
"3016","ʿAwf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه said: > **""Indeed, I hope the hour of Friday is in one of three times: when the muʾadhdhin calls, and while the imām remains upon the minbar, and at the iqāmah.""**"
"3017","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites Ḥā Mīm al-Dukhān on the night of Friday or the day of Friday, Allah shall build for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"3018","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever recites on Friday the sūrah in which Āl ʿImrān is mentioned, Allah and His angels shall bless him until the sun sets.""**"
"3019","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever says before the dawn prayer on Friday three times: 'I seek forgiveness from Allah, besides Whom there is no god, and I repent to Him,' his sins shall be forgiven, even if they are more than the foam of the sea.""**"
"3020","Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no prayer among the prayers more excellent than the Fajr prayer on Friday in congregation, and I do not consider anyone among you who attends it except as forgiven.""**"
"3021","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the dawn prayer every Friday, ""Alif Lām Mīm, the revelation of the Book,"" and ""Has there come upon man"": > **""He used to recite in every Friday in the dawn prayer 'Alif Lām Mīm, the revelation of the Book' and 'Has there come upon man.'""**"
"3022","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite in the morning prayer on Fridays, Alif Lām Mīm Tānzīl al-Sajdah and ""Has there come upon man,"" and he used to do so regularly: > **""He used to recite in the morning prayer on Friday Alif Lām Mīm Tānzīl al-Sajdah and 'Has there come upon man,' and he continued to do so.""**"
"3023","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday, in the first rakʿah ""Alif Lām Mīm Tānzīl"" al-Sajdah, and in the second rakʿah ""Has there come upon man"": > **""He used to recite in the Fajr prayer on Friday, in the first rakʿah 'Alif Lām Mīm Tānzīl' al-Sajdah, and in the second rakʿah 'Has there come upon man.'""**"
"3024","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ prostrated in the morning prayer in Tānzīl al-Sajdah: > **""The Prophet ﷺ prostrated in the morning prayer in Tānzīl al-Sajdah.""**"
"3025","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Send abundant blessings upon me on the luminous night and the radiant day, for your blessings are presented to me.""**"
"3026","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever combines fasting on Friday with visiting a sick person, attending a funeral, giving charity, and setting a slave free, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"3027","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""There are five deeds which, if anyone performs them in one day, Allah records him among the people of Paradise: fasting on Friday, going early to the Friday prayer, attending a funeral, and freeing a slave.""**"
"3028","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever prays the Friday prayer, fasts on that day, visits a sick person, attends a funeral, and attends a marriage, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"3029","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ addressed them one day and said: > **""Indeed, Allah has prescribed Friday upon you in this standing of mine, in this hour of mine, in this month of mine, in this year of mine, until the Day of Resurrection. Whoever abandons it without an excuse, whether under a just ruler or an unjust ruler, may Allah not unite his affairs for him, and may there be no blessing for him in his affair. Beware, he has no prayer; beware, he has no Hajj; beware, he has no righteousness; beware, he has no charity.""**"
"3030","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, Friday is obligatory upon him, except for a slave, a woman, or a child. And whoever dispenses with it through amusement or trade, Allah will dispense with him, and Allah is Rich, Praiseworthy.""**"
"3031","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Friday is obligatory except upon those whom your right hands possess or one who has an ailment.""**"
"3032","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Friday is obligatory except upon a woman, a child, a sick person, a slave, or a traveler.""**"
"3033","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There are five upon whom there is no Friday prayer: the woman, the traveler, the slave, the child, and the people of the desert.""**"
"3034","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""There is no military expedition upon women, nor Friday prayer, nor accompanying a funeral.""**"
"3035","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to attend Friday and not to absent themselves from it, and he said: > **""Each one of you has more right to his seat when he returns to it.""**"
"3036","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to trim his nails and shorten his mustache on Friday before going out to the prayer: > **""He used to trim his nails and shorten his mustache on Friday before going out to the prayer.""**"
"3037","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever trims his nails on Friday is protected from evil until the like of it [the next Friday].""**"
"3038","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever bathes on Friday, applies perfume if he has any, wears the finest of his clothes, then goes out until he comes to the mosque, prays whatever voluntary prayer seems fit to him, harms no one, then listens attentively until he prays, it will be an expiation for what is between it and the next Friday.""** And in another narration: > **""Then he goes out in a state of tranquility until he comes to the mosque.""**"
"3039","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever bathes on Friday, then wears the finest of his clothes, applies perfume if he has any, then walks to Friday in tranquility, steps over no one, harms no one, prays whatever is decreed for him, then waits until the Imām departs, he is forgiven for what is between the two Fridays.""**"
"3040","ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī narrated that Nubayshah al-Hudhalī used to narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: > **""When a Muslim bathes on Friday, then comes to the mosque and harms no one, if he does not find that the Imām has come out, he prays whatever seems fit to him, and if he finds that the Imām has come out, he sits and listens attentively until the Imām completes his Friday prayer and his speech. If he is not forgiven for all his sins in that Friday of his, it will be an expiation for the Friday that follows it.""**"
"3041","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever washes his head and bathes, draws near and comes early, approaches and listens attentively, will have for every step he takes the reward of standing a year in prayer and fasting it.""**"
"3042","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""When a man purifies himself and performs purification well, then comes to Friday without engaging in idle talk and without behaving ignorantly until the Imām departs, it will be an expiation for what is between it and the next Friday. And in Friday there is an hour in which no believing man coincides asking Allah for anything except that He gives it to him. And the prescribed prayers are expiations for what is between them.""**"
"3043","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما was asked by a man about bathing on Friday, whether it is obligatory. He said, ""No, but I shall narrate to you the beginning of bathing. The people were needy, they used to wear wool and used to water the date-palms upon their backs, and the mosque of the Prophet ﷺ was narrow with a low roof. The people came in wool and sweated, and the minbar of the Prophet ﷺ was short, only three steps, so the people sweated in their wool and their odors spread, and they harmed one another until their odors reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was upon the minbar, so he said"": > **""O people! When you come to Friday, then bathe, and let each one of you apply the finest perfume he has if he has any.""**"
"3044","A man from the Anṣār among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""It is a duty upon every Muslim to bathe on Friday, to use the siwāk, and to apply perfume if his family has any.""**"
"3045","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever washes and bathes on Friday, then draws near to where he can hear the sermon of the Imām, and when the Imām comes out he listens and remains silent until he prays it with him, there is recorded for him, for every step he takes, the worship of a year, its standing in prayer and its fasting.""**"
"3046","Thawbān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""It is a duty upon every Muslim to use the siwāk, to bathe on Friday, and to apply of his family's perfume if there is any.""**"
"3047","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""O company of Muslims! Whoever among you comes to Friday, let him bathe, and if he finds perfume there is no harm upon him in applying some of it, and the siwāk is incumbent upon you.""**"
"3048","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on one of the Fridays: > **""O company of Muslims! Indeed, this is a day which Allah has made a festival for you, so bathe, and the siwāk is incumbent upon you.""**"
"3049","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever comes to Friday, let him bathe.""**"
"3050","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to bathe once every week, meaning Friday: > **""He commanded us to bathe once every week, meaning Friday.""**"
"3051","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever comes to Friday, let him bathe.""**"
"3052","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Bathing on Friday is obligatory upon every one who has reached puberty.""**"
"3053","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Bathing on Friday is part of the Sunnah.""**"
"3054","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Bathing on Friday is a Sunnah.""**"
"3055","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever comes to Friday, let him bathe.""**"
"3056","Sahl ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Among the rights of Friday are the siwāk and bathing, and whoever finds perfume, let him apply some of it.""**"
"3057","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Bathe on Friday, for whoever bathes on Friday will have expiation for what is between Friday and the next Friday, with an addition of three days.""**"
"3058","Abū Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Friday is an expiation for what is between it and the Friday that follows it, with an addition of three days, and that is because Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: 'Whoever comes with a good deed shall have ten times the like of it.'""**"
"3059","Salmān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""O Salmān, do you know what Friday is?"" He said, ""It is the day in which Allah gathered your father, or your parents."" He said, ""No, but I shall tell you about Friday: there is no Muslim who purifies himself, wears the finest of his clothes, applies perfume if they have any, otherwise water suffices, then comes to the mosque and listens attentively until the Imām comes out, then prays, except that it will be an expiation for him between it and the next Friday, so long as killing is avoided, and that applies for the whole of time"": > **""O Salmān, do you know what Friday is? There is no Muslim who purifies himself, wears the finest of his clothes, applies perfume if there is any, otherwise water suffices, then comes to the mosque and listens attentively until the Imām comes out, then prays, except that it will be an expiation for him between it and the next Friday, so long as killing is avoided, and that applies for the whole of time.""**"
"3060","Salmān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""O Salmān, what is Friday?"" He said, ""Allah and His Messenger know best,"" three times. Then Salmān said, ""Friday is the day in which your father was gathered, or your parents were gathered"": > **""O Salmān, what is Friday? Friday is the day in which your father was gathered, or your parents were gathered.""**"
"3061","ʿAtīq Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq and ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever bathes on Friday, his sins and errors are expiated, and when he sets out walking, there is recorded for him for every step twenty good deeds, and when he finishes the prayer he is rewarded with the deeds of two hundred years.""**"
"3062","Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever bathes on Friday, his sins and errors are forgiven, and when he sets out walking to Friday, he will have for every step the deeds of twenty years, and when he finishes the Friday prayer he is rewarded with the deeds of two hundred years.""**"
"3063","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Indeed, bathing on Friday draws out sins from the roots of the hair completely.""**"
"3064","ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah narrated that his father came to him while he was bathing on Friday and said, ""Is this bathing of yours for janābah or for Friday?"" He said, ""For janābah."" He said, ""Repeat another bath, for I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say"": > **""Whoever bathes on Friday will remain in a state of purity until the next Friday.""**"
"3065","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever bathes on Friday, then applies of his finest perfume, wears the finest of his clothes, then goes early and does not separate two persons until he rises from his place, then listens attentively until the Imām finishes his sermon, he is forgiven for what is between the two Fridays, with an addition of three days.""**"
"3066","Aws ibn Anas رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever rises in the morning on Friday, washes his head and bathes, comes early, walks and does not ride, draws near and does not engage in idle talk, will have for every step a deed from among the deeds of righteousness, of fasting and prayer.""**"
"3067","Anas رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and it is good, and whoever bathes, then bathing is more excellent.""**"
"3068","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and it is good, and whoever bathes, then bathing is more excellent.""**"
"3069","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and it is good, and whoever bathes, then bathing is more excellent.""**"
"3070","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sometimes bathe on Friday and sometimes leave it off at times: > **""He would sometimes bathe on Friday and sometimes leave it off at times.""**"
"3071","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever performs ablution on Friday, then with it, and it is good, and whoever bathes, then bathing is more excellent.""**"
"3072","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon on Friday when a man entered stepping over the necks of the people, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""One of you comes late, then steps over the necks of the people and harms them."" He said, ""I did nothing more than hear the call and perform ablution."" So he said: > **""One of you comes late, then steps over the necks of the people and harms them. And is this a day for ablution alone?""**"
"3073","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه said that bathing on Friday is recommended but not compulsory: > **""Bathing on Friday is recommended but not compulsory.""**"
"3074","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had two garments which he would wear on his Friday, and when he returned they would fold them up until the like of it: > **""He had two garments which he would wear on his Friday, and when he returned they would fold them up until the like of it.""**"
"3075","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah and His angels send blessings upon the wearers of turbans on Friday.""**"
"3076","Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه narrated that the first of the Emigrants to come to Madinah was Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, and he was the first to gather them there on Friday before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived, and he led them in prayer: > **""The first of the Emigrants to come to Madinah was Muṣʿab ibn ʿUmayr, and he was the first to gather them there on Friday before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived, and he led them in prayer.""**"
"3077","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Friday is upon fifty men, and there is no Friday upon fewer than fifty.""**"
"3078","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When seventy men among us go to Friday, they are like the seventy of Mūsā who came as a deputation to their Lord, or even better.""**"
"3079","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""The angels sit on Friday upon the doors of the mosques with scrolls, recording the people, and when the Imām comes out the scrolls are folded."" He said, ""O Abū Umāmah, is there no Friday for one who comes after the coming out of the Imām?"" He said, ""Indeed there is, but he is not among those recorded in the scrolls"": > **""The angels sit on Friday upon the doors of the mosques with scrolls, recording the people, and when the Imām comes out the scrolls are folded.""**"
"3080","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The angels sit upon the doors of the mosques and record the first, the second, and the third, until when the Imām comes out they raise the scrolls.""**"
"3081","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه said that when Friday comes, the devils go out driving the people to their markets with banners, while the angels sit upon the doors of the mosques recording the people according to their ranks, the foremost and the next who prays after him, until the Imām comes out. Then whoever draws near to the Imām, listens attentively, and does not engage in idle talk will have two shares of reward, and whoever stays far from him, listens, remains silent, and does not engage in idle talk will have one share of reward. And whoever draws near to the Imām but engages in idle talk, neither remaining silent nor listening, will bear two shares of sin, and whoever stays far from him but engages in idle talk, neither remaining silent nor listening, will bear one share of sin. And whoever says ""Be silent"" has spoken, and whoever speaks has no Friday. Then he said, ""Thus did I hear your Prophet ﷺ"": > **""When Friday comes, the devils go out driving the people to their markets with banners, while the angels sit upon the doors of the mosques recording the people according to their ranks, the foremost and the next who prays after him, until the Imām comes out. Then whoever draws near to the Imām, listens attentively, and does not engage in idle talk will have two shares of reward, and whoever stays far from him, listens, remains silent, and does not engage in idle talk will have one share of reward. And whoever draws near to the Imām but engages in idle talk, neither remaining silent nor listening, will bear two shares of sin, and whoever stays far from him but engages in idle talk, neither remaining silent nor listening, will bear one share of sin. And whoever says 'Be silent' has spoken, and whoever speaks has no Friday.""**"
"3082","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said, ""When Friday comes, the angels sit upon the doors of the mosques and record those who come among the people according to their ranks: a man who offers a camel, a man who offers a cow, a man who offers a sheep, a man who offers a chicken, and a man who offers an egg."" He said, ""Then when the caller to prayer calls and the Imām sits upon the minbar, the scrolls are folded and they enter the mosque listening to the remembrance"": > **""When Friday comes, the angels sit upon the doors of the mosques and record those who come among the people according to their ranks: a man who offers a camel, a man who offers a cow, a man who offers a sheep, a man who offers a chicken, and a man who offers an egg. Then when the caller to prayer calls and the Imām sits upon the minbar, the scrolls are folded and they enter the mosque listening to the remembrance.""**"
"3083","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Attend Friday and draw near to the Imām, for a man may be of the people of Paradise yet lag behind from Friday and so be delayed from Paradise, though he is of its people.""**"
"3084","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever washes and bathes on Friday, goes early and comes at the first hour, draws near, listens and remains silent, will have two shares of reward.""**"
"3085","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ with the same chain that he said: > **""The one who hastens to Friday is like one who offers a camel, and the one after him is like one who offers an ox, and the one after him is like one who offers a sheep, and the one after him is like one who offers a chicken.""**"
"3086","Wāthilah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, sends the angels on Friday upon the doors of the mosques, recording the people, the first, the second, the third, the fourth, the fifth, and the sixth. And when they reach the seventh, they are in the position of those near to sparrows.""**"
"3087","Shaddād ibn Aws رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever washes and bathes on Friday, goes early and comes at the first hour, then sits close to the Imām, listens and remains silent, will have for every step he takes the deeds of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer.""**"
"3088","Abū Ṭalḥah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever washes and bathes, goes early and comes at the first hour, draws near to the Imām, remains silent and does not engage in idle talk on Friday, Allah records for him, for every step he takes to the mosque, the fasting of a year and its standing in prayer.""**"
"3089","Abū ʿUbaydah narrated that ʿAbdullāh said, ""Hasten to the Fridays, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, manifests Himself to the people of Paradise every Friday upon a dune of camphor, and they will be near to Him according to their hastening to Friday. Then Allah, Mighty and Majestic, grants them of honor something they had never seen before that. Then they return to their families and tell them of what Allah has granted them."" He said, ""Then ʿAbdullāh entered the mosque and found two men on Friday who had preceded him, so ʿAbdullāh said, 'Two men, and I am the third; if Allah wills, He will bless the third'"": > **""Hasten to the Fridays, for Allah, Mighty and Majestic, manifests Himself to the people of Paradise every Friday upon a dune of camphor, and they will be near to Him according to their hastening to Friday.""**"
"3090","Wāthilah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""No circles should be formed on Friday before the coming out of the Imām; rather they should face the Qiblah. And none on the day of ʿEid after the prayer.""**"
"3091","Al-Arqam ibn Abī al-Arqam, who was among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Indeed, the one who steps over the necks of the people on Friday and separates two persons after the coming out of the Imām is like one dragging his intestines in the Fire.""**"
"3092","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated that while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the sermon, a man came stepping over the necks of the people until he sat close to the Prophet ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prayer, he said, ""O so-and-so, what prevented you from joining with us?"" He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I was keen to place myself in the position you see."" He said, ""I saw you stepping over the necks of the people and harming them. Whoever harms a Muslim has harmed me, and whoever harms me has harmed Allah, Mighty and Majestic"": > **""I saw you stepping over the necks of the people and harming them. Whoever harms a Muslim has harmed me, and whoever harms me has harmed Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"3093","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Do not eat reclining, and do not step over the necks of the people on Friday.""**"
"3094","ʿAmmār ibn Saʿd narrated that ʿUthmān ibn al-Azraq entered upon them in the mosque on Friday while the Imām was delivering the sermon, so he moved apart and sat in the mosque. They said to him, ""May Allah have mercy on you, had you joined us it would have been easier for you."" He said, ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say"": > **""Whoever steps over the necks of the people on Friday after the coming out of the Imām, or separates two persons, is like one dragging his intestines in the Fire.""**"
"3095","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ missed one of his Companions and said to him: > **""Where were you, for I did not see you? Did you not attend the prayer?""** He replied: ""Indeed I did, but I came when the people had already settled in their places, and I disliked stepping over the necks of the people."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Quite right.""**"
"3096","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command us to attend Jumu'ah and not to stay away from it: > **""When the believers are called upon for the duty of Friday and they rise, then if one of them returns to his seat, he has the greatest right to it.""**"
"3097","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you dozes on Friday, let him move to his companion's place, and let his companion move to his place.""** It was said to Ismāʿīl: ""Even while the imām is delivering the khutbah?"" He said: ""Yes."""
"3098","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: There was the trunk of a date-palm in the mosque against which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would lean his back when he spoke on Friday or when some matter arose about which he wished to address the people. The people said: ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, shall we not make something for you sufficient for your standing?"" He replied: > **""There is no harm upon you if you do so.""** So they made for him a minbar of three steps. When he sat upon it, the trunk moaned like the lowing of a cow, grieving for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. So he embraced it and stroked it until it calmed down."
"3099","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray facing a trunk, and the mosque was but a thatched shelter, and he would deliver the khutbah leaning against that trunk. A man among his Companions said: ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, shall we make something for you to stand upon on Friday so that the people may see you and hear your khutbah?"" He said: > **""Yes.""** So they made for him three steps. When the Prophet ﷺ stood as he used to stand, the trunk inclined toward him in yearning, and he said to it: > **""Be still. If you wish, I shall plant you in Paradise so the righteous may eat from you, and if you wish, I shall restore you as you were, fresh and moist.""** It chose the Hereafter over this world. When the Prophet ﷺ passed away, the trunk was given to Ubayy, and it remained with him until termites consumed it."
"3100","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ used to stand by a piece of wood, leaning upon it while delivering the khutbah every Friday, until a man of the people came to him and said: ""If you wish, I shall make something for you such that when you sit upon it, it will be as though you are standing."" He said: > **""Yes.""** So the minbar was made for him. When he sat upon it, the piece of wood yearned with the yearning of a she-camel for her calf, until the Prophet ﷺ came down and placed his hand upon it. On the next day I saw that it had been moved, so we asked: ""What is this?"" They said: ""The Prophet ﷺ came last night with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and they moved it."""
"3101","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a piece of wood beside which he would stand. A man came, and the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to make a seat for him. When the Prophet ﷺ stood upon it to deliver the khutbah, the piece of wood beside which he used to stand yearned until the people of the mosque heard its yearning. The narrator said: I asked al-ʿAwfī: ""Did you hear it yourself?"" He said: ""Yes, by my life, I heard it."" Then the Prophet ﷺ came and embraced it until it calmed down."
"3102","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ used to stand by a piece of wood, leaning upon it while delivering the khutbah every Friday, until a man from Rūm came to him and said: ""If you wish, I shall make something for you such that when you sit upon it, it will be as though you are standing."" He said: > **""Yes.""** So the minbar was made for him. When he sat upon it, the piece of wood yearned with the yearning of a she-camel for her calf, until the Prophet ﷺ came down and placed his hand upon it. On the next day I saw that it had been moved, so we asked: ""What is this?"" They said: ""The Prophet ﷺ came with Abū Bakr and ʿUmar and they moved it."""
"3103","Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""If I take the minbar, then my father Ibrāhīm took it; and if I take the staff, then my father Ibrāhīm took it.""**"
"3104","Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm narrated from his father: > **""The first to deliver the khutbah upon pulpits was Ibrāhīm.""**"
"3105","Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to deliver the khutbah leaning his back against the trunk of a date-palm. It was said to him: ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Islam has spread far, the people have grown many, and delegations come to you from distant lands; if only you would order that something be made upon which you may appear."" He said to one man: > **""Will you make the minbar?""** He said: ""Yes."" The Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""What is your name?""** He said: ""So-and-so."" The Prophet ﷺ said: > **""You shall not make it.""** Then he called another and asked: > **""Will you make the minbar?""** He said: ""Yes, if Allah wills."" The Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""What is your name?""** He said: ""Ibrāhīm."" So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Then set about making it.""** When it was made, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ ascended it, and the piece of wood yearned with the yearning of a she-camel until the people of the mosque—or he said the people of Madīnah—heard its sound. He came down and embraced it until it calmed, then said: > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, had I left it, it would have yearned until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"3106","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray toward a pillar in the mosque and deliver the khutbah leaning against it, relying upon it. By the order of ʿĀ'ishah رضي الله عنها this minbar of his was made for him. When he went to it and left his station by the pillar, the pillar moaned with a loud moaning—until the Prophet ﷺ left his station—longing for the Prophet of Allah ﷺ. So the Prophet of Allah ﷺ walked to it and embraced it, and when he embraced it the sound we had heard subsided. The narrator said: I asked: ""Did you hear it yourself?"" He said: ""I heard it, and so did the people of the mosque; and it is one of the pillars adjacent to the chamber."""
"3107","ʿĀ'ishah رضي الله عنها said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to deliver the khutbah leaning against a trunk. A Roman passed by and said: ""If Muḥammad would call me, I would make for him something more comfortable for him than this."" So he was summoned for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he made for him a minbar of four steps. The Prophet ﷺ ascended the minbar to deliver the khutbah, and the trunk yearned as a she-camel yearns. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came down to it and said: > **""What is the matter with you? If you wish, I shall supplicate to Allah and He will return you to your enclosure; and if you wish, I shall supplicate to Allah and Allah will admit you into Paradise, where you will bear fruit and the commissioned Prophets of Allah and His God-fearing servants will eat from your fruit.""** She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Yes.""** Then the trunk sank away and was gone."
"3108","Sahl ibn Saʿd رضي الله عنه said: I was sitting with a maternal uncle of mine from the Anṣār when the Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""Go out to the forest, bring me some of its wood, and make a minbar for me upon which I may address the people.""** So he made for him a minbar having two steps, and the Prophet ﷺ would sit upon them."
"3109","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to deliver the khutbah leaning against the trunk in the mosque. When the minbar was made, the trunk yearned for him, so the Prophet ﷺ embraced it and it calmed down."
"3110","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to deliver the khutbah on Friday, on the day of Fiṭr, and on the day of Aḍḥā, all upon the minbar; and on Friday, when the mu'adhdhin fell silent, he would stand and deliver the khutbah."
"3111","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered a khutbah with his back toward the Multazam."
"3112","Al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه said: We used to pray Jumu'ah with the Prophet ﷺ, then we would depart and hasten toward the thickets, yet we would find of shade nothing but the measure of the place of our feet. And in one narration: we would find of shade nothing but the place of our feet."
"3113","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه was with the people on a Friday in the mosque of Kūfah, while ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir was the governor of Kūfah appointed by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd was in charge of the treasury. ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd looked at the shade and saw it was like a sandal-strap, so he said: > **""If any of you would attain the Sunnah of your Prophet ﷺ, let him go forth now.""** The narrator said: By Allah, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd had not finished his speech before ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir came out announcing the prayer."
"3114","ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه said: We used to pray Jumu'ah, then we would depart and find no shade of the walls in which to take shelter."
"3115","Bilāl رضي الله عنه used to call the adhān for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on Friday when the shade was the measure of a sandal-strap, at the moment the Prophet ﷺ sat upon the minbar."
"3116","Anas رضي الله عنه said: We used to pray Jumu'ah with the Prophet ﷺ, then we would return and take the midday rest."
"3117","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: When the sun had declined, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray Jumu'ah, and we would return finding no shade in which to take shelter."
"3118","Zayd ibn Wahb said: We used to pray Jumu'ah with ʿAbdullāh, then we would return and take the midday rest."
"3119","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque on Friday, he would greet those seated near his minbar; and when he ascended the minbar, he would turn toward the people and greet them."
"3120","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: I heard the Prophet ﷺ say: > **""When one of you enters the mosque while the imām is upon the minbar, there is to be no prayer and no speech until the imām has finished.""**"
"3121","Al-Sulayk رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you comes while the imām is delivering the khutbah, let him pray two brief rakʿahs.""**"
"3122","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: Al-Nuʿmān ibn Qawqal entered while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was delivering the khutbah on Friday, so the Prophet ﷺ said to him: > **""Pray two rakʿahs and be brief in them. When one of you comes on Friday while the imām is delivering the khutbah, let him pray two rakʿahs and make them brief.""**"
"3123","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever speaks on Friday while the imām is delivering the khutbah is like the donkey carrying volumes of Scripture; and the one who says to him, 'Be silent,' has no Jumu'ah.""**"
"3124","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat upon the minbar one day, addressed the people, and recited a verse. Beside me was Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, so I said to him: ""O Ubayy, when was this verse revealed?"" He refused to speak to me. Then I asked him again, and he refused to speak to me until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came down. Ubayy said: ""You have of your Jumu'ah nothing but what idle talk you engaged in."" When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished, I came to him and informed him, saying: ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, you recited a verse while beside me was Ubayy ibn Kaʿb; I asked him when this verse was revealed, but he refused to speak to me until you came down, and Ubayy claimed I have of my Jumu'ah nothing but what idle talk I engaged in."" He said: > **""Ubayy has spoken the truth. When you hear your imām speaking, remain silent until he finishes.""**"
"3125","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ said to a man: ""You have no Jumu'ah."" The Prophet ﷺ asked: > **""Why, O Saʿd?""** He said: ""Because he was speaking while you were delivering the khutbah."" So the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Saʿd has spoken the truth.""**"
"3126","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd entered the mosque while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the khutbah, sat beside Ubayy ibn Kaʿb, and asked him about something or spoke to him about something, but Ubayy did not answer him. Ibn Masʿūd thought it was resentment. When the Prophet ﷺ finished his prayer, Ibn Masʿūd said: ""O Ubayy, what prevented you from answering me?"" He said: ""You did not attend Jumu'ah with us."" He asked: ""Why?"" He said: ""You spoke while the Prophet ﷺ was delivering the khutbah."" So Ibn Masʿūd rose and entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and mentioned that to him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Ubayy has spoken the truth; obey Ubayy.""**"
"3127","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ addressed us on a Friday and mentioned a chapter. Abū Dharr said to Ubayy: ""When was this chapter revealed?"" He turned away from him. When the prayer finished, Ubayy said: ""You have of your prayer nothing but what idle talk you engaged in."" So he asked the Prophet ﷺ, who said: > **""He has spoken the truth.""**"
"3128","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When you come to Jumu'ah, draw near the imām, listen to the khutbah, and do not engage in idle talk.""**"
"3129","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه said: The Prophet ﷺ used to deliver the khutbah on Friday and remind us of the Days of Allah, then he recited a chapter. Abū al-Dardā' nudged Ubayy ibn Kaʿb and said: ""When was this chapter revealed, for I have not heard it until now?"" He gestured to him to be silent. When they finished, Ubayy said: ""You have of your prayer nothing but what idle talk you engaged in."" Abū al-Dardā' informed the Prophet ﷺ of what Ubayy had said, and he said: > **""Ubayy has spoken the truth.""**"
"3130","Abū al-Dardā' and Abū Dharr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited upon the minbar on Friday, and then the narrator recounted the same narration as before."
"3131","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""There is no one who attends Jumu'ah without engaging in idle talk or behaving ignorantly, who performs ablution well and attends it with the imām, except that it will be an expiation for what is between it and the following Jumu'ah; and no one prays a prescribed prayer except that it will be an expiation for what is between it and the prayer that follows it.""**"
"3132","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: Speech is disliked on four occasions: on Friday, on the day of Fiṭr, on the day of Aḍḥā, and during istisqā', once the imām has ascended the minbar and begun speaking, until he descends."
"3133","Abū Qays said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd entered the mosque on Friday wearing clean, pure, fine white garments. He looked for a spacious place and sat in it without stepping over anyone. When the imām came out, two men were talking, so he took pebbles and pelted them with them. They looked at him and fell silent. When the imām came down, he said: > **""Did you two not know that you were in prayer?!""**"
"3134","Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī said: A man asked ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd to recite a passage to him while the imām was delivering the khutbah on Friday, but ʿAbdullāh did not speak to him. When the prayer was finished, ʿAbdullāh said to him: > **""What you asked about is your share of Jumu'ah.""**"
"3135","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""It suffices as idle talk that you say to your companion, 'Be silent,' once the imām has come out on Friday.""**"
"3136","Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah said: I heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه while he was upon the minbar and the mu'adhdhin was calling the iqāmah, seeking news from the people and asking them about their affairs and their prices."
"3137","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to deliver the khutbah on Friday standing, then he would sit, then he would stand and deliver the khutbah again."
"3138","It is narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to deliver two khutbahs on Friday, separating between them with a sitting."
"3139","Al-Sā'ib ibn Yazīd رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to deliver two khutbahs for Jumu'ah, sitting between them."
"3140","Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah said: I witnessed ʿUthmān delivering the khutbah upon the minbar standing, and I witnessed Muʿāwiyah delivering the khutbah sitting. Muʿāwiyah said: > **""Indeed I am not ignorant of the Sunnah, but my age has advanced, my bones have grown frail, and your needs have grown many; so I wished to settle some of your needs while sitting, then I shall stand and take my share of the Sunnah.""**"
"3141","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to deliver the khutbah holding a short staff."
"3142","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to address them on a journey leaning upon a bow."
"3143","Saʿd al-Qaraẓ, the mu'adhdhin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrated that the Prophet ﷺ, when he delivered the khutbah on Friday, would deliver it leaning upon a staff."
"3144","Al-Nuʿmān رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivering the khutbah, saying: > **""I warn you of the Fire! I warn you of the Fire!""** So much so that had a man been in the market, he would have heard it from this station of mine. He said: Until a bordered cloak that was upon his shoulder fell at his feet. And in one narration: the people of the market heard his voice while he was upon the minbar."
"3145","ʿAlī—or al-Zubayr—رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to address us and remind us of the Days of Allah, until its effect would be recognized in his face, as though he were a warner of a people whom the affair would befall in the morning; and when he had recently met Jibrīl, he would not smile laughing until Jibrīl had ascended."
"3146","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out one day and called out three times, saying: > **""O people, do you know what my parable and yours is? The parable of a people who feared an enemy coming upon them, so they sent a man to scout for them. While he was thus, he sighted the enemy and came forward to warn them, fearing the enemy would overtake him before he could warn his people; so he waved his garment: 'O people, you have been attacked! O people, you have been attacked!'—three times.""**"
"3147","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: Ḍimām ibn Thaʿlabah came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""Shall I not charm you, O Muḥammad?"" So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""All praise is due to Allah; we praise Him and seek His help. We seek refuge in Allah from the evils of our souls and from the misdeeds of our actions. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide; and whomever He leaves astray, none can guide. And I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger.""**"
"3148","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Every important matter that is not begun with 'Al-ḥamdu lillāh' is maimed—or cut off.""**"
"3149","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما said: > **""It is not of the Sunnah to invoke blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ upon the minbar on Friday.""**"
"3150","ʿAbdullāh—that is, Ibn Masʿūd—رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed us, saying: > **""Ammā baʿd.""**"
"3151","Shaddād ibn Aws رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""O people, this world is a present commodity from which the righteous and the wicked both eat; and the Hereafter is a true promise in which a Powerful King will judge, establishing truth and nullifying falsehood. O people, be children of the Hereafter and do not be children of this world, for every mother is followed by her child.""**"
"3152","Nuʿaym ibn Maḥḥah said: Among the khutbah of Abū Bakr رضي الله عنه was: > **""Do you not know that you go forth in the morning and return in the evening toward a known term? Whoever is able to complete his term while engaged in the work of Allah, Exalted is He, let him do so; and you will not attain that except through Allah, Mighty and Majestic. There were a people who set their hopes upon others, so He forbade you from being like them; and do not be like those who forgot Allah. Where are those brethren of yours whom you knew? They have gone forth to what they sent ahead in the days of their predecessors, and therein they have met with wretchedness or felicity. Where are the first tyrants who built cities and encircled them with walls? They have come to be beneath rocks and wells. This is the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, whose wonders never cease; so seek light from it for the day of darkness, and seek clarity through its affair and its exposition. Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, praised Zakariyyā and his household, saying: {They used to hasten to good deeds and supplicate Us with hope and fear, and they were humbly submissive to Us}. There is no good in speech not intended for the Face of Allah, nor any good in wealth not spent in the path of Allah, nor any good in one whose forbearance does not overcome his ignorance, nor any good in one who fears the blame of a blamer regarding Allah.""**"
"3153","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه would come every Thursday and stand without sitting, saying: > **""Do not put the people to trial, for among them are the weak, the elderly, and the needy. Let not the span of time grow long upon you, nor let hope distract you, for everything that is coming is near. Indeed, what is distant is what is not coming. Among the most evil of people is the idler by day and the carrion by night.""**"
"3154","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to say when he sat: > **""You are in the passage of night and day, with lifespans diminishing and deeds preserved, and death comes suddenly. Whoever sows good will soon harvest with delight, and whoever sows evil will soon harvest with regret; and every sower shall have what he sowed. The slow one does not outstrip his share, nor does the greedy one attain through greed what was not decreed for him. Whoever is given good, it is Allah who gave him; and whoever is shielded from evil, it is Allah who shielded him. The God-fearing are masters, the jurists are leaders, and sitting with them is an increase.""**"
"3155","Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited Barā'ah on Friday while standing, reminding them of the Days of Allah."
"3156","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ used to recite upon the minbar: {Say: O disbelievers} and {Say: He is Allah, the One}."
"3157","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Prophet ﷺ delivered the khutbah and recited in his khutbah the end of al-Zumar, whereupon the minbar shook twice."
"3158","ʿAbdullāh—that is, Ibn Masʿūd—رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ: > **""Indeed, brevity of the khutbah and length of the prayer are a sign of a man's understanding; so lengthen the prayer and shorten the khutbah, for indeed some eloquence is sorcery. After you there will come a people who lengthen khutbahs and shorten prayers.""**"
"3159","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that when the Prophet ﷺ would dispatch a commander, he would say: > **""Shorten the khutbah and lessen speech, for indeed some speech is sorcery.""**"
"3160","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: You are in a time whose preachers are few and whose scholars are many; they lengthen the prayer and shorten the khutbah. And there will come upon you a time whose preachers are many and whose scholars are few."
"3161","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to seek forgiveness for the believing men and believing women, and the Muslim men and Muslim women, every Friday."
"3162","Muʿāwiyah رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed those who dissect sermons with affectation as hair is parted."
"3163","Bashīr ibn ʿAqrabah رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""Whoever stands to deliver a sermon seeking thereby nothing but show and repute, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will station him in the station of show and repute.""**"
"3164","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever catches the khutbah, then Jumu'ah is two rakʿahs; and whoever does not catch it, let him pray four. And whoever does not catch the bow shall not count the prostration until he catches the bow.""**"
"3165","Muslim ibn ʿIyāḍ said: I asked al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī رضي الله عنهما about the two rakʿahs of Jumu'ah, and he said: > **""They are a makeup for what is besides them.""**"
"3166","Abū ʿUtbah al-Khawlānī narrated from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to recite in the Jumu'ah prayer Sūrah al-Jumuʿah and the chapter in which the hypocrites are mentioned, and that when the Prophet ﷺ walked, he walked briskly with purpose."
"3167","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: Among what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite in the Jumu'ah prayer was al-Jumuʿah, by which he would encourage the believers, and in the second, Sūrah al-Munāfiqūn, by which he would alarm the hypocrites."
"3168","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Prophet ﷺ said: > **""Whoever catches a rakʿah of Jumu'ah has indeed caught it, except that he must make up what he missed.""**"
"3169","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever catches a rakʿah of Jumu'ah, let him add another to it.""**"
"3170","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever misses the last rakʿah, let him pray four.""** Maʿmar said: Qatādah said: ""He prays four."" It was said to Qatādah: ""Ibn Masʿūd came while they were sitting at the end of the prayer, and he said to his companions: 'Sit, for you have caught it, if Allah wills.'"" Qatādah said: ""He meant only: you have caught the reward."""
"3171","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said: > **""Whoever catches a rakʿah of Jumu'ah, let him add another to it; and whoever misses both rakʿahs, let him pray four.""**"
"3172","Jābir رضي الله عنه missed Jumu'ah, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded him to give a dīnār in charity."
"3173","Abū Qatādah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever abandons Jumu'ah three times without necessity, his heart will be sealed.""**"
"3174","Ḥārithah ibn al-Nuʿmān رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""One of you takes grazing livestock and attends the prayer in congregation, but his livestock become troublesome for him, so he says: 'If only I sought for my livestock a place more abundant in pasture than this.' Then he moves away and attends nothing but Jumu'ah. Then his livestock become troublesome for him again, so he says: 'If only I sought for my livestock a place fuller than this.' Then he moves away and attends neither Jumu'ah nor the congregation, and his heart is sealed.""**"
"3175","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood to deliver the khutbah on Friday and said: > **""It may be that a man is but a mile from Madīnah when Jumu'ah comes upon him, yet he does not attend it.""** Then on the second he said: > **""It may be that a man is two miles from Madīnah when Jumu'ah comes upon him, yet he does not attend it.""** And on the third he said: > **""It may be that he is three miles from Madīnah and does not attend Jumu'ah, and Allah seals his heart.""**"
"3176","Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: I heard my uncle narrating from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: > **""Whoever hears the call on Friday and does not come—or does not answer—then hears the call and does not come—or does not answer—then hears the call and does not come—or does not answer—Allah, Mighty and Majestic, seals his heart and makes it the heart of a hypocrite.""**"
"3177","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: > **""Whoever abandons Jumu'ah three Fridays in succession has cast Islam behind his back.""**"
"3178","Usāmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever abandons three Jumu'ahs without excuse is recorded among the hypocrites.""**"
"3179","Ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""Whoever hears the call on Friday and does not attend it, then hears the call and does not attend it, three times, his heart will be sealed and made the heart of a hypocrite.""**"
"3180","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""May it not be that one of you takes a flock of sheep at a distance of two or three miles, and when Jumu'ah comes he does not attend it—three times—and Allah seals his heart.""**"
"3181","Kaʿb ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه narrated from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: > **""People will either desist from hearing the call on Friday and then not attending it, or Allah will surely seal their hearts; then they will surely be among the heedless.""**"
"3182","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""I fear for my Ummah only the Book and milk.""** It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, what is the matter with the Book?"" He said: > **""The hypocrites will learn it and then dispute with it against those who believe.""** He said: It was then said: ""What is the matter with milk?"" He said: > **""People who love milk will go out from the congregations and abandon the Jumu'ahs.""**"
"3183","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: > **""The ruin of my Ummah lies in the Book and milk.""** They said: ""What are the Book and milk?"" He said: > **""They will learn the Qur'ān and interpret it contrary to its interpretation; and they will love milk, so they will abandon the congregations and the Jumu'ahs and take to the desert.""**"
"3184","ʿAmmār ibn Abī ʿAmmār, the freedman of Banū Hāshim, narrated that he passed by ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah while he was at the river of Umm ʿAbdullāh, pouring water over his boys and freedmen. ʿAmmār said to him: ""O Abū Saʿīd, it is Jumu'ah."" So ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah said to him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say: > **""When there is pouring rain, let each of you pray in his lodging.""**"
"3185","ʿAbdullāh—that is, Ibn Masʿūd—رضي الله عنه said: We had no ʿEid except in the forenoon, and I remember us performing Jumu'ah with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the shade of al-Ḥaṭīm."
"3186","ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr al-Ḥibrānī said: I saw ʿAbdullāh ibn Busr, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he had prayed Jumu'ah, go out and circle through the market for a while, then return to the mosque. It was said to him: ""Why do you do this?"" He said: > **""I saw the chief of the Muslims do it.""**"
"3187","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما said: Two ʿEids coincided in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ—a day of Fiṭr and a Friday. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in the ʿEid prayer, then turned to them with his face and said: > **""O people, you have attained goodness and reward; and we shall gather for Jumu'ah. Whoever wishes to gather with us, let him gather; and whoever loves to return to his family, let him return.""**"
"3188","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: My close friend ﷺ counseled me with three things I shall never abandon, neither in travel nor in residence: sleeping after witr, fasting three days of every month, and two rakʿahs after Jumu'ah. Then Abū Hurayrah added the two rakʿahs of Ḍuḥā after the two rakʿahs following Jumu'ah."
"3189","ʿIṣmah رضي الله عنه narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""When one of you prays Jumu'ah, let him not pray anything after it until he speaks or leaves.""**"
"3190","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to bow four rakʿahs before Jumu'ah and four after it, without separating between them."
"3191","ʿAlqamah ibn Qays narrated that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه prayed four rakʿahs on Friday after the imām had given the salām."
"3192","Qatādah narrated that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه used to pray six rakʿahs after Jumu'ah."
"3193","Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd used to teach us to pray four rakʿahs after Jumu'ah, until we heard the saying of ʿAlī: > **""Pray six.""** Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: So we pray six. ʿAṭā' said: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān would pray two rakʿahs, then four."
"3194","Jābir رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ took part in six expeditions before the prayer of fear, and the prayer of fear was prescribed in the seventh year."
"3195","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: > **""The prayer of sword-fighting is one rakʿah; in whatever direction a man faces, it suffices him.""**"
"3196","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrated from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the prayer of fear: The people were commanded and took up their weapons upon themselves. One party stood behind them facing the enemy, and another party came and prayed with him; he prayed with them one rakʿah. Then they stood and went to the party that had not prayed, while the party that had not prayed with him came forward and stood behind him; so he prayed with them one rakʿah and two prostrations, then gave the salām with them. When he had given the salām, those who had faced the enemy stood, pronounced the takbīr all together, bowed one bow, and performed two prostrations after he had given the salām."
"3197","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ set out on one of his expeditions and met the polytheists at ʿUsfān. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Ẓuhr and they saw him bowing and prostrating, he and his Companions, some of them said to others: ""Had you charged against them, they would not have known of you until you fell upon them."" One of them said: ""They have another prayer dearer to them than their families and their wealth; so wait until it comes, then charge against them in one assault."" So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And when you are among them and establish the prayer for them} to the end of the verse. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, they all pronounced the takbīr with him, then he bowed and they all bowed with him. When he prostrated, the row nearest him prostrated with him, while those behind them remained standing facing the enemy. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prostration and stood, the second row prostrated, then stood; and the row nearest him stepped back while the others advanced and came nearest the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he bowed, they all bowed with him; then he rose and they rose with him. Then he prostrated and those nearest him prostrated with him, while the second row remained standing facing the enemy. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prostration and sat, those nearest him sat, and the rear row prostrated, then sat and prostrated with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave the salām, they all gave the salām. When the polytheists saw them—some prostrating while others stood—they said: ""They have surely been informed of what we intended."""
"3198","Abū al-ʿĀliyah al-Riyāḥī narrated that Abū Mūsā was at al-Dār in Iṣbahān, and there was no great fear upon them that day; but he wished to teach them their religion and the Sunnah of their Prophet ﷺ. So he arranged them in two rows: one party with weapons facing their enemy, and one party behind them. He prayed one rakʿah with those nearest him, then they stepped back upon their heels until they stood in the place of the others, passing through them until they stood behind him. Then he prayed another rakʿah with them and gave the salām. Then those nearest him and the others stood and each prayed one rakʿah by themselves, then greeted one another. Thus the imām completed two rakʿahs, and the people one rakʿah each."
"3199","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ led us in the prayer of fear once; he never led us in it before it nor after it."
